      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR NicLuz, A new and pretty form of bolero takes the arm of a loose plissé sacque, reaching only to the waist line, in soft white cashmere or silk, slung from the shoulders by lace or velvet straps, leaving the arms free, with only a frilling of lace round the armholes, or a sort of winged lace sleeve falling away from the arm underneath.. These are pretty to wear with lace or foulard frocks. There has been recently introduced a hat in the shape of the " Regulatim" sailor, the broaclOnp-turned brim round shape, such as children afeEr in imitation of the Jack Tars. This hat must be set well back on the bead, and a single quill is thrust through the brim in front. Wide sailor shapes, in silvery silk straws, are trimmed with coloured straw bows, painted with white spots, in imitation of foulard, the bowl] being thrust through with a couple of quills. The straw berets, crumpled up into a fitting band, which rests on the head, look smart and workmanlike, with a couple of silk choux and a quill at the side, whilst a somewhat similar shape, only with a beefeater crown, is made in serge, and is light and not to be harmed.</p>
        <p>A white linen gown, made with a flounce about eight inches deep, had a broad band of thick, coarse creamcoloured guipure along the top of the flounce and two other bands higher up. Bands of narrower guipure trimmed the sleeves above the elbows and three bands crossed the bodice in front, the folds of linen being carried down the shoulders to the centre, and there very slightly pouched above the belt. An orange.chiffon belt and collar finished the dress. A white voile had all the seams sewn with openwork over a white under- skirt. The bodice was made with similar seams all round a yoke of white silk embroidered in fine black tambour stitch round pale blue flowers. The belt was chine silk folded very flat and repeating in the design the pale blue tint of the flowers. A few of the fiat hats appeared, but they do not seem to be at all popular.</p>
        <p>I may perhaps convey an idea for a pretty frock by suggesting that it should be in clear white muslin, mounted over a blush rose silk slip; the skirt, gathered on the hips, has an insertion of deep ecru lace let in above the volant, and is edged with a quilting of rose-satin ribbon, which is re- • . peated on the bottom of the skirt. The elbow sleeves are treated in the same way, and bordered with a deep frill of lace, whilst the full bodice, which opens over a lace transparency, has a pretty n fichu of rose crepe de chine, bordered with lace, and crossed in front, being caught in with roses at the waist, the embroidered ends being left loose. The bergere hat is covered with roses. Another dress is in very fine blue serge, with short jacket turned back with tabs held down by silver buttons and opening over a vest of blue-spotted pique. The deep cape collar is turned back with scarlet velvet • revers, and red and blue sailor's bow, knotted at the throat. The elbow sleeves turn back with a gauntlet above white lawn underleeves, and the skirt is set into deep box-pleats.</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>fk. f- -7,, _</p>
        <p>A word of warnin3 about moths may be in season. The very dry wcathor has been favour- able to these innocent and attractive-looking little mischief workers. A good moth hunt may profit- ably employ a morning, and it is as well to remember that the whole race has a particular dislike of turpentine and cloves as well as nap- thaline and bitter apple. Tobacco is said to annoy them, and pepper is, for a while, adapted to keep them away from the box in which furs or woollens are kept, but pepper soon loses its firei- ness, and then the moths simply laugh at it. All furs and woollen garments should be well beaten with a rattan, then carefully brusbed, and every little fold and seam and overlapping piece ex- amined. They are said to prefer woollen materials wherein to found a family and die, but they by no means disdain flannelette as a residence; and where even that is absent, they will cheerfully put up with cotton. In fact, nothing is sacred to these ethereal-looking, but most destructive, little creatures, and a thorough overhauling of the wardrobe should be made at least one during each of the moth-months, June, July, August, and sometimes September as well.</p>
