      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>(Cop.yri:7ll!.)</head>
        <p>(Cop.yri:7ll!.)</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Crathie Church, which Queen Victoria used formerly to attend when at Balmoral, and where the late Empress Frederick has worshipped too, is now a fine modern building. It is very different from the old Church, which V 74.3 puilod down a few years age, to make room for the present one, and which was a building of the plainest devription. The Royal pews, those of the Queen, the Prince of Wales, and the Household, were Nn the horseshoe gallery, as were also one or two others be- longing to the neighbouring lairds. But often the num- ber of illutrious personages was increased by the atten- dance of Sovereigns And Princes, who were guests at Balmoral, and who joined in WO simple service in old Crathie Church.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt some cause for anxiety about the safety of Sc Paul's Cele-drat. in L mdon, but there i nothing new in the appearance of cracks in the walls of the strueture, about whi h we have heard so much lately. S me peeple seem to heve thought that the building was breaking up,and they may be seen in little groups looking for the cracks of which none but experts can see any sign. It has however always been knowu that the old foundations were weak, and more than a hundred years ago the building was strengthened by huge iron ties in different parts. The Cathedral is nevertheless likely to outlast a good many generations yet, though the custodians are no doubt Wise in opposing all schemes of underground railways that woo tend to still further weaken the foundations of the gri at build- ing. There is always a good deal of state and ceremony at Blenheim Palace, where the Duke and Duchess et Marl- borough have been entertaining a large house party, in connection with the great Unionist dcmoestration. The Dulness has a great ambition to restore the political influence of the ducal family into which she has married but the Dike seems likely to be somewhat overshadowed by his cousin, the ming and clever Mr Winston Churchill. On the scciel sicle however, the Duke and Duchess have every advaiitage, and nothing could have been mere successful than the pelitical gathering at Blenheim, at which some 3,000 Unionists were invited to Bee the splendours of the princely mansion and grounds. Blenheim Palace, named after the battle fought on August 13th 1704 is situated at Woodstock,near Oxford, and dates from the year 1722,though it was commenced in 1705 It was built at the public expense, as a testi- mony of gratitude to the first Duke of Marlborough,and cost over half a million of money. It is an expensive place to kept up, being s three, and for many years it w.s not keep in the best of order, but when the presen Duke came of age,he had the place thoroughly done up, putting in new windows and having alltsorts of modern conveniences added. The house is now in a thorough state of repair aed the nark, which f xtends to nearly 3,000 acres, is very beautiful. It contains a striking monument of the great Duke, and there are some fine trees representing the disposition of the different batta- lions when Marlborough gained his decisive victory over the French •na Bavarians- The order for general mourning has occasioned little trouble or expense. This is the season when the majority of us reckon upon" finishing out" our summer outfit, and few are witnout something black or white left in their wardrobes, that can be donned for the 21 days —ending on the 29 h inst. It is a fact patent Le all that th average woman appears much more distinguished in black and white than she does in vivid tones, and it seems mere than probable that we shall cling to the former happy combination well on into the autumn,and possibly during the early winter months. The present open fronted type of bodices gives scope for great variety of vests. These, with tape, loops and ties cunningly adjusted, need neither sewing nor pinning to the dress. A V shined front takes but a small piece of material to cover it, and affords an opportunity of using up scraps, with which we were tempted at the sales. Failing such supplies as these any piece-drawer will yield enough to make a smart vest, for bands of ribbon and lace insertion can be run together alternately (tucks, and lines of baby ribbon, bide seams) and even plain black silk or cloth can be glo.ified for its Anal purpose by painting a design upon it in oil or lustre colours, or by transferring " pine" patterns from Paisley scraps, and out-lining and enriching these with stray needfuls of silks, and with sequins and tinsel thr-ads. Spotted silk scarves are inexpensive investments that yield ae excellent return. The plain or ring spots of black on a white, and white on black ground,have taken the place formerly o?.cupied by Paisley pstteres, and certainly assort better with the other accessories of dress. These spotted fnulard squares form a capital trimming.for the plain country or seaside hat,and make up also into waist swathes, chemisettes, and even whole bodices. Cut up, they form strappings on some widely contrastine material such as face-cloth, or serge, or in larger pieces, form ornamental " false hems " to collars and revers, or wide l bands for trimming shirts and bodices. Another use for these squares, in this pocket-less age, is as reticules. The square needs a lining, preferably of the material of the dress with which it is to be worn, and the two fabrics, each hemmed, are united simply by two circles of stitching traced perchance, by the aid of a plate, and for the outer ring, almost as large as the material will allow. The draw cords peas through this , channel, and emerge throngh holes left in it for them at opposite points.. Not vet bay, we discovered all the methods of utilis - ing the pretty dress muslins en evidence this season.' They are now being turned to account tor lampshades, an.: for this purpose answer very well indeed, setting out delightfully, and remaining clean quite long enough. The muslin should be chosen by candle light and tested up against the lamp, when it will be found that the fullest patterns come out best, and the brightest colours pink, green, and yellow, are especially acceptable. It is not enough to gather in the muslin to fit over the I stand, it needs edging with lace, or with small ball fringe, and generous bows of two inch wide ribbon most be connected with ruches' and streamers of the same. On a pale, bright, lampshade, black velvet rib- bons have a value of their own. The.greatly increased value of fine pearls is Illustrated by the extraordinary high prices which have lately been realised. In one or two instances as much as £20,000 or more has been paid for a necklace. Only the finest pearls perfectly equal in shape and size and without defects would, of course, realise such a fancy price, but as a rule a necklace of pearls which was worth £5OO a few years ago,is now worth three or four times as much. This rise in value is due to increased demand rather than any falling off in the supply, and no doubt the :dearer they get the more fashionable they will become. American women, who are always practical, are said to be buying them on the expectation that they will still further greatly increase in value, but unless pearls are taken great care of,they are liabie to deterior- ate. Perspiration is fatal to them, ard they Shluld always be gently rubbed and cleaned after being worn. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>An old lady, Miss Thirras, who died at Aberdeen, bas left her entire estate to her servant and two farm girls. The amount is £31,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>A GIRL OF THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. C. N. WILLIAMSON? Author of "The Barn Stornaers," "Fortune's Sport," "Miss Nobody," "Her Royal High- ! uess," "Lady Mary of the Dark House," &amp;a.</p>
        <p>SUMMARY OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Sheila Cope is taken by her supposed mother to the theatre, and there sees two people who interest her strangely. One is a young man in the pit, to whom she is indefinably drawn, and the other a woman in the stalls. The latter catches Sheila's eye, and deliber- ately exposes a purple scar on her arm. Sheila is startled, for a similar scar on her mother's arm has been a mystery to her since child:, -)d. When Lady Cope notices the woman with the ,car she hastily leaves the theatre, putting Sheila into a cab and bidding her drive back to the hotel. On her way home, the girl catches a glimpse of her mother and the woman with the scar seated in a four. wheeler. Subsequently the young man of the Lyceum pit renders her an important service. When Sheila reaches the hotel her mother is not there, but presently arrives in a dying condition, with her right arm horribly scarred, and a fresh burn where the scar had been. Murmuring incoherently of "the scar—after all these years— revenge," and "the West Wing," Lady Cope dies of heart disease. It is noticed that she was wearing a black opera-cloak instead of the yellow one in which she went out. Sheila instinctively connects this with the woman of the scar. Next day Sheila goes down to Arrish Mell Court, her Dorsetshire home, where her mother is buried. She resolves to carry out her mother's dying wish and investigate the West Wing. After a patient search, during which she is startled by mysterious noises, she finds nothing but a tiny- key in the pocket of an old tea- gown of her mother's. Next day Sir Roger Cope, her cousin, who ignores his title and works as a solicitor, calls. He proposes to her, and when refused fells her that she is not the daughter of Lady Cope at all, and, as the dead woman left no will, the property belongs to himself as next-of-kin. He gives her the address of her real mother, and Sheila resolves to go to her. She finds her, with two girls of a second marriage, living in a squalid street in Peckham. Sheila takes up her abode, and tries to obtain work from various agencies. As she is returning from a trip to one of them she is startled to notice a man in the street regarding her with a look of curious malignity, though when he sees her notice him he turns away. Soon she receives a letter from Roger Cope renewing his offer, and writes a few lines rejecting it. Her money runs out, and she sells some of her clothes, but on her return from the sale her purse is stolen by a fellow- passenger in the tram.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHAPTER XI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XI.</p>
        <p>'MUCH TELLS OF ROGER'S ALLIES.</p>
        <p>My face told the story of my misfortune. In an instant everyone in the tram seemed to know what had happened. "Lost your money, miss ? " inquired the con- ductor. _</p>
        <p>I could hardly answer. It might have been worse, for I might have been suspected of trying to "steal a ride" on false pretences ; but I did not think of that at the time, and my cup of despair seemed full to overflowing. "My purse has been stolen," I said. "It was in. my pocket when I came into the tram, I know." Two or three people had got out since then. I had not noticed their departure, but I remembered, when my opposite neighbour reminded me of the fact, that among the persons lately gone was a woman who had sat next me. She had come in as I did, and, after sitting down, crowded against my side so that I attempted to move further away; had asked of the tram in general how soon we would ;each a certain street—a street whose name I had not heard before. A man had informed her that, to pass the street in question, she must take another tram, whereupon, muttering something, she had risen and gone out.</p>
        <p>Already the tram was half a mile away. All agreed that it was useless for me to try to do any- thing, except to report the affair at the police- station. A nice old lady with a market basket paid my fare and called me "my dear," saying she hoped that there had not been much money in the purse; and my fellow-passengers soon forgot me in an animated discussion of other experiences with pickpockets, which seemed at once to make them all friends. I arrived at Easel-street at last, very miserable. Everything was now to be done over twain, and I bad parted from my possessions without having anything to shew for them. I knew that when I told my story I should be reproached for care- lessness, and I opened the door of the front room with reluctance. "Sheila!" exclaimed a well-known voice, and Roger Cope sprang from a chair as I entered. •</p>
        <p>I started back with a faint cry of dismay; but, before I could carry out my quickly-formed design of running away, he had taken two strides across the room and caught me by both hands. My mother, busy with the preparations for our frugal noontide meal, looked on at the greeting with a knowing smile, and I was suddenly pricked with the conviction that Roger had been enlisting her as an allyt ...."Do say you are glad to see me," he pleaded, in his charming voice. Well groomed, perfectly dressed, with an orchid in his buttonhole, and, if anything, hand- somer than ever, he was like a bird of Paradise alighting in a barnyard. 4I can't say it,' I answered. "I am not glad. You said in your letter, which I only got this morning, that you would wait for word from me. I have written since to beg that you would not come." "That's what I was afraid of," laughed Roger, quite boyishly. "I have a prophetic soul ; you see, I really couldn't stay away any longer." "Jenny, I'm surprised at you, bein' so rude as to tell the gentleman you don't want him,' scolded my mother. "If 'twas one of the others, now; but you, brought up as you've been! Why, Fan and Totseyt.would know better." My mother brought forward a hideous vase freshly filled with exquisite flowers. "See what the gentleman's brought you," she reproachfully remarked. "Well, you might at least have the grace to thank him." "That's not necessary from her to me, Mrs. Newlyn," said Roger. 'She knows very well that everything I have is hers, if only she will take it." He turned to me with an imploring look. "Sheila, Mrs. Newlyn has been exceedingly kind while I have been waiting and hoping for you to come in. But lam interrupting her, I know. Won't you come out and walk for a little while, so that we may quietly talk over past and future?" "I would rather not," I said. "For—your letter made me think you had the same things to say that I have already answered. There's nothing more tu add."</p>
        <p>"Then I must speak to you here!" exclaimed Roger. '"Your mother knows. It was right that I should tell her. And—she's just given me reason, to hope that she'll stand my friend. 0 Sheila, do be good to me! I swear you shall never regret it.", "I don't love you, Roger. I never shall love you," I said, firmly. "And so we could not be happy together. I meant what I told you before,' and I mean it just as much—even more, if possible' —now. If my life lay near yours we might be agreeable acquaintances at most. As it is, we can only be—strangers." His handsome face hardened, and pressing his lips together he took up the immaculate hat which stood among the cracked dishes and cooking utensils on the table. My mother moved between him and the door. "Oh, don't go, sir! Don't take her at her word!" she ejaculated. "Girls will be girls. She'll be sorry enough by-and-bye, I promise you." "I wish I could think so," sighed Rog,m, looking like a persecuted angel. "But I must go. Sheila doesn't want me here." "It's my • house, not hers!" cried my mother, sharply. "You are good to me. But I didn't come to male her unhappy. I wish for her happiness more than anything else in the world. I'll go now, though I leave my heart behind, as she knows well. Yet I can't give up hope—not quite—till she is the wife of another man." With this for an "exit " speech he was goounte,, dramatic to the end—the sympathetic hero of the scene in which I played the female villain. As the door closed upon the handsome figure my mother turned upon me. You must be mad! girl PshrDieokeydo.li k"nDoowywhat you'veouknowjust dn whatusy you're ab ee ? "The only thing I could do," I answered. But I was almost/ frightened at the look in her haggard eyes. "You've deceived me," she went on, fiercely. "You've just the same as lied. You came here and pretended you'd been driven away from your old home. It wasn't true. You left because von were a root. And now, as if luck was never tired of following some People that don't deserve it, rou've got the chance wain. You shan't refuse</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 16, 1901</head>
        <p>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 16, 1901</p>
        <p>it, you shan't. Stich a man Why, there an't be harkily his like in Fngland. He might be, prince. And you've got nothin', nothin' but yea. face. He's got everything ; all he wants is for you to take it and him with it. But you won't." "I can't," I said, quietly, though my voice was shaking. "You don't understand.' "I understand that you ought to be in Bedlam. Why, a Queen's daughter might snap at him; and you—but I've no patience, I can't talk about it. I can just tell you this, my house is no place for fools."</p>
        <p>"Very well," I said, faintly, "since you think me that, since you don't want me here, I—l'll go." "Oh, I don't mean that, Jenny!" she cried, her tone changing quickly. "I've got a temper, as you might know by this time. You oughtn't to aggra- vate it—just for nothin'." She came to me and laid both thin, work-worn hands on my shoulders, tears bursting suddenly from her eyes. "My girl!" she sobbed. "My girl, do this--for me. For us all. Think what it means. Think what it would be for Fan and Totsey. I don't say for myself. It don't matter much any more. But ey're young. They've had a hard life, and ihey've no better before them, unless you do this. 1. was talking' with him—Sir Roger Cope, I mean. he'd do anything for the girls if he was your husband. He said so straight out. 1 was so happy, I could have screamed. I never thought for an instant you'd say' No' to him, and ruin all our lives."</p>
        <p>With that worn face and tho.e beseeching eyes so near mine, I was dumb. My heart smote me. How selfish I had been, never even to think of what I could do for these poor soul-starved creatures, who had my blood in their veins! How selfish, how cruel I was even now, because my mother's appeal left me cold, and the voice of my spirit whispered: "You cannot do this thing that is asked of you, for them or anyone else on earth." I could bear no more. "Oh, mother, mother, you are killing me!" I cried, and flung myself free from her claspina hands—not because the contact was hateful, but because I was desperate, scarcely knowing what I did. "I'll work for you get money somehow, but—l'd rather die than marry Roger Cope. I told him I didn't love him, but it's worse than that, I.hate him. He's a hypocrite—a snake." I waited for no more—l could not endure to hear her answer, and have to retort upon that again. Turning, I flew out of the room, and, running to my own, dared to bolt myself in. Time passed on, and nobody came to call me. I knew that the dinner-hour must be long past, and I was faint with hunger, for I had break- fasted on a slice of bread; but I wished to think out some definite plan for the future before I saw my mother again. I wished to do this, yet I could not; for the preliminary pangs of starva- tion had begun their work. My head swam; I could not concentrate my thoughts. At last evening was falling. I had stayed for at least five hours in the little back room. It was time for Fan to come home from the factory. I wondered greatly what had been happening outside, and why fhad been left so long undisturbed. The silence seemed ominous, but at last it was suddenly broken by a great clamour in the next room. Some heavy object fell with a loud thud, accompanied by the crash of breaking dishes. Above the noise rose Fan's voice loud and shrill. She was screaming in hysterical passion, and flinging the furniture about, as I had known her to do once or twice before.</p>
        <p>With a quickly beating heart I laid m, hand on the bolt, slipping it back, yet hesitating when it was done to venture out. As I stood, trembling and undecided, there was a sound of rushing foot- steps in the passage, a weight was dashed against the door, which was violently opened, almost knocking me down. Fan broke in upon me, her handsome, bold young face distorted into crimson ugliness. "You cat—you deceitful cat I 'she shrieked. "I've lost 'in' —and all through you. You've ruined me You've spoilt my life, and on top o' that you refuse to make up to me in the only way you can. It's me earns the money 'ere. I keep this 'ouse, and I h'order you out of it. D'ye 'ear?" She shook me as she spoke, until my breath well-nigh left my body. I could only gasp I "What--do you—mean?" "She hasks what I mean I " mocked Fan, furiously turning to my mother and Totsey, who had come into the passage and were standing close to the doorway of the back room. Mrs. Miggits from upstairs had rushed down also, and was hanging over the banisters, drinking in the scene with greedy relish, her children crowding behind her.</p>
        <p>Fan did not care that the Miggits family saw and heard; she did not care that in all probability a larger audience was by this time collecting at the front door. But I cared; I could not shriek my answers, like a fishwife, trying to scream her down.</p>
        <p>_ "Tom 'as now thrown me over!" she panted. "That's wot I mean. And it's because of you. 'E's 'card things about you—things 'e wouldn't tell me. But 'e won't 'ave nothin' more to do with me so long as you're in this 'ouse, makin' the whole ny'bour'ood ring with talk. 'E met me at the fact'ry, and we 'ad it out. Then I comes 'ome, and I 'ear the chance you've thrown away— the chance fur the lot of us. You might o' myde the world a sort of 'eaven for us all, and I'd of snapped my fingers at Tom. But instid of that, you sits here in h'idleness, eatin' my bread. I won't stand it, I won't. I never liked you. Now the sight of you and your airs mykes me sick! Out you go this night.' She pushed me from her, and I staggered back- wards, half falling on the bed. "I will go," I gasped. "After this--I could not stay." I got up again, feeling strangely dazed, my arms bruised, and sparks dancing before my eyes from the rough treatment I had received. "You're too hard on her, Fan," I heard my mother plead. "She has paid her way up to now, and well, too. I'd never have charged her so much board if you hadn't made such a fuss. We've bin better off with her than without her; Tx; onese against her character. I don't to t what 't care Stephens says. He's a boast against another, anyhow. And mebbe she'll come round and see things different from what she does now." "She goes out o' this 'ouse to-night, or I do, that's all," pronounced Fan. I had been almost too giddy to stand at first, but now I groped to the wall, where I had hung up my hat and coat, after I had come in, hours ago. "I —I want to go," I stammered. "Don't worry, mother. I shall be all right. I won't stoop to defend myself. And I—know people who will take me in. This must have come sooner or later, I see now. I'll write to you, and—and by-and-bye I'll send you money, I hope. Good- bye. You have meant to be kind." . "But----.. my mother had begun to falter, when Fan' cut her slue. "Don't you begin with no 'buts !' " she cried. "I won't rave 'em. I'm the breadwinner, I am. ryi-got the right to sye who stops or goes." Her great black eyes dominated my mother. The girl was like some savage young animal at bay. I did not wonder that the two who knew her moods best cowered before her now. There was a look on her face, with the twitching lips apart shewing strong white teeth, which said that she might do me a mischief if I defied her. I took my mother's shaking hands and pressed them. Perhaps 'I ought to have kissed her, but somehow I could not. I murmured something , confused about seeing her again, and then, when I had given the sobbing Totsey a fleeting embrace, I pushed blindly pest the group of curious ones who had gathered in the passage, and went out into the open air. I had told my mother that I had friends who would take me in if I asked, and I had not deliberately uttered a falsehood. I had only said the first thing that occurred to me, with the desperate impulse to escape. Indeed, there were doubtless people in the old set from whioh I had vanished who would receive me if I threw myself con their kindness, but I had no intention of esing that. Dusk was falling early, for a storm threatened. *lmre were faint grumblings of thunder, which I was scarcely conscious of hearing. Great purple cloud-banks boiled up from the west and over- spread the sky. As I ran out into the street, a big drop or two of rain splashed down upon my upturned face. The man Welcome, just coming home from his unknown "business," caught sight of me as I left the house, and I saw by his eye that it was in his mind to follow. I quickened my pack, and hurried round the corner. Then I began to run, flying through street after street until I knew that he must hare lost scent. At last I stooped, bewildered and completely lost.. CHAPTER XII. - - wevaaLoo mamas.</p>
        <p>It teemed to me that I had come to the end of the world—my world. Nobody wanted me. Nobody *wed. It would be better if I were dead. Rain began to pour down in sheets, and 111 Vas</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>loon wet to the skin. The streets were emptying, for people had hurried into shelter from the storm, Ind only here and there could be seen an umbrella, black as a scurrying bat over the pavement, that shone with rain beneath the gas-lamps. It occurred to me that I might stand under the protection of some doorway until the worst of the storm should be over; but in my doll despair it seemed hardly worth while to care for the wretched body which had ceased to be of importance to me or anyone else. So I went through the downpour, stolidly; and at last, when I had walked for a very long time, and it was really dark, I came to a great bridge over the Thames.</p>
        <p>It was in my way, going straight on, to come to it; and I proceeded at first steeped in the strange apathy that was part of my condition. But ml eyes fell upon the black water flowing underneath: and I stopped suddenly, fascinated. It was as it someone had spoken in my ear, saying: "There no place for you in the world. Perhaps there ma. 9 be, somewhere else—somewhere beyond. You could find out here."</p>
        <p>The thunderstorm was long ago over, but rain still fell heavily and steadily. Except when an omnibus rumbled by, there was scarcely anyone on the bridge, and nobody who passed paid any attention to me, as I cowered close to the stone wall in one of the embrasures. In my black dress and hat, my slim little figure must have melted into the shadows of night.</p>
        <p>Two forces in my nature began warring together as I looked down at the dark, flowing water, with a thought in my mind that never in my life had harboured there before.</p>
        <p>"You are young," one voice said. "Only eighteen. It is pitiful that you, who have been petted and loved, and told that you were beautiful, should die like this. Who knows what the years may yet hold for you if you stay and see ? "</p>
        <p>But the •other voice answered: "It's no use. ' What can a girl like you do against the world? You haven't a penny. You can't buy even a piece of bread to eat, and you can't ask for charity. , You're only a little grain of dust, not of any importance at all. Thousands of girls as young as you must have died in this way. It saved them from worse things, and it will save you. You tolt4 Roger Cope that you would rather die than marry him. Well, now you can die. And when knows, perhaps he will be sorry that he hounde, you to such an end; for he will really be more to, blame than you." "That's a coward's argument I" the first voice, cried. "It would be brave to fight the battle out, weak to throw down your arms at the first attack." "No, it's brave to die. It takes courage to throw yourself over into the cold, deep river, and not struggle or scream for help at the last; to breathe the water into your lungs and be choked by it till you drown. If you don't do this thing—now, when you are helpless, without money or friends who would not think it a great burden to help you—it is because you are afraid of the suffering, the horror."</p>
        <p>"It is a wicked thing, an unforgivable crime, to destroy your own life," appealed the first voice.</p>
        <p>"No, for God is merciful, and pardons all sins," said the other. "He will understand. Do it— now, while you have the courage I " A whirlwind seemed to snatch my soul in a re- sistless grasp. Quick as lightning I sprang up on the stone seat. Blind and deaf save to the tempest of my own spirit, I put life behind tue—stretched out my arms to death. I thought I was falling, that I had thrown myself over. It was as if my body were lifted by the force of my own passionate self-abandonment. For an instant I knew nothing. Then a man's voice spoke close to ray ear. "Poo, child 1 For Heaven's sake what has brought you to this?" I had not fallen. The river was not to have mt. after all. This man's hands had dragged me back, snatching me from the stone bench and holding me fearfully to his side. I did not answer his question. I don't think that he expected it. I did not even struggle to free myself, for the conviction of his strength robbed me of all mine, as he half-carried, half-led me off the bridge. I had not seen his face, know- ing only that he was tall and very, very strong; but suddenly a light dazzled my eyes, and, scarcely realising what I did, I looked up at him. At the same instant his eyes were on my face, and by the exclamation of astonishment he gave. I guessed that it was for the first time to-night. "Great Heavens I" he ejaculated. As he recognised me, so did I know him again. It was the man who had looked up from the pitS to our box at the Lyceum Theatre on the night of "The Bells "•, the man who had followed' me in a cab when I drove away; the man who had saved me and my money from the hands of a thief. "You 1" I whispered, half beneath my breath. The word must have told him that I had not forgotten, but I was unconscious of its meaning. And suddenly I began to cry. My hands went up to my face, wet with cold rain, and the burning tears which gushed from the eyes that I hid lest he should see them. I did Ist know why the sight of this man had broken down all my self-control, but I sobbed heavy, tearing sobs that seemed well-nigh to wrench body and soul asunder.</p>
        <p>"That it should be you of all women on earth," he said. "I thanked Heaven that I was in time to save a life; but that it should have been your life 1 Now I thank Heaven a hundred times over." "I wish you'd let me diet" I sobbed. "You won't wish that—to-morrow."</p>
        <p>"There are no to-morrows for me," I answered. "I've come to the end of everything. And now— you've brought me back, to go through it all, all over again. Let me go!'Yu had no right—you shall let me go." "I shall not," he said, holding me tightly, as for the first time I began to struggle in his grasp. "I have the right, not to ask questions, but to keep you from doing a thing you would never have dreamt of doing if some trouble hadn't driven you half-mad for a moment. I had the right to do that, and because I have saved you I have another right as well. Forget me hp-morrow if you like, but now you Will have to Ilbey me. Tell me the address of some relative or friend whom you love, and I will drive with you there in a cab. I refuse to leave you until I know that you are in 'safe bands."</p>
        <p>"You said that you had not the right to ask questions," 1 exclaimed. "Not to ask questions about yourself, or the trouble which has driven you out of your senses. But there are others which, for your own sake, must and will ask. Tell me that address." "There is no such address," I said, bitterly, still fighting with my tears. "I have no relatives or friends to whom I can go." "Then I will take you to mine." As he spoke he raised his hand to the driver of a cab, who saw the gesture from a distance and whipped up his horse. "I can't—l will not—" I had begun, when he stopped me. "Why ? Because you're afraid of me? Because you don't know me, and so can't trust me ?" "No—o ;it isn't that." "Then come. You must." "I will not." The cab had drawn up at the pavement and he had stopped me beside it. "Do you know what I shall do if you refuse?" His voice was very firm, even stern. . .</p>
        <p>, "No. Except that you will let me go." "I will not let you go. Do you think I don't know what you would do if I left you alone as you wish? Ido know. And rather than you should have the chance I will give you in charge to the police for—attempting to kill yourself. I would cut off my hand rather than do such • thing; nevertheless, I will if you force me to it." I uttered a little broken cry of shame and horror. "You are cruel," I said.</p>
        <p>"Only to be kind. • If you would let me take you to a friend of sour own—lint you say Ow no one. You say ydi trust me. Prove it, I beg or you." you begged things on that other night," I ,exclaimed, in bitterness. "To-night, because lam the most miserable creature in all this miserable world, you have commanded."</p>
        <p>"You think that the reason? You think that makes the difference? Well, what does your opinion of me matter? AU that signifies is that you should be saved and then protected. Will you get into this cab now? If you are afraid of me, I will tell the man where to drive and let you go alone. But I warn you that I will follow and watch that you take no unfair advantage of such liberty." I hesitated. "You—surely do not mean what you amid about the police?" "I_swear that I do mean it."</p>
        <p>"WroT;e11:"- I got into the cab. Leaning out I use on the point of saying, r4ekless of consequmces, that I would go to an hotel where I was kuown, when suddenly everything reeled and turned black, and then—then the world ceased to exist for me for a little while.</p>
        <p>After a time—l do not know how long—l began dimly, dreamily to rememher that there was an I. But I remembered nothing elae, nothing at all of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>wnat had happened. I thought that I had been !asleep, and I felt an extreme disinclination to wake ;up. "Oh, see, sir, her eyelids are trembling!" said ft woman. "Use the sal volatile again," directed a man's voice, which I seemed to know. It was this which convinced me that I must rouse myself. With an effort I opened my eyes, to look straight up into a face that brought back a whirling rush of recollections. Once more I was in the Lyceum Theatre glancing carelessly about, my gaze concentrating at last on the features of a man which appeared strangely dependable. Once more the thought was in my mind: "He would be a good man to ask if you needed help." Why, he had helped me twice It was curious that I could remember the occasions, but not the ways. Thee everything flashed back to nee. I was in a plain room, not large, but stately compared to those I had known of late in Easel- street. The walls were lined with books in dark bindings, that absorbed the light which came from a tall, green-shaded student lamp standing on an open desk. Near this desk I was lying in a deck- chair extended to its full length, while above me bent the face I knew and another face that was strange. The latter wore round spectacles that gave the eyes a surprised look, and it had a quaint little nose which turned up in such - comical manner that I found myself smiling. "How did l come here?" I asked, in a low, tired voice. It was the man who answered. "In the cab. Just as I was waiting for your decision to know whether I was to go with you or to follow, you settled the question by fainting. Sc I brought you here, because I knew that Mrs. Jennett would take good care of you. And when I am sure that you are quite well I will go away." Somehow my heart sank at the last words. I did not want him to go away—though I took myself instantly to task for the feeling. ' "You're as pale as a little ghost, miss," said Mrs. Jennett, of the turned-up nose. "Drink this, won't you? I've been making it hot." She held a cup to my lips, and I drank some chocolate, which tasted better than anything I had ever tasted before. I could feel the blood begin to flow more warmly through ray chilled veins. "And your clothing is soaked through and through, miss," went on Mrs. Jennett. "Would you be offended if I should offer you some things of mine till your own can be dried ? " "Of course not," I protested. "You are very kind. kit I can't stay here to trouble you. I must be going soon." "We'll talk about that again to-morrow," said she. "If you're strong enough to come to my room, I'll make you comfortable inside ten minutes.' She slipped an arm behind my shoulders, and lifted me up. But, as-I tried to get on my feet, I discovered that I was not half as strong as I had hoped. Sparks fell in a shower before my eyes; I turned giddy, and would have sunk back again—for Mrs. Jennett was a little woman and could not give much help—had not the man caught me in his arms as if I had been a baby. "o:ien the door," he said. Mrs. Jennett ran to obey, and before I could speak I found myself being carried upstairs. Another door was opened, and I was deposited in an easy-chair, near a clean, white bed. "Lucky this was my day for changing the linen," I heard the little woman exclaiming. "Now every- thing's all ready for her just as it is." "How do you feel ? " asked the man, looking at me. In saving me from a fall and catching me in his arms to carry me here from the room below his coat-sleeve had been pushed back. As be spoke he pulled the white cuff and dark brown sleeve into place, his left arm still bared from above the wrist. My eyes fell upon it, and I gave a loud, startled cry. "What is it? Have I hurt you F" he asked, quickly. By this time he had rearranged both sleeves, and, seeing it no more, I could hardly believe in the actual existence of the strange thing which I had caught sight of for an instant. In my astonishment I had sat up, and continued to stare at him, my lips apart. "Mrs. Jennett, make haste with the sal volatile " he exclaimed. "I'm afraid that she will faint again." But I gently pushed the sal volatile away when it was obediently presented to my nostrils. I could speak now.</p>
        <p>"No,-no," I said.. "I'm not ill. But—" And I looked straight up into the man's anxious face. "Tell me who you are ? " He laughed. "I ought to have told you that before," he replied. "But, you see, I had so little chance. My name is John Bourke." "Yes, miss," proudly broke in Mrs. Jennett. "And he's the John Bourke. Oh, you may scowl and shake your head, Mr. Bourke—but only fancy her not knowing." I knew no more now than I had known before, since the name of John Bourke suggested nothing. All my thoughts were concentrated, however, not on what I heard, but what I had seen. He guessed this, and his eyes questioned mine in a puzzled way. "Why did you cry out and suddenly ask for my name ?" he demanded. "Don't tell me if you would rather not," he hastily added. "But--" "I would rather tell!" I spoke on the impulse of curiosity, keen as the stab of a knife. "I cried out because—l saw something strange on your arm. A heart-shaped scar." (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
