      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUIS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUIS.</p>
        <p>Annual Gala. j One of the na4)st attractive galas ever held under the auspices of the Warrington S winaming Club took Placl at tbH Corporation Baths on Saturday evening. 'Mr° ' was a large attendant- of spectators, who thorougblY appreciated the capital programme submitted, TO Mayor (Alderman II Roberts),presided,and in the cootie of the evening spoke briefly up ,n the value of the 110 of swimming. The premier attraction was the water polo match, North v Midlands, which resulted in a victor) for the N )rth by four goals to one. Considerable interest centred in the race for the championstliP of Warrington, which carries with it 0° Licensed V:ctuallers' Perpetual Challenge Trophy, Tbe event was very keenly contested the finish being an el' ceptionally tight one. The first position was secured by W Ashton, who only obtained the honour by inning. R Herter came in second and W Creighton third. Another veryjnteresting item was the contest for tbe Thornton Perpetual Challenge Bowl (school WV cbampionsbip)- Thomas Pembertoa, of People's Colleger swam splenOidly and easily came in first. fie is thus tile holder of the trophy for the second year in succession Jialin Wain wright gained second place and Stanley ince third. In the 50 yards' members handicap J Warree came in first, C Ashton second and A Buakioy third. During the evening rtliSC Christina Duckworth gave 5,0 extremely creditable display of ornamental and trio Swimming.</p>
        <p>THE POLO MATCH. Several alterations had haen made in the testa originally in'ended to represent the North. Owing to family bereavement F Battersby, of Stockport, was un- able to take Ms position at half back. This was diger pointing, but even greater d'sappointment was es' I pressed when it was notified that Jfi Derbyshire, the ex-amateur champion, was unable to figure in the for ward division. E Robinson, also an internatiorie` , player was away from the back division. Able substitutes were, however, found in T Wildgoose, of Hyde, J W Shaw, of Osborne, and J Gommersall af Everron. The teams lined UP as follows :—North; A G Robertson (Warrington), goal; T Coe and J 8 Gamersall (Everton), backs ; T Wild' goose (Hyde), half back ; and J W Shaw (Osborne), e V Swift (Wigan), and S Roney (Swan), forwards. Mid; lands ; G H Taberer (Leicester), goal ;U E Saarp ana J E Taylor (Leicester), backs ; J Lakin (Leicester), hal! back ; and J A Jarvis (Leicester), J Snow (rreicester)l and F B Daniels (Aston) forwards. Jarvis, the Mdlaaa captain, who has performed such smart records during the present season, was the cynosure of all eyes, Like I Tiers and Nottall he is the beau ideal of a cbanapioti ' swimmer. Losing the toss his team had to defend the deep end. 'the opening phases of the march were wit- nessed in deep water. Coe and Wedgoose were instra• mental in maintaining the aggressive, and weak efforte to clear by the Midland hack % almost caused disaster. Jarvis, however, cleared effectively, and the cheers- were prolonged when Robertson effected a wonderful clear' ance. The North renewed the attack and were more sue; cerf4ul than in the first venture, for a coneertea movement between the trio of forwards resulted le Shaw turning a clever pass to good account. NO sooner had the ball been taken from the centre than Coe shot at long range, and only a partial clearance being effected Shaw again scored a few seconds before the interval. Suocess attended the Midland team 00 resuming, lor after Lakin had led the way to the deeP end Jarvis scored with an oblique shot. This reverSe bad a good effect on the North team. Their combior tion improved as the game proceeded, and the material° of a sequence of transfers resulted in Swift adding • third goal, while a beautiful piece of combinatioa between Wildgoese and Shaw saw the latter defeat Tabeier frun a short range. The final score read North, four goals, Midlands one. After the match the executive of the Northern Coda' ties Swimming Association met and selected the follow' log team to represent the North against the Seuth London to-day. Goal, C 8 Smith, Wigan ; backs,T Coe (captain) and E Robinson, Osborne ; half-back, Y., Wildgoose, Hyde ; forwards, P Ripley, (Hyde), tr Wilkinson (Osborce),and F V Swift (Wigan.) Reserve, W Gallagher (Openshaw), and J W Shaw (Osborne.). 110, REGAI TA AND SWIMMING GALA' AT ROCKSAVAG E.</p>
        <p>List of Winners. The Runcorn Boatinv, Club held toeir first annoal. regatta and swimming gala, on the Weaver Canal, 0: Rocksavage, on Saturday afternoon, and despite f.l3';', unfavourable atmospheric conditions, the venture !al' attended with a considerable measure of success. Bart Joseph Stokes (chairman of the Runcorn Board o daardians) and Mr J. Griffiths, R ' ocksavage b*d kinißl put their land on either sine of the Canal at the diarot of the Boating Club Committee, aud by means2,f canvas screens, bunting, flags, &amp;c., quite an adrairau'r g for aquatic sports was made by the energetic ones o that body. The weather brightened up consideral about 2.30 and gave promise of a Mae, often:noel,' !Is' about 5 o'ch.ch heavy showers of rain began to interfrrat the proceedings and delay the programme, What ten'd there was, however, proved of a very high order, the swimming ok Mr A, Manning, of Birkenhead,eVo',;(4, considerable praise. ' The polo match between LiverP":s Nomaos and Chester Amateurs, which resulted in a 01, for the former by 4 goals to one, was a most interestioo item, as wan also the exhibition of life savinq. kelt At the conclusion of the progrAmme Mrs J (Rocksavage) presented the prizes to the succe competitors. ota Mr W Millinger (bon. secretary) moved a hearty V A of thanks to Mrs. Stokes for distributing the prizes, a 0: to all those who had assisted the club in making r.b° gala so successful. Mr .1 Stokes, on behalf of his wife, briefly acknoW• ledged the vote of thanks, and expressed the hope tbst the gala would be b.t the first of maay sucsessful 00° to follow. The weather had been against them a little. but be felt certain that all who had witnessed the sportS had thoroughly enjoyed them. (Applause.) The proceedings were brought to a close with a 104/` gramme of dance music admirably played by Crosfield's SoaplWorks Brass Band, Warrington, Mr E Staltl3 conducting. The officials included :—Official handicapper Mr 3 $ Mellody ; starter and timekeeper : M- 8 Fall ; judges Messrs J Stokes,J Griffithsa Hurstfield,H E Lang; eo' mittee .1 W Wiswall,F Abel, A Abel,R Bennion,G Moore. S Bankes, A E Ward,J G Millington. I 0 Williams ;.t013,.' secretary : Mr W A Milliner, 36, Trentham-streepf Runcorn. The several reaults were as folio,. 9 : Fifty yards boys' swimming race(4 miles radius).-4.4 prize, silver watch ; 2nd, centre-second watch ; 3' photo album. First neat W Sutton, Widnes; J Dwyeto Widnes. Second beat : J Barnes, Widnes ; A Haig,. Frodsham. Third beat ; E S Skellon,Widnes •, W Hayes, Runcorn. Fourth beat : J T Rougheedge, Frodshara..i Bignall, Weston. Final :1, J Bigaall (12) 2. Sutton, Widnes (4) ; 3, J Dwyer, Widnes (scratch). , Quarter-mile single pair sculls (handicap)..-15_,t1 marble clock; 2nd, camera. Ist beat, W Thorns: (w. 0. ; 2nd beat, It Johnson (w. 0. ; 3rd heat,. .4 Savage (3secs.); 4th heat, G A Williamson (Seem) ; sr„" heat, J W Wiswall (7secs.) Final: 1, S W Wiswall ;st , J Johnson. . • Seventy yards members swimming race.-Ist prizt" silver cup (presented by C N Irven, Esq.) 200+ mandoline. Ist, F Abel (Ogees.) ; 2nd, 9 Fall (scrateb). Two hundred and twenty yards swimming race„ (scratcb).-Ist prize, massive marble clock ; 2nd, O'f, eaters ; 3rd, tourist.' glasses. Ist heat, A Mannina Birkenhead ; J C Healey, Liverpool ; H Sandford, Cher ter. 2nd heat, A E Edwards, Chester; J A Barker. Evertnn ;A E Perkins, Liverpool. Final; Ist heart . Manning; 2nd, J A Barker; 3rd, A E Edwards. Pa first, second and third half round.. Greasy pole.—lst (writing desk), A Bennett. Quarter mile double pair scull race (lady sreerer)Pri,r,„" three umbrellas 1, Mecums W Thomas and F Abel, fdls' B Thomas (steerer). One hundred yards awimming race handicap (operq.! Ist, case of cutlery; 2nd biscuit barrel ; 3rd, smoker.: cabinet ; Ist heat, A Furness, Warrington, SC; W Harpur, Liverpool Gymnasium. S C ; 2arl heat. Boulton, Chester; W Jaysson, Widnes; 3rd beat, A Fee Liverpool SA; A E Perkins,Edge Lane S C,4th heatei Byes, Everton, S AtJ W Wood, Liverpool Gymnasium; SC. Final; 1, A Furness ; 2, A Frost; 3.J W WOor7, Obstacle swimming race.—lat (spirit tantalus), Frost, Liverpool ; Zad (brief bag), A E Perkins, Edga' Lane. ot Owing to the rain the Club championship was 0 0 decidd, hence the important event will be run ° Saturday next. Mari</p>
