        <p>At the Scarborough Police Court, on Monday, —, - Cook, an elderly woman, residing at No. 2, London 11 Yard, Newborougb.erreet, Scarborough, was Cbarg.„ with having professed to tell fortune&amp; The evidene' was that on July 1 Edith Stevenson and Jane Atkinsol went to tbe accused's house to have their fortunes tol 2 The woman shuffled a pack of cards and told Stevensol; to cut them, which she did. The defendant said de, from the cards Stevenson would have trouhle wit"e. dark man. Stevenson paid her 13 for the fortii°, telling. Stevenson,in reply to Mr Royle (for the clefene...l.° admitted that she had been to the defendant's sece times previously to hive her tortune told.—Mr ROVI4; About this dark man. Have you met him ?--Y.'3s. Royle : Weil, then, she told you the truth, for wL"" • you ought to be grateful.—The magistrates imPosecif to fine of £5, including costs, with the alternative. oti month's imprisonment, and refused time in will C pay the money.</p>
