        <p>&amp; HI th toet, ther4 atee tOn, BUtti Bain 8,11, itith (dein Dem</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. Important Business. A re th eating of the Warringtm Town Council was held evg? Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Tuesday tli3i^a'ag. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided and Or Were also present : —Aldermen Holton, Shaw ekoicks. Councillors Smethurst, Tinnion, Plump- B 1114:11fiam Roberts. Greeniog, Brough, F R Roberts, Belleen Slmpson, Peacock, Jam-‘s Evans, Shillcock, 13:4"2, John R )berts, Davies, Malkin, John Evans, °Mete, Duncan, A J Jolley, Ellison, A Jolley, Rogers, With Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), 8 Wilson ilenuty town clerk), T Longdin (borough surveyor), J vps (water engineer), W S Haddock (gas engineer), Grimsdale (electrical engineer). THE LATE EMPRE3S FREDERECK. IRE MAYOR'S rßourE TO HER MEMORY. segiefore the commencement of the ordinary business the rti'tse'or said it was his sad duty to have to move a rage- p.„...len with regard to the death of the Empress `oerick. He moved : "That this Coune..ll has heard Ilt..tb deep regret of the death of e Empress Frederick, ,sllnthe "" desires to exoress to his Majeety the King and the 82Mbers of the Royal Family its profound and heartfelt tYotnloattly in their sad bereavement." The Mayor, helletinuing, said they would probably know as much as iv' ',Oneself did of the history of the late Empress, and he let sure he was only expressing theirand his own views *reels be said that a more able or more honourable te-e'isle had never occupied a similar position When set? remembered that she was married in a country litZe distance away, at a very early age, the domestic te;illt which was in some respects very different from eurrounding her childhood, and when they remem- rhe splendid influence for good, especially among !omen of her adopted country the late Empress 151,;eihe magnifieent fight she made that her late hies- Might occupy his position on the throne of kOlamy. and finally the splendid struggle she tqls against the fell disease which was Illee Precursor of the rnd—a display of bravery cf, were only accustomed to aseociate with men 02:e these circumstances combined with the fact that ee'wea a child of our own laud made her memory very eik to us. They felt with the King and the Royal llY.who had suffered one tiereavement after another. iiirse circumstances proved that sorrow was not con- toa the poor, but found its way into every house. them to their Sovereign to strehgthen the ties which bound Alderman S&amp; Sovereign. his court and family. t le ;iv GREEN seconded, and the resolution was unanimo Passed. THE LATE DR. GORNALL. IS SERVICES TO THE TOWN AND CORPORATION. The Meyon said that a resolution,as follows, had been Llseited by the Health Committee : " That the commit- record7 their deep regret at the loss which the Court. lii.trid.the town have sustained by the death of the ogi;l,lcal Officer of Health, and their warm appreciation iLOO zeal and devotion displayed by him in the efficient B eaeslerge of his duties in the several public offices `e'itti he filled during a period of 23 years." He now otreed this as a resolution, expressing the sincere sym- erY of:the Council in similar terms, and also directing Gilt a Copy of the resolution should be sent to Mrs h 'Mall with an expression of condolence with her and ti(iie family in their sad bereavement.He explained That in e absence of Alderman Sutton, the chairman of the tieeealtia Committee, it fell to hie lot t move the resolu- He had no fear that there would be any difference 442 Pinion in the Council as to the assiduity, the fidelity 0,21 loyalty with which the late Dr Gornall had carried b'elt IS duties to the town and Corporation. They had y ell singularly fortunate in their officials for many p.ers past,and he did not think any ot ier officer would .73.nt it when he said that none had had a more trB,2a.gle eyc" to the interests of the town than the late v!inical officer. He could speak of him in other capaci- s, and there had beer a strong bond of friendship Zrween Dr Hornell and himself lasting nearly thirty :f..08. but beyond that and with regard to his public teevice he knew sufficient to always respect his memory , zld remember his work for the town. Alderman SHAW GREEN seconded. He said he had enown Dr Gornall all the time he had lived in Warring- ton and to know him was to appreciate him, but they had to know him well to appreciate him at his true value. What he had done forth° town of Warrington Would not appear yet, but would bear fruit in years to tome. The vote was unanimously carried. THE NEW POLICE BUILDINGS. PROGRESS OF THE WORK. z,At a meeting of the Watch Committee held on July the architects having informed the Committee that s Vice premises room and the magisterial part of the new V i ice premises would be ready for use early n October, 4, was resolved that the Mayor be requested to (mem the dee on the 11th of October nee' ; also that a suitable e;glet be placed In the entrance of the premises. The krnittee further resolved that tenders be obtain d 1p„,kll goods and materials provided for in the lump sum el t, uded in the contract,and that the Paying and Sewer- totternittee be requested to complete the paving of .riore-courts. e MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minute*. Icierman MONKS, in seconding, asked it the whole o se,"eenew police buildings would be ready. It did not th,. to hitrithat they would be ready,* and he took it In the event of the whole of the premises not being 717, the opening would be postponed. toee e hie Mesas explained that the whole of the premises lo or+ not be ready at this date, and that if they would er, at the resolution of the Watch Committee they rti?llid notice that the architects had informed the com- wittee thet the sessions room and the magisterial part teLllt buildings would be ready, and this would enable 14'n to get away from the present premises, where the teeeet.nodings were extremely unpleasant, and where nad had to spend so much time. iee'tuncillor TINNION asked if arrangements for receive the cells and so forth would be ready. Dre MAYOR replied that the cells were ready at the tha4ene time, and in reply to a further question, said Ittp„,the magisterial portion would be ready, and there jee'd be no inconvenience. ne Minutes were then confirmed. THE TRAMWAYS SCHEME.: SANCTION OF THE LOAN, g, LONDON COUN TY COUNCIL AND INSURANCE</p>
