      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>COMPANIES.</head>
        <p>COMPANIES.</p>
        <p>At 11,44 a taeqincz of the Finance and Eitate Committee, do, gri the 22-id July letter was read from the Lon- "'Ay Council calling attention to the report of D'neet Committee of the House of Commons on the c0,,,'"10n of fire brigades. &amp;o and to the proposal of the retaittee.that all fire insurance oompanies should be RS • reC. by law to bear a certain portion of the tll 8 Connection with fire extinction, and requesting t"linti of the Council thereon. It was resolved Rata,'" committee approve of the proposal. At the gkiest:n,"ing the committee resolved that at the re- the Street Improvement and Lighting 14 Commit. t.. ,o_"btices Lo served to terminate the tenancies of the DlorttY at the corner of Knutsford-road and Wilders- e44sewaY. The following letter was read from the</p>
        <p>Board of Trade:--"Railway Department, 7, Whitehall- gardens, London, 4th July.. 1901. Warrington Corpora- tion Tramways Ordrr, 1900. Sir,—With reference to Previous corraspondcnCel ern directed by the Boara of Trade to transmit herewith an order s motioning the borrowing by the Warrington Corporation nf a sum of sixty thousand and fifty pouads for the purpose of the above-named order.—l an., sir, your obedient ser- vant, T W P BLOMEFIELD. The Town Clerk Warring- ton." Alderman J Snew GREEN moved the adoption of the minutes. Alderman Mcosits seconded. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION asked what was the amount of rentals which would he lost by the closing of the pro- perty at the corner of Knutsford-road and Wilderspool causeway. Alderman Sam Gam; said the decision to give the notice closing the premises had been made at the request of the SJeet Improvement sod Lighting Corn rnittee, who contended that the time had now arrived when the premises should be taken down, and when it was necessary to have a wider space at the point, and consequently notices were served. The rentals received for the premises were not much, and it was Possibly desirable in the general interest that they should be taken down. Tnough the committee had striven against losing anything they felt that the buildings seemed such a reasonable proposal that they could not but accept it. In reply to Councillor SMETHURST, Alderman SHAW GREEN explained that tho question of fire insurmice companies being required by law to bear a certain portion of the expenses in connection with the provision of fire engines and fire appliances had been before the London County Council sad they were of opinion that such companies did not contribute suffi- ciently large sums in return for the benefits they re- ceived from the municipalities who had fire engine appliances. Consequently they submitted this resolution to them. The London Wunty Council were also deter- mined to have an insurance fund of their own in the future. Large municipalities were taking this matter up, and it might be that municipal insurance would be an important factor in the future. The minutes were adopted. THE MANCHESTER PORT SANT CARY AUTHORITY,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S CON fRIBUTIONS. THE CONDITION OF SANKEY BROOK. " NUISANCE INCREASING YEAR BY YEAR" INTERESTING DISCUSSION. At a meeting of the Health Committee the CHAIRXIAN reported that he had attended with the Town Clerk and Borough Surveyor before the House of Commons on the Bill to confirm the Manchester Port Sanitary Order, that the case for the Corporation had been very ably and very clearly placed before the committee by counsel, but that as the Bill was strongly supported by the Local Government Board the object of the Corporation to be excluded from the order was not successful. The Chairman further reported that the Bill was altered by the exclusion of Whiston from representation and contribution, and that their proportion of the expenses would fall upan the group inoluding Widnes,Wirral,and Chester. It was resolved that • petition be presented against,and that the Town Clerk be instructed to oppose the Bill in the House of Lords. At a later meeting, the Council being required by the General Order of the Local Government Board to give notice of their intention to appoint a Medical Officer of Health, the committee resolved that such notice be given. At the same meet- ing of the committee,a letter having been received from the Public Health Cemmittee of the Lancashire County Council respecting the condition of Sankey Brook and requesting the Corporation to treat the sewage from the borough discharged into it, it was rrsolved that "the committee be prepared to j in with other authorities concerned in considering a scheme for effectively dealing with the condition of the brook, and that they' Will treat the sewage from this borough on receiving an assurance that all other authorities will treat the se age from their respective districts so as to stop the pollution of the brook." In moving the adoption of the minutes the Mayor said he was sorry that their opposition to the Order:in- cluding them in the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority bad not been successful and they had to be satisfisd, if they would allow it to be put that way, to pay for something the cause and necessity for which they had it to sense contri•uted to and they were compelled by law to assist in bearing part of the liabilities of an opulent city like Manchester, and a wealthy borough like Salford, who ought to bear their own burdens. (Hear, hear.) With regard to Sankey Brook, the com- mittee hid received a letter from the Lancashire County Council, requesting them to treat the sewage from the borough discharged into it. It was true that they might to s:me extent contribute to the condition of Sankey Brook, but their c mtribution was an extremely small matter and in fact infinitesimal when compared with that the tcae Countymefrom elsewhere. Therefore he was of opinion h Council and other authorities concerned ought to invite the Local Government Board to hold an inquiry and agree to abide by the decision of that board as to what course should be taken in the purification of this brook. It would be the height of absurdisy for them to deal with their small amount of sewage while so large a volume of filth was psured in higher up the brook. As soon as the other people were prepared to move in the matter, that Couneil was prepared to move but as small contributors they did not think they should begin first. Councillcr BAIRD seconded. Councillor TINNIOF said he did not like the way in which the matter was put in the resolutioe of the com- mittee. So long as the brook was allowed to remain in is present condition it was a very serious evil and a very serious danger to the health of the into btaants of the neighbourhood. He did not agree with the resolution. He would move an amendment omitting the words, "and that they will treat the sewage from this borough on receiving an assurance that all other authorities will treat the sewage from their respective districts," and hence the amendment took the following form :—"That the commitVe will be prepared ta join with the other authorities concerned ia considering a scheme for effec- tively dealing with the condition of the brook . . . so as to stop the pollution of the brook." Continuing, Councillor Tinnion said the matter should be taken hold of bodily. He would not say the resolution was shelv- ing the question, but it was not meeting it with the spirit of determination with which they snould meet it. Tne Mayor had already said that the pollution on the part of Warrington was in flni esimal, and from this be took it that the cost of dealing with that part of the sewage would be likewise infinitismal, but thtt term could not be used with regard to the advantage to the borough which would result provided they could have that brook fairly cleansed and cleared out and be able to prevent other authorities from polluting it. He theaugnt it was a matter that should be now tiken in hand, and the amendment that he proposed would show that they were prepared to do something. It' did not commit them to any definite scheme. In the past they had passed many resolutions, but did not appear to be any nearer a satisfactory conclusion. The County Council was now moving in the matter, and he believed in con- sequence of communications received from the many inhabitants living along the banks of the brook. HP thought the Warrington Corporation and the Lanca- shire County Council should join hands, and they would then be able to remedy this very dangerous and serious evil in their midst. The other authorities including</p>
        <p>Helens, which he believed was the most important con- tributor to the condition of the brook, would have to give way before the powdr which would be enforced by the Lancashire County Cauneil and Warriogton Town Council. He also expressed tue hope that the committee would prepare an effective schema, and that the matter was within reasonable distance of being altered Alderman 13.41,T0N, in a cooding, said he supported the I amendment because it committed them to nothing and. -was sufficient. lie did not think they needed to qualify their expression of willingness to remedy the condition of things, and it was best to leave their bands untied in getting rid of the nuisance. Councillor PLUMPTON supported and said that,. in his opinion the amendment was not qaite strong enough. (Laughter). When he read the resolution of the com- mittee he wasi extremely surprised. Ile never saw any- thing 40 weak-kneed. (Laughter). How long would they have to wait to get thd assurance of the other authorities ? It was not the other districts that were suffering. It was Warrington. Vyny should Warrington wait for these assurances before they took any steps? He had alwaye understood that they were very anxious to deal with the question and had thought that it was the Lancashire County !Donnell that was the stumbling-biook in the way: That body had obstructed them on the last occasion in London,and now they got a letter from the Lancashire County Council, and they said to them in reply that they were prepaped to wait until they got the assurances of the other authorities. Why could not they deal with their sewerage at once?' The MAYOR Because it will-be no use. e Councillor ProntrioN: If it wit' be no use Die setting an example to the other authorities. Let us clean ab...ut our own door first. Continuing he said so long as they were contributors they could not take any action against Se Helens. St. Helene was a larger town than Warrington and they were receiving all the sewage from St Helene in addition to that from Other places, down the brook. Be was informed on very good authority that if the pollution continued the Winwick Asylum authorities were going to turn their sewage into the brook. If the Winwick authorities were I going to turn in their sewage from their hundreds of patients and the general staff, what condition would they expect the brook to be in? He could not pass along the streets without people complaining to him about the nuisance. If he told them they were trying to remedy matters, the people would retort "But what are you trying?" Tney could only reply that when the other districts would give an assurance they would put themselves right. He was pleased to hear the remarks of the Mayor suggesting the holding of a Local Govern- ment Board inquiry. That was what in his opinion they really wanted. They ought to join hands with the Lancashire County Council and petition for an inquiry. The property owners and tenantry,load,he remarked ,done all they could, and it was the duty of that Corporation to encourage and strengthen as far as possible the hand of the Lancashire County Council. If they could get an inquiry it would, in his opinion, satisfy everyone. Councillor BENNETT also supported. He felt it was impossible to speak too strongly. The condition of the brook was a " crying shame." He also questioned as to which authority had jurisdiction over the brook. The MAYOR said therein lay the main difficulty as no one had authority and the Local Government Board could not act in the matter only by invitation. Councillor BENNETT asked whether in the event of the Local Government Board being asked by all the authori- ties to hold an inquiry they would have full power to compel action ? The Mayon again replied in the negative. Alderman SHAW GREEN asked whether in the event of them ceasing the pollution at Warrington they could not take measures. .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR replied that what they would have to do would be to take action against every individual con- tributor and every Corporation polluting the brook and they would naturally shrink from such a huge piece of legal work. Councillor TINNION asked whether by joining hands with the Lanchshire County Council they would not have the tower of compulsion or of putting some pres- sure on the other authorities. The MAYOR again replied in the negative, aa d added that the effect of the committee's resolution might probably have some moral effect. Councillor BENNETT said he wanted to get to the bottom of thi matter. He felt that failing conciliatory means they should not shrink from legal proceedings. It was intolerable that the inhabitants of the locality should have to submit year after year to the nuisance. Councillor JAMES EVANS said he was quite as anxious as any other per6on that the nuisance should be ended and he would be willing to take part in any movement that would advance them one step in that direction. At the same time he did not think much could be done by discussing the question further unless they had the full text of the County Council's letter before them. He thought that the co-operation of the Council and the Public Health Committee of the County Council would be a step in the direction of bringing about a better condition of things. _ . _</p>
        <p>Alderman Mote's asked if they were acting wisely in saying they contaminated the brook. The MAYOR said the Medical Officer had clearly stated that there were two local sources of contribution to the stream. One from the gas works, the product of which was beneficial, as it acted as a deodoiser, and the other was the sewer outletovtich was of course crude sewage. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS said he would be very sorry if an impression went forth that any members of the Council were not interested in the matter because they did not live in the locality effected by the nuisance, ,He thought all of them, without exception were most anxious to remove any cause of nuisance. He was of the opinion that the committee's resolution went further than Councillor Tianion's amendment. As one of the representatives of the Ward through which the brook pased he had received repeated complaints from the inhabitants of the district and he should certainly do all he could to eupport the Council in any steps they might take to stop the nuisance. At the same time thererwere other nuisances in the town quite as bad, and peOple living in Museum-street had been nearly poisoned by the stench arising in that part of the town. Councillor BURTON said he was disappointed to find that they had got no further and that the Mayor said there was no remedy by which they could prevent these people polluting the brook. The MAYOR pointed out that be had not said they had no remedy, but the remedy would mean such a huge legal action that they would hesitate to undertake it. Councillor BURTON remarked that the pollution at Warrington should be remedied, since it would cost but a small amount. The MAYOR said it would cost many thousands more than was anticipated by many Councillor BURTON further remarked that of his own knowledge the nuisance was increasing rapidly year by year, and those who paid rates were fairly entitled to claim its abatement. Councillor Brionein said he could not be silent in th matter, and thoroughly endorsed all that Councillor Tianion had said. He walked along the brook on Sunr day afternoon and he had never known the smell to b- so bad before. He was like Councillor Burton, of the opinion that the stench increased year by year. Be su pp lrted the amendment. In reply, the MAYOR said the amendment was simply the expression of a pious opinion. The recommendation ot the Committee went a great deal further. The recom- mendation accepted a certain amaunt of liability and a certain amount of responsibility, and as such it would have some m )ral influence upon the other authorities contributing to the nuisance. Councillor TINNION contended that the amendmnt went further than the resolution. The MAYOR, continuing, referred to the question of the disposal of the sewage of VVinwick Asylum and sad it was a very serious problem. The asylum authorities, however, would not be allowed tie turn the sewage into Sankey Brook, because the Lancashire County Council was moving in the matter at the request of certain authorities' and its controlling influence over the Asylums Board would never allow the Board to do the v. ry thing which the Council was trying to prevent. The question which the County Council had to face was one of the very things which would force consideration and probably some kind of legislation in connection with the condition of the brook. That being the case be thought the move of the committee in showing a disposition which they had never shown before—and they were the first of all the authorities contributing to show that disposition— would assist the County Council in any movement which they might try to effect. He therefore hoped the Council would allow the resolution to stand. On its being put to the meeting only four voted for the amendment, and consequently the resolution of the committee was confirmed.</p>
        <p>The minu-,es were then approved. A LEVEL CROSSING IN PRIESTLEY STREET. TEIE LASING OF THE TRAM LINES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee it was resolved that the plans submitted by the WhitecroSs Company, Limited, for the level crossing in Priestleyt street be approved. The Borough Surveyor reported tha the manholes to the sewers in the streets in which tram- ways are now being laid would require reconstructing clear of the tramways, when it was resolved that the work be done and charged to the Tramways Committee It was'also resolved that the tender of Messrs. Bennie. and Thompsmi for the supply of 1,000 tons of 6-inch grit setts at 13s 51 per ton be accepted. rhe comm ttee also decided that the footpath at the corner of Mill-lane and Lovely-lane be altered so as to give a wider carriage way, and that the Chairman and Deputy. Chairman be deputed to settle the proposed line. An application having ben made for the sower in Odver-street. to be altered to receive the sewage from the new Wheat Sheaf Inn, it was re.olved that the alteration be made at the expense of the owners. Councillor TINNI)N, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, referred to the resolution with regard to the level crossing in Priestley-street. He said that an ex- amination of the plans showed that the Whitecross company bad done everything the committee could re- quire to be done for the construction and maintenance and the safe working of the traffic. Toe company was quite prepared to Meet the committee in every possible Iway. At a meeting of the committee on the previous</p>
        <p>night a question was raised rsgarding the levels of a good many of the streets on whien the tramways were tv be laid. The Borough Surveyor informed the com- mittee that in many instances if the tram lines were laid according to the plans and suggestions of Messrs Preeee and Carden"- they would b 3 at such a level that the new pavement joining to them would be consid r• ably higher than the ,vrassat psvemeat. A particular place mentioned was Palmyra-square. The result there would •lets that ,thi3 'wohden pavement on the whole `length of pee north side would have to be taken up and made to join with the pavenn.:nt of the tramway. The committee thought that matter should have the careful consideration of the Couzcil. The expense would be considerable. The committee did no: care to undertake it nor did they care to put it upon the Tramways Com- mittee. It was thought advisable that a joint com- mittee should go into the question and decide what should be dose. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST seconded. He thought that by the adoption of Councillor Tinnion's suggestion a large amount of money might be saved. The minutes were adopted. A NEW PICTURE FOR THE ART GALLERY. SCHOLARSHIPS RENEWED.</p>
        <p>At a in.eting of the Musxum Committee a circular was read from the Board of Education with reference to the insurance of future loans against theft and dam- age in transit, when it was resolved that the secretary take the necessary steps. It was decided that a com- mission be given to Mr H Woods, R.A., far a picture on the terms suggested by the sub-committee. The following resolutions were also passed : That the Town Council be requested to place upon the next annual estimates a sum of £2io for current expenses on tech- nical instruction account, in addition to such amount as may be necessary tor repayment of loan on new building ; that the following scholars be re-appointed for a second year : Geoffrey Bolton, Senior Middleton scholar; William H Houghton, Junior Middleton scholar ; Mary Gandy, Monks scholar ; and that two Junior Middleton scholarships be offered for competi- tion.</p>
        <p>--The MAY,OR proposed the alontion of the minutes. Councillor GREENINS seconded. Alderman BOLTON asked for information with regard to the terms of the commission given to Mr H Woods for a picture. Be supposed it was one of the usual ways of doing things and they would have to pay the piper and whistle when they got the bill. He also asked why the committee wanted 450 for current expenses and whether they were shorc of money. The MAyou said the Council always made a grant in aid of technical instruction. The committee's accounts were made up later than the borough accounts. With regard to the picture all the particulars regarding that would be submitted at the proper tinae. It was really a matter the committee was not bound to submit. They bad done it as a matter of courtesy. Councillor TINNION : We are bound to pay for it. The MAYOR said the Council would not be asked to pay for it. Some years ago they had a bequest from Mr Beamont which it was decidei to use as a purchasing account. They had added some small savings to it so that they would be able to pay for this picture when they received it. (Hear, hear.) Mr Henry Woods was a gentlemen who had reflected great credit, not only upon himself but upon his native town, and it was quite time Warrington had a specimen of the splendid work be was capable of doing, to adorn the walls of the art gallery. Mr Woods had been extremely kind and courteous in the whole matter and received their proposal in a way which would make the whole negotiations extremely pleasant, The committee would be only too pleased to submit the matter when they had got the picture. (Hear, bear and laughter.) The minutes were confirmed,</p>
      </div>
