      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>GRAVE DIGGERS TO HAVE UNIFORMS. AFTERNOON INTERMENTS.</head>
        <p>GRAVE DIGGERS TO HAVE UNIFORMS. AFTERNOON INTERMENTS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Cemetery Committee was held oti July 22nd, when the following letter from the Home Office was read:—" Home Department, Whitehall. 19th July, 1900. Sir,—With reference to your letter of the 112th inst. lam directed by the Secretary of State to say that eine fee of 3s for afternoon interments proposed Itn be payable to ministera under Section 3 of the Burial Act, 1900, appears to him under the circumstances to be hardly adequate. lam to inquire whether the Town Council are willing to suggest a fee of 33 6d instead.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, CHARLES S MIIRDWR. The Town Clerk, Warrington." It was resolved that the recommendation be agreed to. It was decided that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to report to the Chairman upon the cost of removing the drinking foun- tain from one of the cemetery lodges to a more con- venient position, and that if the Chairman is satisfied he be authorized to put the work in hand; and that four suits of uniform be provided for the use of the grave-diggers when attending funerals and that the coats and caps bear the words " cemetery officers." The minutes were approved, on the motion of Coun- cillor JOHN EvANs, seconded by Councillor DUNCAN. "TEE TEMPLE" IN THE PARK. COUNCIL DECIDE TO PULL IT DOWN. CONDITION OF THE AVIARY.</p>
        <p>At a meetieg of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee it vas resolved that the General Purposes Committee be requested to hand over to this committee the portion of "Springfield" estate proposed to be added to Queen's Gardens. At a later meeting the park keeper having reported that the aviary front was in want of repair it was resolved that the aviary be re-fronted and an estimate obtained of the cost. The head gardener haviag reported the dangerous condition of the building known as the" Temple" near the pond, the committee resolved that the same be taken down. Alderman BOLTON, in moving the adoption of the minutes, referred to the resolution re the pulling down of the" Temple." He remarked that he did not know why the building should be called a temple. (Laughter.) ,It was originally used as a store room for fruit. At the [ present time it was considered to be in a dangerous Isondition. When the building was pulled down the committee intended to decorate that portion of the park and form another walk. This would place the park under proper supervision. One of the green houses was lolling to nieces and he should like that to be pulled v down too. Councillor SMETHURST seconded. Councillor BENNETT did not see the necessity for taking the "Temple" down. He had examined it, and he thought the danger had been exaggerated, with a view of securing its demolition It would cost eta ire to pull it done than to put it into a stable condition. Alderman BOLTON: The Borough Surveyor says it is [ dangerous. . Councillor BENNETT ;aid he personally objected to its disappearance. It was characteristic of the place, and was associated with a very distinguished f who owned the park for generations- There was nothing els like this building in Warrington. (Hear, hear and laugh,er.) If the committee wanted to null something down let them pull down those two trees (Pointiag'. into the park) whieh seemed to be almost dead and were certainly extremely ugly, (Hear, bear.) The com- mittee seemed to have a perfect psssion for pulling buildings down, and only a few months ago it was sug- gested that the buildings adjoining the Town Hall on '-e - should be pulled down. Re moved that the be confirmed with the exception of the one I to. C nmeitlor BURTON : I second for the sake of doing it. (Laughter.) The amendroe.t was lust, only the proposer and seconder voting for it, and the minutes were approved. lIIARICErs COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>disappearance.</p>
        <p>fbe left I minutes be</p>
        <p>',referred</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PROPOSED COLD STORES.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED COLD STORES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the Bth July, the above com- mittee resolved that the tolls received for the use of the land in Orford-lane be paid to the Open Spaces Sub- Committee after deducting one-third to cover expenses. A proposal to convert a portion of the Market riall into a cold stores was considered, when the committee resolved that arrangements be made for inspection by the committee of the Cold Stores in Manchester and Liverpool. The annual report of the Inspector of the Weights and Measures was submitted to the Council. It stated that four traders bad been proceeded against for breaches of the Weights and Measures Act and fines Inflicted to the amount of 8s and costs. Councillor ROGERS proposed th•o confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor JOHN Roasarrs seconded. Councillor SMETHURST said the propossd cold stores would be a boon and a blessing to the town. Councillor Timer:mg asked what one-third of the tolls received f Jr the use of the land in Orford-lane would be. He was under the impression that the balance of the amount r.ceived after paving expenses was to be handed over to the Open Spaces Sub-Committee. Councillor A J JOLLEY contended that there seas no necessity for the whole of the committee to go to Man- chester and Liverpool. He asked what possible adv.n- tage could be derived from an inspection of tho stores by ordinary laymen? They had officials who could re- port or they could obtain a tenor from an expert. an moved that the minutes be confirmed with the exception of that minute. Councillor JAMES EVANS e condei and stated that it was an invasian of a previous decision of the Council, to send a whole committee as a deputation. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS said he was sorry the amendment had been proposed. The matter was one of supreme importance. Already a move had been made to fl At a private company with this very object in view, and the Corporation had a valuable piece of property in the Market Hall which at present was bringing in very little revenue. They were indebted to the Town Clerk for this suggestion. To his mind , it was a most admirable suggestion. The Market Hall, ' which was at present a white elephant, could be made much more remunerative. With regard to the wholelof , the committee going to Manchester and Liverpool be thought it very important that they should do. They wanted to know a good deal about these cold air stores TO his Mind it was More important that the committee should go there than that another committee should visit places to inspect tramcars. (Laughter.) The-com- mittee knew that these stores were very remunerative. Councillor TusNaosr : And very cold. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>1 Counaillor W R'nErrrs said they had every reason -to believe chat the scheme would be excf'elingly remunera- tive. Toe committee could visit these places at a very small expense. It would not be,as expensive as Sending tbree to Eastbourne for a week. (Laughter.) For the sake of a few psuods, he hoped the amendment would not be passed. Aliltrman MONKS said he would vote for the amend- medt, He hid a little practical experience in this direc- tion. He went to one of these cold stores in Liverpool ,ix or eight we ks ago to obtain information and sea mom working. but he learned far more from an inspec- tion of the plans and the estimates submitted by practi- cal engineers than from his inspection of the place. Be thougut it would be a waste of money for the committee to go. With iegard to it being a profitable industry, he might say as one interested in it, that if they studied the balance sheets of the limited companies engaged in it they would find it was not so profitable as Councillor Roberts .made it out to be.</p>
        <p>Counciiior TiNsruN siid !More were far too many depu• , Cations of outings. He would 110 t objETt to the Chin.- man, Deputy-Coairman and Borough Surveyor going, but did oot see way tha whole committee should go. Atderin in BouroN thought it the cirnmittes did not understand what these stores were and bow they were worked they should go and see thim. They could in addition have the work explained by experts. Councillor ROGERS, in reply to Councillor Tinnion's first remarks, said that one-third of the tolls for the Orford-lane land was less than the expenses, and the committee were dealing generously with the Open Space Committee. With regard to Councillor A J Jolley's objection to the committee going to Manchester and Liverpool be could only say that the committee were emb irking upon an undertaking they knew very little about, and before spending th- p rblic in )ney they de- sired to see whether it would be justifi A by the results. If the minute was not agreed to lie,and he hid no doubt the other members, would go at their own expense. The amendmeat was lost, and the minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>INADEQUATE DREDGING OF THE RIVER.</head>
        <p>INADEQUATE DREDGING OF THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivers Co n:nittee a letter was read from MeFers M ,nks, Hall and Co., Limited, com- plaining of the inadequate dredging of the river, when it was res Avad " that the Borough Surveyor inform the engineer of the Manchester Shin Canal Company that the plant employed in the dredging is altogether insuffi- cient, and requiring the company to put on additional dredgers." Mr R T Fairclough, the representative of the Corporation, on the Upper Miriey Norigation Com- mission, reported on the agenda of the last meeting,that the Commissioners " inspected the buoys and lightships between Liverpool and Runcorn, which are in good con- dition. Tioe charts of the channels showed little alter- ation except that the entrances to Weston and Bridge- water Locks had improved,but that t. bank was forming at the entrance of the Widnes dock. The plans of the transporter bridge were laid before us ann considered. It was resolved that a sub-committee Bbou'd be form.d to consider and safeguard the interests of the traders while the bridge was building, and 1, your representa tiv elected upon that committee, and a meeting is to be called at an early date." .</p>
        <p>The minutes were adopted on the motion of the Mayor:, seconded ...by Alderman _BOLTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The above commiitee presented the root of Pro- fessor Boyd Dawkins, which appeared in last S aturday's " Examiner "on the tow' s water supply. A letter was read from the solicitors of tae Myddleton Hall Eatate with respect to the fixing of cattle troughs, according to the plan originally agreed .soon, and it was resolved that the engineer be authorized to carry out the work. The Town Clerk reported that he had received the agreement with the Runcorn Rural District Council with respect to the prevention of pollution of Hatton Brook, and the c:mmittee resolved that a duplicate of the agreement be executed by the Corporation. A letter having been read from the Runcorn Rural District Council asking upon what terms the Corporation would continue the mains for the supply of the township of Thelwall, it was resolved that the Corporation would be prepared to do the work on a guarantee of 8 per cent. of the actual cost being given and subject to the usual conditions of agreement •, an application having been received irom the Leigh Rural District Council for the Supply of the townships of Culcheth and Kenyon with ' water, and to take over at a valuation the mains already laid by the Council within the limits of 'the supply of the Corporation, it was resolved that the pro- posal .be agreed to subject to the terms of an agree- ment to be prepared by the Town Clerk. Councillor dorm EVANS moved the adoption of the minutes, and referred to the satisfactory report of Pro- fessor Boyd Dawkins. Alderman MONKS seconded and it was agreed to. ELECTRIC LIGHTING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>--•- --- Mr Preece attended a meeting of the above committee and after discussing several matters with the committee Intimated his approaching departure for India and that he would be away for about four months. but that in his absence one or other of his partners would attend to all matters affecting this committee. It was resolved that the new boiler be insured with the Vulcan Boiler and General Insurance Company, Limited. The minutes were confirmed on the motion of Alder- man MONKS seconded by Alderman BOLTON. TRAMWAY DEVELOPMEN TS. IMPORTANT RESOLUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Tramway Committee recommended that applica- tion be made to the Board of Trade for prolongation of time for the completion of the tramways. At a meeting on the 17th July the committee inspected the buildings at the old Gasworks, which it is proposed should be utilized and made good for car sheds. They subse- quently conferred with Major Cardew, who submitted • plan showing the proposed alterations, wine; was ap- proved, subject to further details being provided. It was resolved that the London and North-Western Rail- way Company be requested to proceed with the con- struction of the rails over the level crossing at Wilders- pool, and that Major Cardew be authorizld to make arrangements with the company as to the cost of the works; that Major Cardew be authorized to arrange with Mr Ewart, the contractor, for the construction of the tramway from Warrington bridge to the level cross- ing and for • short distance on the other side of the crossing ; and that additional cross-overs be constructed so as to clear the following premises :-8 to 14, 'Touts- ford-road ; 275, Knuteford-road ; 686 to 698, Knuteford- , road ; and that the poles for the overhead equipment to be erected in Wilderspool-causeway and the widened 1 portion of Reutsford-road be placed in the centre of such roads, and that provision be made for the lighting of the roads by alternate arc and incandescent lamps to be fixed to the poles. At a meeting held on the 24th of Jaly, it was resolved that tramway No.lo be proceeded with, and tenders obtained for the construction of the works. The Fire Engine Committee having requested the Water Committee to fix hydrants on both sides of the tram rails at the cost of this committee; it was resolved " That this sommittee is not prepared to authorize such expenditure,and that a treseel be pro- vided similar to those in use in Liverpool by means of which fire hose can be carried over the tramways." The MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes and in referring to the proposed construction of tram way No. 10 stated that this tramway would run from Warrington to the end of Stafford-road near the Stockton Heath swing bridge. For obvious reasons it was necess- ary that the length from Warrington bridge over the level crossing of the London and North-Western railway should be completed without delay. 'This included a portion of tramway No. 10 and the committee thought it desirable to complete this section. Alderman MONKS seconded. Councillor Smethurst asked for further information with reference to hydrants. The MAYOR said the fixing of the hydrants as proposed by the Fire Engine Committee would cost such an enormous amount that the committee were compelled to decline to do it. In Liverpool they had movable tressels which e ,ab e the fire engines to work wit'iout interfer- ing with one of the two lines in the event of such an important circums'ence as a fire in operation. That answered the purpose quite as well. The minutes were approved. AN IMPOSITION OF £7OO PER ANNUM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>-- -- THE CANAL COMPANY AND 'THE LIGEIC RAILWAY</head>
        <p>-- -- THE CANAL COMPANY AND 'THE LIGEIC RAILWAY</p>
        <p>CO MPANY. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the csub-committee appointed "to cousider the application t candidates for the appointment of assistant surveyors and to report upon the reorganizatiot, of the Surveyor's Department," reported that they bad considered 73 applications for the appointment, and that, after Interviewing five selected candidates, the committee unanimously ~greed to recommend Mr A M Ker, of Coventry, for the appointment. It was resolved that Mr A M Ker, of Coventry, be appointed the assistant surveyor of this boroup,'h at a salary of £175 per annum, and subject to three months' notice to terminate the appointment. Toe sub-o.otnmittee report on the re- organization of the Surveyor's department will be sub- mitted at a later meeting. A letter was read from Mr J J Webster suggesting a. an aiternative to the widening of Warrington bridge by means of c intilevers the construction of a single span larch in front of the existing bridge, the cost of which I would not be rendered useless in the event of the old bridge being subsequently altered to allow a free water- way. It was res3lved that the Borough Surveyor obtain a sketch design and estimate of the cost of widening the existing bridge by means of cantilevers,and that he prepare an estimate and report on the cost of wideningl the approaches to the bridge. The Manchester Ship Cana- Company having required the promoters of the War rington and Northwich Light Railway, among other conditions, to pay an imposition of £7OO per annum for the right to use the hivhways over cbe swing bridges at ' Stockton Reach and Knutsford-road, it was resolved that the promoters be required to withdraw railway No 1 from the Order. The AlAvon in movingthe confirmation of the minutes, said the Council would be aware that they had an agreement with the promoters of the Warrington and nrthwich Light Railway to take over a certain part of that work providing the conditions under which they obtained power to do ths work were considered reason- able. When the conditions were dliclosed the commit- tee found they had agreed to pay the Manchester Ship</p>
        <p>Canal Company £350 a year for each cf the bridges at SVckt fleatn and EQutstord-r.oad,in other words that the Manchesti.r Ship Canal Company bad had the audacity to aAt, and the promotes of this scheme had been so extremely foolish as to grant toe demand for £7OO a year for pertni3sion to cress the King's highway. Tne pro, sial was as monstrous as it Iv as iji tic, and the committee could not for a moment tolerate tuch a thing. That part of the Northwich scheme had been given up. It opened the way for reason.ble arrange- ments with the people living on the other side of the swing bridge—their friends in Stockton Heath—who,he thought, would be prepared to support them when they made application to Parliament to extend their powers somewhat in that dilection. _</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON seconded, and the minutes were approved. THE OPENING OF TEIE POLICE BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR asked m.mbers of the Council to keep October 11th, the date on which the magisterial portion of the new police buildings were :o be opened,free hem other engagemen:s, as he inten ,ed to ask them as well as the magistrates t,) j)in him in a sacial function upon that date. • The Cauncil then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MECHANIC'S LUCK.</head>
        <p>A MECHANIC'S LUCK.</p>
        <p>Some people appear to be—born under an unlucky star : their only consolation is the hope that sooner or later a more favourable orb may shine on them. This happy wish has been realised, says the " Western Mail." in the ease of Mr John F Skinner.% house painter and decorator, of 36, Jesvel-street, Barry Dock. Tnough only thirty years of itrze, he has had an experience which, happily, does not fall to the lot of avery man. Mr Skinner's story, in his own words, is as follows Ells bad luck will b 3 seen at the very beginraing. He had not even health to cmsole him. " Shortly before last Christmas," he said te the , " Mail " reporter," I contracted a severe cold. Within a wsek I had entirely lost the use of both legs ; my feet and legs were benumbed and the Fkin cevered with a mass of little white blotches. My condition was so desperate that I could not keep heat in my body, nor move about without assistance. It spread from my leg up to my body, and I bad great pains in the region of the heart sod across the small of my back, with con- tinual noises in my head asid ears ['he cl,ctor gave me medicine regularly ; I was suffning, he said, from acute rheurnat.sm and kidney disorders. " But one evening I waa reading the report of a case similar to my own, which had been cured by Dr ' Williams' pink pills for pale people. My wife, having herself previou,ly heard of these pills, procured some for me, and before I had flaished the second box the pains in my back were entirely gone. I have continued up to now to take the pills,and my legs and back are as well and strong as they were before I met with this unfortunate illness. I can asaure you that a couple of months ago I never thought I should get round at all. Dr Williams' pink pills have worked wonders in me—in fact, they sr:latched me from the door of death.</p>
        <p>. . "I have not had the least twinge of rheumatism since," said Nir Skinner, in cmclueion, wherein he veri- fied oace again the fact to which so many are now waking up, namely, that Dr Williams's pink piils for Pale People (if you take care to get the genuine) are what so many doctors hive sought in vain—a true cure for rheumatism. They have also cured sciatica, paralysis, indigestion, •r nis, consumption, ladies' ailments, and St Vitus' dance : they can be had genuine (with Dr Williams' namo) from most chemist, or direct, by post, from Dr William' medicine com- pany, Holborn-viiduct, London, at two and ninepence per box, rix loxes thirteen and nine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire Association. Final—Club Championship. Leigh Railway v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>The runners-up for the county club championship met at Whitefield on Saturday afternoon to play off the final event in the presence of a moderate gathering of spectators. Mr R Williamson, treasurer of the associa- tion, acted as referee. The good majorities gained by I the Leigh team during the progress of the competition I made them the favourites. though this impression wag I to some extent weakened by the results of the first four games, which gave the opposing team a small majority'. In the second portion of the game, three of the War- ringtsn side dropped for totals little removed from half-game. The only event unsuccessful for Leigh was a very close struggle between T Bolton and W Hudspitb, in which the c-ntestants peeled at 19, ar d their trundler ultimately dropped for the odd shot. Warrington,three marks to the fore at third time, bad lost 20 when half- time was called, and when the third set of contestants crossed the green were in need of a quarter of a century to bring them level. Further and more decisive lortune then attended the winning side,whicts in the course of a couple of games nearly doubled the distance between their play and that of their opponents. The two clos- ing coutesta were both wins for Leigh,and enabled them to retire from the encounter with the heaviest fav -ur- able balance since the establishment of the competition. Scores :</p>
        <p>LEIGH RAILWAY. WARRINGTON. 7 Baines 8 J Harrison 2l G Hadspith A Boone A Hole 21 W Wood ... 16 13 Harrison 2l B Taylor lO T Hudspith 2l 3 Ashton W Hudspith J Parr 2l G Ball l2 J. Grundy G Ward 21 B Butterworth 6 H Harrison 2l li Bothwell W Topping 2l A J Jones</p>
        <p>236 Majority, 68 or Leigh.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB v. LIBERAL CLUB. This return game was played on the green of the Conservative Club oa Saturday afternoon, and resulted In a win for the Conservatives by 62 points. As the match on the Liberal green ended In the Liberals winn- ing by 128 points, the total result of the two games showed • majority of 66 points in favour of the Liberals Appended are the scores In Saturday's match : CONSERVATIVE CLUB. LIBERAL CLUB. S. D. S. D. P Riohardson ll} H Dairteth D Bawling 7 " L auklaad ll 3 Lee 113 7 1 9 H Tamer S Wood J Pennington J Genders H Booth ll J J Savage 7 8 Caldwell lo} T Looker (c.pt) .. ... 1 t G Warberten W Killington }13 B J Mitehell J Holland T Taylor (capt) llltl3 - 0 H Hibbert 9 1 T Horrabln ll J Bennion 5 /12 W Davies ll 113 B A Sanders P Bradburn W Kerfoot ql 11 J B Warren J Gittens B Bidden ll 1,3 IT 1° Freeman I. 11 2 J Thornton 4l • A Lawton</p>
        <p>T0ta15...195 IE3 195</p>
        <p>T0ta15...171 85 171</p>
        <p>318</p>
        <p>Liberal Beare k.... 256</p>
        <p>Conservative majority. 62 Liberal (home) majority l2B Conservative (home) majority</p>
        <p>Total Liberal majority _ 66</p>
        <p>Primrose League Fete at Walton. The annual fete in connection with the Dares- bury Habitation of the Primrose League took place in the grounds of Walton Hall, by permission of Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bar:, on Saturday. The weather in the forenoon gave cause for some anxiety, but fortunately kept fine during the afternoon. The grounds, stables, conservatories, piggeries and shippons were open for inspection, and most of the members took the opportunity of visiting them. Sir Gilbert's kindness in this respect was greatly appreciated. The Walton Brass Band played selec- tions of music on the lawn during Nthe afternoon. Addresses were delivered by Mr Wynne Jones (Ru)- ing Councillor) and Mr Cobbett. A hearty vote of thanks was afterwards passed to Sir ,Gilbert and Lady Greens)) for their hospitality.—Sir Gilbert Greenall briefly replied, He thanked them heartily on behalf of himself and his wife for the manner in which the members had received them that afternoon. In the name of the habitation he also thanked Mr Cobbett for his kindness in coming there to speak to them. (Ipplanse.)—The Ruling Councillor said they had with them that afternoon their former dame president, Mrs Cull Greenall—i (applause)—and he thought it would be very un- grateful on their part if they did not recognize her presence. (Applause.) The meeting then con- cluded, and an adjournment was made to a large marquee, where an excellent tea was provded.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA ha a a world-wide reputation as a I dolts, strengthening beverage, at.d a valuable nutritive fopd the "Lancet" says it represente " the standard of highest purity." Always insist on having C.ADRURY'S—eoId only in packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are o`ten substituted to he sake of extra profit. —Advt. (2)</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS, KNEECAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicose Veins, and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Legs. Sprains ; they are porous, light in texture. Nn d inexpensive, and are drawn ol like an ordinary atocaing.—Prices 4a 6d, 7s 6d, and 109 6d each.—Postages free.—WHITE AND WRIGHT Surgical Instrument and TrussMakers,,93. Renshaw-street, top of Bold-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HEALTH OF NEWTON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF NEWTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Medical Officer of Health's Report.</p>
        <p>IDr. Watkins, medical officer or health for the Newton in-Makerfielu Ur ban Dietrict Couecl, in his report for the quarter ended 30 h June, as that in his last report he hoped and expected that by the 30,h June they would have B!itii,geut, facts from the recent census CO produce a more correctly approximate estimate of the population. He now sutj iced some facts and estimates i—l'opu'atiou enumerated at Census ot 1901, 16,699,pepulatem eo imatec at date of 1891 Ceasus,based I on the rate ot increase between 1881 and 1891, 15,634. excess and enumerated over estimated population 1,065. , Estimates or population based on the assumption that 1 the same rate ot iacrease will prevail uatil the Census of 1911, as that found to exist between the Ce senses of 1891 aid 1901, at middle of 1901, 16,808, at date of Census (eud of Ist quar.er) of 1911, 21,682. There were registred during the quarter ended the 30:h June, 1901:—BirthA 142, deaths 51, excess of births 91. 'floe butt, rate was 33 7, and tee death rate 121, per tuousand or the population (16,808), estimated to the middle of 1901. Tue infantile death rate was 1506 per cent. The deaths under five years of age were 45'9 per cent of the tot I numb ne. The deaths from the in the township, and scarletthefevpeoroullaintiothne. principal zytnelle diseases (wueopmg cough 1, and en pital at Warrington), we:e equal to an Itesorliactfieovnear annual rate tot 009 per teousarel of The cases et infectious sickness (76) notified to me— scarlatina 67, dipetheria arid membranous croup 5, en. teric fever 1, and erysipelas 3, total 76—were eridal to an annual rate of 18.08 p.r thousand of the population. Tweatt-five of the c.ees were rem ived to the Isolation hospital, of whom one patient died. Scarlet fever was preeenc in epidemic form throughout the quarter, and its increased 'mei:P.:deuce ass to be explained by (a) insuffieient accommodation in the isolation hospital, amounting at times to ansolete want of any accommod- ation for our patient, ; (b) overcrowding in tee many iastances in private dwellings even ; (3) want of disia- fecting plant where clothing, bedding, and other material can be efficiently treated. Tire toregoing three cacses, accentnateu by an abnormally dry season, with a correspo .ding decrease in the water supply—he'll defective i quantity and irregular in its distribution to , the higher parts of the district—had combined to pro- duce the unhappy circumstances existing. It was pro- vidential that scarlet fever was as a rule anon-recurrent disease, or the cases might have been many times multiplied. The type of the ep.demic had been a mild one, turtunately, and it was some consolation to know that a large per centage of the children, at a most sta. ceptible age, had been rendered practically iMMatt. from the dis 'see threugh having had 'lc attack. Other wise the fatality would, doubtless, have been much greater, From the foregoieg the Couecil would be pre pared to learn that it was not wise to defer any lorige the provision of an isolation hopital within the distrio and an efficient disinfector for public use. The absolute necessity fortan improved water supply—both in (pan. tity and constancy, had been forced upon the Council, and heilknew it was the subject of anxious considers tion and care. Extended sewerage and efficient tr 'at meet of the sewage between Mill-lane and the Vulcan was needed. Milliogford brook bad been the subject of complaint many timee this summer. The report was adopted at Monday's Council meeting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>rIUNCORN BOARD- OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>rIUNCORN BOARD- OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this body was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday, Mr J Stokes pre- sided and there were also preseoti—Messrs S Taylor. T Wood, T Lea, J Dougtle. the Rural District Councillors, with Mr Geo. F Ashton (c:erk), and Mr J Cannata (master).</p>
        <p>TELETVNIC COMMUNICATIONS. The District Manager and the National Telephone Company wrote with respect to the telephonic commu... nication for the house stating that it was a most diffi cult thing for him to definitely say upon what terms a separate connection could be made from Dutton Work- house to the Frodsham Exchange. Approximately, however, he should say that for a separate line from Dutton Wurkhouse to the Frodsham Exchange. the cost would be about £5O per annum, and before going definitely into the question he should be glad to know if the Committee would entertain this sum. The Clerk stated on receipt of this letter he wrote to the General Post Office asking if they could connect the Workhouse with their system. The result was that the Post Office authorities had sent down an engineer to see the position of the House, and hal intimated they would send in a report shortly. The engineer seemed to consider it wouldlbe a very cheap matter as the Post Office wires passed the House. (Hear, hear.) -</p>
        <p>AN AWARD. Mr John Lithiby, assistant secretary of the Local Goverment Board wrote acknowledging the receipt of a lette: of 'the 25% ult, and in reference to the request which it contained, informing the Guardians that the reports oi the Local GJvernmeat Board's inspectors on vaccination were made only for the Board's information; but they might state that each of the public vaccinator; in the Runcsrn Union was, after the last inspection of public vaccination in February last, recommended for- an award under section 5, of the Vaccination Act, 187.</p>
        <p>THE CO JE DIFFICULTY. The Clerk intimated he had renewed but one applies. tion in response to the advertisements for aco dr. The candidate was Miss Louis Clayton, of Knightsbridge, Loncinn.</p>
        <p>The Master suggested that the person should be en. gaged kw a month on trial. This was aereed to.</p>
        <p>THE PAINTING CONTRACT. The Clerk reported 6 tenders were received for paint. log required, and the tender of Mr J Paypter to do the work for £9 was ultimately accepted. THE FIRE ESCAPES, Mr Williams reported that the special Committee appointed to consider .the suggestions of Mr Dansey with regard to fire escapes met that morning,and arrived 'at the conclusion that it would be best to. ask Messrs Merryweather, who were accustomed to that class of business to send an engineer down to the House, and to submit a detaiied report as to the probable coat of the practicability of the suggestions which had been made by Mr Dansey. TOO committee pretty much agreed with Mr Dansey, for there was very little difference between what they (the committee) recommended and what Mr Dansey suggested. At the same time they could not give any idea of the cost or of the probability of those things being worked out P atisf aetori 17 It was therefore suggested that when he (Mr Williams) went to Manchester in a week or two he should call upon Messrs Merryweather with regard to the matter. The committee therefore asked that the matter lie over until he paid th v:sit alluded to, and they had the enginerr's report. The Chairmao thought th qu ,stion of fire escapee &amp;td came up a few yrars ago, and he a.ked if there was any record of what was (I on -on that occasion. He believed some outside staircases were then recommeaded in one or two instances _</p>
        <p>Mr Williams remarked that the suggestions then made were much more numerous than those at the present time. It was thouzht they would not be able to gain much inf irmation by visiting Warrington, for what had' been c.rried out there did not at all compare with what was required at Dutton. Mr Pilling pointed out the class of work required was outside Messrs Merryweather's business. Mr Williams said his information was quite at varianoe with the remarks of Mr Pilling. It was decided that Mr Williams shtfiald visit Wrists Merryweather and also Warrington, make the necessary Inquiries, and report.</p>
        <p>TREATS FOR THE INMATES. The Muse Committee reported the receipt of iilus- trated p4perS for the use of the inmates from biro Linaker, Rrodshain ; and that lAdy Broke, 01 Norton Priory, had very kindly entertained the children at her residence.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr Moseley Williams, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded blth ladies.</p>
