        <p>AFTER the exaltation The Government's Defeat. of victory the humiliation</p>
        <p>_ of defeat comes with added bitterness, and Ministers who were glory- ing in the success of their party at Blenheim on Saturday are to-day bewailing the first defeat of the session. At the conclusion of a remarkable eulogy on the Colonial policy of the Government Mr BALFOUR, on Saturday, expressed his confident belief that "a similar courage and a similar per- sistence" would be shown in dealing with home affairs. He has not had to wait long for an answer. The courage of the Government frequently verges on rashness, and their persistence is apt to be in the wrong direction. It was a combination of these qualities in the wrong place which led to their being defeated on Monday night by a ma- jority of twenty-two. The catastrophe occurred on the discussion of the Factory Bill, which came before the House for consideration in its amended form after the dinner hour. The Government, although pressed in Committee on the subject of a half-holiday on Saturdays after twelve o'clock for the textile industries, which employ large num- bers of women and children, declined to commit themselves to the proposal; and although warned that the representatives of the great Lancashire and Yorkshire manufacturing constituencies, both Liberal and Tory, had united in favour of the half- holiday, Mr RITCHIE persisted in opposing the twelve o'clock clause with all the weight the Gov- ernment could bring to bear against it. He had ample warning during the debate on the clause, which included a sensible appeal from such a repre- sentative Unionist as Sir WILLIAM HOIILDSWORTH nevertheless he courted defeat with a hardihood that was worthy of a better cause. Mr BALFOUR may prate glibly enough of courage and persistence, but unless these admirable qualities are used in the service of a clear judgment and a wise and benevo- lent desire to serve the public interests they will only lead to disaster. Well will it be for the country if the calamity which must sooner or later overtake this vaunting party of physical force and class selfishness does not as well involve the nation and the Empire. We have already had an example of what courage and persistence, from the Tory point of view, can achieve in the matter of national education, and no doubt the Leader of the House had in view the campaign which must shortly be entered upon in the country for the redemption of education from the clerical power when he called for " the same persistence in home affairs." The untoward fate which has overtaken the Govern- ment on the Factory Bill, however, may act as a warning that unless the true interests of the people are considered as the main object in social legislation the Unionist party, however strong it may appear, will be found to be reckoning without its host. There is a tendency in the possession of power to develop overweening pride ---the pride that goeth before a fall. Indications have been painfully manifest that this danger ous influence is already at work in Government circles in the disdainful contempt with which all interests are treated which do not happen to contribute to the upholding of the Government position. It so</p>
        <p>happens that the interest of the multitude is to be numbered among those interests to which the present Cabinet give scant courtesy. Oa° blind- ing passion has hidden the truth from the eyes of • of the people, but as the cause of the momentary aberration passes away, and the Government be- comes more reckless, the eyes of the multitude will be opened, and then the day of reckoning for the present perverters of power will have arrived.</p>
        <p>IT is evident the inhabi- The Maltese Trouble. tants of Malta are not going to sit still under the out. rageous injustice that has been perpetrated upon them in the name of the British Government. The committee who are at the head of the agitation have issued an Appeal, in which they say: "We are a little unarmed people, but we desire it to be known that we have decided to die rather than to live as slaves." The imposing of the taxes levied by Order in Council has given the necessary ire' petus to the popular movement, and a mass meet- ing, at which, it is said, there were from twelve to fifteen thousand people present, has been V to protest against the imposition. Feeling is bitter, and indignation and discontent are spri among the people. The taxes, though they for. the immediate point of opposition, and one which will take some trouble to overcome, are nt, in themselves objectionable. It is the deprivation of their liberty which has exasperated the people. The Maltese have always supposed that in the right of the Governing Council—in which thei have some representation—to levy taxes had a guarantee against Imperial oppression ; but now that the right has been rudeli Snatched from them they are brought face to face with a deadlock which is rendering there obstinate and desperate. The taxes were those ' generally levied in the usual way, which,the Coun' cil refused to levy as a protest against the polieY that was being thrust upon the island of making the people choose between English and Italian in the education of their children. The people wistt to have both languages taught, and there is Ile reason why they should not have them. If English is the official language and necessary es such, Italian is no less the language of commerce and equally indispensable. It is surely a niggarell9 policy that denies a boon like this to people whore it is desired to make loyal subjects of the Empire As the Secdo, an Italian paper, remarks, in core' meriting on the Appeal, as well might Plc CHAMBERLAIN change the language of Gibrali where they speak Spanish, or the language Cyprus, where they speak Greek. It is foreign to the genius of British policy to deprive a subject race of any of its educational privileges' the policy has all along been one of stimulg' ing education, and it has never been a matter of difficulty to engraft on the educational tree of knowledge an acquaintance with Vie English language sufficient for all purposes. If the Government are departing from this tins°. honoured policy abroad, as well as at home, thele will be held responsible for the consequences. Maltese have hitherto been a loyal, peaceably disii posed and law-abiding people. It is no aesto of a nationalist rebellion, or disaffection even' an e if nationalism is forced into rebellion, as thevvr seems every indication of its being, we shall Ito.° qid whom to blame. Information comes of anul:rds toes being placarded all over Valetta,large e„r°.•,, making hostile demonstrations, and the JLOv Jack being torn down. It will be useless to the people for these outrages, to which they hal been provoked by a policy of stupid, obsti•asto blundering on the part of the Imperial Gov't.% ment. It has been kent a secret until within ni fortnight of Parliament rising for the vacatio But, crowded as the business of the House Commons is, it will be strange if time is not fount: before the House rises to call attention to a ette of things which is a blot on the fair name 4of British Empire, as the home of ,the brave and free the seat of justice and of liberty.</p>
        <p>they</p>
        <p>7 1 I f Itart , Of tag!,</p>
        <p>T</p>
        <p>There are matters for reflection, as well as I°Cr congratulation, in the latest returns of the TAW% Association, whose members are now engage";„ the Co-operative Festival at the Crystal Paia.'t; This association, it may be well to explain, esls4 to promote the principle of co-partnershiP,.; labour in industry, a system which is to be tingu.ished. from that commoner form of produ", ton in which the workman gives his labour t° 5 co-operative society on precisely the same ter°l•, upon which he would be engaged by the bestyr.lo vate employers. Such workmen sometimes, it true, receive a yearly or quarterly bonus, or allowed In some form or other to receive a P°t-g tion of the profits, but these arrangeal°4„:oo are equally made in co-operative factorlie and 137 those ordinary firms by-whom workppoge, are kindly and generously treated. The socie,tild in which the partnership principle is strict ic carried out have decreased by one in the pastYle which would suggest some slight check in movement, though it appears, on the other 11500 that the profit made in one hundred and °ta societies now at work increased from £138,614,, £145,641. The association attributes the decre'ot to the high rate of wages and good emploP2'lor, of last year, making workmen less inclineu,te char go, while at the same time political 0010, ment drew their minds from reform. If, hoviPhila good wages kave such an effect, the result oi trade would probably be worse, for the workflo though willing enough to share in the Pr° be which his labour helps to create, would iiloss anxious to be saddled with his share in th° which might ensue in any particular year.</p>
        <p>Perhaps one of the saddest features of the lie is the gradual decline In sickness away frora too field of action of some of its best known agto .They have passed from the bracing, though I)her influences of the campaign home to the ISl°tdoir Country, there to sink slowly away in the 05,00 o. fan infirmary or a nursing home. Such a "tlie is that of the Rev. Father Timonoy, chaplain t°,4lig Australian bushrangers, of whom an inteo obituary appears in the "Times." An Iriabylpel with a certain restrained love of adventure tile excitement, Father Tirnoney migrated frorae land of Erin some fifteen years ago, and boil° ol Sydney a Diocesan Inspector of Schools. Tilrolo ardour was quickened in him when the out, and he volunteered for the front—of coal'o' a ministerial capacity. On the veldt he„,,o to tracted an affection of the throat and he en; re' St. Thomas's Hospital to die. 'The case, e marked upon because Father Timoney had '„, are him such a distinguished career. But ther,;,loo, hundreds of young fellows who have corn" orie Invalided, who have apparently recova.r° 'ate° then have fallen back into sickness agale--Thel suffering from the after-effects of enteri.c• ohlie are so distributed that they do not attention, as they would do if grouped t°^os/ Some of them are provided for; a groat oleo are, not, and it is especially hard when volun.t°°,etto` t 5 stricken in this way. Our patriotism re god shown in helping such people than in loud of approval of military pageants. exciteletlior</p>
        <p>'Wel' the . 010</p>
        <p>To adequately gauge the growth of rington and District Agricultural Some"Ft ifis; needs to recall its small beginnings whpil2ociet•l' known as the Warrington Mare and Foal s coo; Then the entries were few and the show slat. befr fined to horses, which i,—</p>
