      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>NIATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>NIATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>- - - - Quite a" cheery tone " is said to have pervaded _the meetinv of the Taff Vale Railway Company on xuesday evening, when, naturally, reference was , ra.ade to the proceedings recently taken by the directors against the Amalgamated Society of Rail- Way servants, and the Cnairman expressed the ?pinion that the importance of the judgment of the •UouQe of Lords to large employers of labour C,Ould not well be exaggerated. B 3 this as it may, 1 both the decision in this case and in that of Quim v lia.tham,likewise disposed of in the House ot lords, val shortly occupy the attention of the Trades Union Congress at Swansea and afford plenty of Mulum for debate. In regard to the first-named !Ilse, counsel's opinion taken by trade unionists 14ntirms the view previously entertained as to te trupertant nature of the judgment and the effect it Will have of restricting the freedom of operation Whith unionists have hitherto enjoyed; and it is regarded as going further inasmuch as it indicates the added powers which the ruling will place with- In the reach of the working man by rendering em- royers liable for damages whenever attempts shall Made to injure Trades Unions. It is held to be a Poor rule that will not work in more than one way, !rid a particularly poor rule of law that cannot be !c) manipulated. This is how the members of the trades unions are looking at the matter at present.</p>
        <p>-- Mr Walter Long, M.P., had to suffer much abusel ra cclunt of his now historical Muzzling Order, ae may be fairly excused if he looks back with I Pride upon what has bten its effect. According to rine ooard of Agriculture's annual report, just out, Q.— Case of rabies has been confirmed in England or Qcotland during the whole of the year 1900. It is added with manifest confidence that in these two Countries the disease which had been present there for centuries, has been entirely stamped out, or eradicated. Isolated outbreaks have been reported from South Wales, but these are put down to the Chance importation of dogs from other countries. While 'j country is able to show an almost clean ill of health so far as rabies are concerned, France Still records some two or three thousand cases a Year. In Belgium, also, the disease is apparently Increasing. In Italy nearly five times as many CalieS were declared in 1900 as in 1899; while in India and America the terrible scourge is exten- 2i2velY prevalent. It is, indeed, the danger of the disease being imported from abroad that the Board Of Agriculture has now to occupy itself in guarding ,aßainst. To those in England who consider that Ole life of a human being is of more value than the lives of the whole of the canine species, the re- elilts must be very gratifying.</p>
        <p>The Truck Act has touched some of our rural toPulatiou in one of their most tender places. [ •"trraers nowadays, like their fathers and great Rrandfathers before them, sent out a well-filled Cider jar to the fields at harvest time with the men !lad the team; for the labourer is still held worthy 44 his cups, and, as a rule, works with a bad grace 'Without them. He is generally content to take a tkillinz, or two less each week from the employer Who provides this or similar refreshment than he Would from the one who fails to make the same Rrateful provision. In some parts of the country Us Act came as a pick-axe, stirring up the ground of ancient tradition and striking at the very roots of eA•thlished custom. It has been so in Worces- tershire, where it has led to a revolt among the taorEue!s on whom the county authorities appear - ImPressing their powers with a very unspar- ing nand. The Home Secretary's aid has actually been invoked in the effort to get rid of th 9 obnoxi- ous clause, so far as that county is concerned, and meanwhile a fund is being raised for the defence of , offenders who shall be brought before the courts. t' It may be one way of what is known as metaphori- • cagy taking the bull by the horns, but the farmers an lab luring men in this case will soon learn that It What is law in one county must be law for another --otherwise endless difficulties would arise—and that the taek they have undertaken is greater than ; they had any previous conception of. ! With the holiday mood still upon them, as it always is in August, the public continue to mani- Zest a lively interest in the question of when that ' Promised additional Bank Holiday is to come in. It Roes without saying that a national holiday to commemorate the unique reign of Victoria the good called for, and the 24th of May has been favour- ably mentioned in a previous letter. But there are Very many Persons now urging that the most suitable oPportunity presents itself somewhere midway be- tween the August bank holiday and that immedi- ately following Christmas-day. There are already four such holidays, rather unevenly distributed,and Partlj so by the movable character of the religious , ,testivals to which they are appended, Easter and Whitsuntide particularly. Between Easter Monday and Whit Monday of the present year there was,of course, a laps, of seven weeks, and between Whit lionday and August sth ten weeks. But before the next general holiday of the 26... h of December, there will have been an interval of more than twenty weeks; and it is being asked why this should be thus,if the people are to have yet another national holiday at some period of the year or other? To divide the time would bring us to about the middle of October, often an admittedly enjoy- able period of the year,and one offering a decidedly better prospect for outdoor recreation, as a rule, than any Easter Monday fixture. In these later years of " Michaelmas summers," October has often • Droved to be a very pleasant month, especially so far as the country districts are concerned,with their rural scenery. But when Parliament reassembles after its own long holiday people may expect to know more upon the subject than they do now.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>--- The civil judicial statistics, just published, have In especial interest for people in the provinces. , -they show, for one thing, what has long been I Urged even by high legal authorities, that there is aregrettable waste of power in connection with tie assize system. The Bar Council reported some tirne since that the business on circuit was carried on at a " gteat waste of judicial time and energy, and at an extravagant cost to the country." In two or three towns on the two Welsh circuits there was •Oe civil business. Taking twenty-five towns on eircuq, the number of civil cases entered and tried Ivas five,or under. County courts continue to do use- -121 work, and are becoming more important every Year. They afford a ready means of recovering ,debts, and during the last few years the legislature cas • , evinced its confidence in the judgment and ;Nal ability of the county court judges by adding r the number and nature of the cases with which ukeY are competent to deal. What seems to be needed in place of the antiquated assize system and ne out of-date quarter sessions, are county or dis- ‘,Flct criminal courts, after the manner of London's ',entral Criminal Court.</p>
        <p>Other Unions please note! During a diSCUSsion 14 the Bristol Board of Guardians on the expenili- Ilire of the board, the Clerk said that out of every vo.lind 16i 71d was spent on the poor, 3s 4td on ad- ;Thnistration,and he:would challenge anyone to find patter results that this in any Union of England ftnd Wales, One must pause here for the reply.</p>
        <p>-- Not long since many Boards of Guardians were 4,linoost continually having their respective vaccina- "Qn officers in" hot water" on the subject of the trosecution of defaulters. A grandmotherly Govern- uten t had pity on both parties, and caused an Order the Local Government Board to be issued con- oirr„ing powr on their officers to act independently are the Guardians, Bu • still many of the Guardians the not happy, and just now a memorial is going v0,,-nund of the unions from the Poplar Board tirch has for its object the revocation of such oth Sr. Some boards, on the ground of consistency er, because the plan has not yet had a fair trial " Yet others because they generally disapprove</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>of so many memorials emanating from particular boards, refuse to sign, but, on the other hand,many have signed the Poplar manifesto. Lovers of peace at guardians'meetings,especially in relation to this question, will prefer that Poplar shall not have its way in dictating to provincial boards of guardians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>"VMIRACTILOIIS ESCAPE FROM DEATH. George McNorton, of Ashridge-street. Runcorn, had a most miraculous escape from death by drown- ing on Wednesday. As he was crossing the Lock on the Bridgewater Canal on his way to his work at the Castner Kellner Alkali Works, his dinner basket caught the winch handle. Tills caused him to fall on to the sill, about 20 feet below, and to re- bound into the water: Fortunately help was at hand and he was promptly rescued. At the result of the fall he has sustained severe injuries to his left ankle.</p>
        <p>NELSON-STREET ANNIVERSARY. The Rev J A B Malvern, a former minister of the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit, was the preacher in connection with the above anniversary, which was held in the Camden Chapel (through the kindness of the friends of that place of worship) on Sunday. He preached to large congregations both morning and evening, and addressed a large gathering of Sunday school scholars and parents in the afternoon assembled under the chairmanship of Mr F J Norman. The singing of the children and choir was a special feature of the anniversary. Collections amounting to .£23 were raised aid of in the Nelson- street Sunday school. FIRE AT WESTON POINT SALT WORKS. About 10.30 on Saturday night an alarm of fire was raised in Weston Point, and later it was dis- covered to have broken out in a wooden erection in the vicinity of the furnaces at the Salt Works. The time keeper, Mr Bisbrown with the assistance of Police Constable Rowlands and others soon extinguished the fire by the liberal use of brine,and thanks to their prompt action, averted what might have been a gr let catastrophe. as there was a shed filled with oil in close proximity to the outbreak. The fire originated through a defect in the furnace brickwork allowing the flames of the furnace to belch forth and catch the woodwork. The damage is expected to be under £2O.</p>
        <p>_ WESTON SONS OF TEMPERANCE, The Nil Desperandum Division of the Sons of Temperance, Weston, held a demonstration on Saturday. About 130 members of the society met at the clubroom, and beaded by the Weston Wes- leyan Brass Band, under the conductorship of Mr John Smith, tney made a circuit of the village. Messrs S Dodd and J Brown marshalled the proces- sion, which was accompanied by the beautiful banner of the order. The halting places en route were Sweetman's Cottage, the village green, Heath- road, Mr Thomas Ford's, Mr James Tudor's, and Mrs W Bankes'. The procession then returned to the clubroom. At 4-30 tea was provided free for the juveniles, and for the adults at a moderate charge, on a field kindly lent by Mrs Hulse. Owing to the rain the sports had to be postponed. The following members of the Runcorn branch were present:—Messrs T Cooke, D Brown, J Johnson, T Holt, and W Cooke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES. NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT]. The Runcorn Urban District Council are said to have decided on the details of the Cottage Hospital scheme, the plans for which are being completed by Mr J Wilding, their surveyor. It is their intention however, of calling a public meeting and letting the public into their confidence. The Chairman is anxious that the work should be commenced dur- ing his year of office, and as he has always taken a deep interest in the work, I only hope his wish will be gratified; The funds in hand amount to nearly 1£682, and the Runcorn Cycle Club deserve the I credit for .£350 as the results of their efforts.</p>
        <p>-- The Runcorn footballers have been out practiQ- ing this last week end, and none of them appear any the worse for wear. Of course, in a prelimin- ary canter it is difficult to decide true form, but as far as the practices indicate there is nothing to be ashamed of. There has been an important meeting regarding the prices of admission also, but as aa:. appears in another column, it only remains for me to say I approve of the action taken. Give Runcorn folk first class football and they will support it ; but even the kids turn up their noses at "toffee matches."</p>
        <p>The annual trip of the Runcorn and Widnes Co- operative Society will take place en Ronday the 19th lust, the destination being Blackpool. The six men injured through the boiler accident at Widnes, on Wednesday by the explosion of a Copper furnace at Messrs Mcliechhie's Works, Dit- ton-road, are now said to be progressing favour- ably. t=mmielimma STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE BAPTIST CHAPEV—Special IrrVices were held here on Sunday last, in connection with the choir fund. In the morning the pastor, Rev J 8 Hughes preached a sermon upon the Influence of Music in the formation of character, the elevation of the mind, and the soothing of the spirit, taking as his text I. Samuel xvi. 23 —David playing the harp before Saul,and driv- ing the evil spirit from him, thereby. In the afternoon there was a fairly good attendance to bear a Service of Song,entitled "Eva " which was rendered by the choir. Mr Percy Randall M.1.C.51. under whose training the choir is conducted. Mr Willie Hellas officiated at the organ, and the reader of the pathetic story was the pastor Rev J s Hughes. Tlie whole service was exceed- ingly good and impressive. The choir which contains some sweet young voicest was well balanced and kept in good tune and tone •by Mr Randall. Mr Hellas acquitted himself faultlessly at the organ, whilst the effective' reading of the pathetic story combined to make this melodious service of song one of the most charming and successful ever rendered in Hill Cliffs Chapel. Before the collection was taken Miss Hellas sang very sweetly and appropriately "A dream of Paradise," by Hamilton Gray. The service of song was repeated again in the evening to a large and apprecia- tive audience, the chapel being well filled. The Pastor, .Rev J S Hughes, preached a remarkably short sermon in the interval on "In my Father's House," John xlv. 2nd verse, a topic in perfect harmony with the ser- vice of song. Mr George Moss conducted, and Mr Willie Hellas again officiated at the organ,and the service was quite as successfully rendered as in the afternoon. Dur- ing the day the following veluntaries were excellently rendered on the organ by Mr Willie Bailee " Manheirn" Gorman l Chorale; " Awake put on strength " by Dr Calcott ; " The Heavens are telling," by Haydn ; "Queen of Angels" by M PiCCOIOMine ; "I know that my Redeemer livetb," by Handel ; " Vital Spark," by Harwood; " Before Jehovah's Awful Throne," by Madan. And at the close of the evening service, the " Dead March " was most impressively played, the whole audience standing in response to the pastor's request, as a tribute to the memory of the late Empress Frederick. The collection for the day were taken by members of the ihoir and amounted to nearly Lfl.</p>
        <p>,ttev</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIPA Y AUGUST 16 1901. RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. I THE THELWALL NUISANCE. The Expert's Opinion Rejected. Action Decided On.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIPA Y AUGUST 16 1901. RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. I THE THELWALL NUISANCE. The Expert's Opinion Rejected. Action Decided On.</p>
        <p>Prices of Admission.</p>
        <p>- - Is. special general meeting of the above club was held at Mr A Riley's, Bridge-street, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening, to consider the advisability of increasing the members' subscription. Mr S Taylor presided, and was supported by Messrs Walter Jones (chairman of the Committee), and Alfred Abel (assistant secretary). The Chairman observed that as they had beard that meeting was convened for the purpose of con- sidering the advisability of increasing the members subscription. The club was naw joining the Northern League, which would mean a very con- siderable increase in their expenses. They would have to journey to various parts of Yorkshire which would necessitate them leaving early in the morning &amp; i in many instances, and involve a great in- ease n travelling expenses. But as they all knew by affiliating themselves with the Northern League they would have visits from the best of the clubs in the to Counties,Lancashire and York- shire and this would mean as far as the spectators and members were concerned a very great treat in the coming seasori. They could not have luxuries without paying for them, and he felt quite sure they would experience no difficulty in disposing of the business that night with satisfactory results. He hoped a unanimous vote would be given in support of the recommendation of the com- mittee. All Runcorn people who took an Interest in football had appreciated their players abilities in the past. (Hear, hear.) He thought he was quite safe in saying that they wero second to none,and therefore the least they could do as mem- bers and spectators was to show their appreciation of their players in a practical manner. He thought everyone would appreciate the move which the 'committee were now making. He felt sure that the forthcoming season would provide them with a grand treat. He asked Mr Jones to state the alter- ations it was proposed to make. Mr Jones explained that there was a rule in the Northern League which said the minimum price of admission to the field should be sixpence, that was for their spectators. Seeing that this was an ad- vance on what they had been previously charging, the committee thought it would not be a fair thing that these people should bear all the burden. They thought as members of the committee that it was their duty to bring the matter be- fore the members and submit another scale of charges and ascertain if it was their wish that such should be adopted The particular rule at present read :—" The annual subscription shall be playing members, 3s 6d ; non-playing members, ss. Non-playing members shall be required to pay an entrance fee of 2s 61. Season tickets (not transfer- able), to admit to the unreaerved portion of the ground to witness all club fixtures, shall be issued, adults, 3s Gd ; boys under 15 years, 2s. In substitu- tion of that was proposed the following :—The annual subscription shall be—grand stand, 12s 6d (incluJing admittance to the ground) ; reserved side, 7a 6d. Season tickets (not transferable to admit to the unreserved portion of the ground to witness all fixtures with the exception of the Northern Union Cup Ties, shall be issued at 5s each. boys (under 15 years of age), 2s 61 ; stand, 2s 6d extra. Mr H Wright seconded, remarking that he endorsed all that Mr Jones had said with regard to the rule re- quiring the spectators to pay at their matches 61 as the admission fee. air J F Moulton, said the recommendation of the committee was not received quite unanimously in the committee meeting. Be regretted to say that in consequi.nce of the players having to be met on the night when the matter was discussed, the resolution was not as fully considered as they should like it to have been. Ile did not wish to introduce any discordant note. He quite agreed with the chairman that the resolution should be passed unanimously. He should, however, like the matter to be discussed in all its hearings and he would propose a small amend- ment which he believed wnuld be for the benefit of the club. To his mind the admission to the stand had always been too high, and 53 6J extra was a great increase. Previously 55 entitled the members to all the privileges of the club as well as to admission on the reserved portion of the ground but if anyone wished to go on the stand they would have to pay 55 6J extra. Personally he should like to see the stand price remain- ing at its present figure, for the reason that he thought if, the price was advanced to 12s 6d the stand members would be considerably reduced. If it remained as it was, h 3 contended that many members would be agreeable to pay the extra 3s which would entitle them to admission to the stand and they must bear in mind that since the stand has been enlarged the reserved side spectators could obtain a better view of the field of play than hitherto. If the stand price remained at 103 6d he argued that they would have a larger number of stand members,'and consequently the funds of the club would be considerably augmented•Mr Moulton then mentioned the question of the vice-presidents,observing that he did not know whether they would succeed In getting the 12s 6J from all those gentlemen. If they paid 10s, 6d and refused to pay the other 2s, he did not know whether the treasurer would be entitled to receive the half guinea, and keep their names on the list as before. His amendment was that the annual subscription to admit to the grand stand should be 103 6d. Mr A. Lightburn who seconded said he did so because ' during the past year the stand receipts had gone down, and he thought if the price was increased they would go down further still,and the,club would!lose some of its , members. I The Chairman said he could not agree with Mr Moul- ton and for this reason : He heard they had 13 of these Northern League matches in Runcorn, and as they were all aware whenever a good team had visited the town to meet that home team they had always brought a large I - contingent of people with them. His experience was, times without number, that when they had good matches with first class teams, strangers had not been able to get on the stand. Therefore they must take that view. (Hear, hear). Out of 17 or 18 matches they had 13 league engagements at home, and he hoped and believed they would bring large numbers of people with them. They mast not lose sight of their financial position. He feared that if they carried the amendment it would not support their financially to the extent Mr Moulton had pointed out it might do. The twopence increase was the greatest hardship to the topside, and he felt sure they at the lower side had no need to complain of that 12s 61 including the stand. (Hear, hear). He gave his opinion with due respect to Mr Moulton. Mr Donellan spoke for the amendment. He thought that an ordinary individual with not a very long purse woald consider twice before he took a stand ticket at 12s 63 when he could get a season ticket without the stand for Ts 8d He was positively convinced that the stand members would be reduced if the proposal was carried. Mr Hancock asked if it was proposed to raise the admission to non members.He thought himself sixpence Was a very low figure, and he did not suppose there was another first class club where they paid that price to get on:the stand, in fact he knew some places where is ffd was eharged, and the stand was no better than there. The Chairman explained that !that had not been before the meeting. Mr Jon°. remarked that that was a matter for the lommittee to settle afterwards. On the amendment being put, it was lost, four voting its favour. The resolution was afterwards carried. On the motion of Mr Dudley seconded by Mr Moulton a vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman for pre- sining.</p>
        <p>COMI</p>
        <p>A serious fire broke out early on (Monday morning at Bishop's Court, Manchester, the residence of the Bishop of Manchester, who is at present in Scotland. When the Salford Brigade reached the house the portion of the building occupied by the servants was well alight, and exciting scenes were witnessed. Four maid-servants, whose escapes bad been cut off by the flames, were res- cued by means of a ladder from a window, The servants' quarters and his lordship's bedroomi were burnt out. The dining-room and drawing-room also suffered. The library fortunately escaped injury. The Committee of the Dissenting Deputies have passed a resolution expressing their disapprobation of the manner in v bleb the second Government Education Bill has been hurried through the House of Commons, and declaring that they will avail themselves of every opportunity which may occur to reverse the policy initiated by the Bill.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY,</p>
        <p>At the Meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council on Tuesday, the Clerk read the following letters : Cuerden Hall, Thelwall, Warrington, August 6th. 1901. To Geo. P. Ashton, Req., Runcorn. Dear Sir.—.l note and thank you for your letter of the 31st ult., enclosing me a copy of Or Penny's report on the tannery nuisance, which is described on page 9 as due to " a septic ferment." which according to Dr Ballard's opinion, therein quoted, "is unquestionably dangerous and indeed a fruitful source of much fatal disease." In the local press reports of your Coun- cil's proceedings at last week's meeting, I observe a letter from Messrs Long and Son, dated 16th ult., dealing with Dr Penny's report, in which the following paragraph occurs : " We hope to furnish you shortly with a report from the highest authority we have been able to obtain respecting the charges made against us. and will leave your Board to draw their own conclusions." In respect to this proposal I respectfully point out :—(1) That the Local Government Board asked your Council for specific information on and with respect to the nuisance in question. (2) That your Council decided to call in an expert to make full enquiries and to report On the matter, in order that the Local Government Board might have the necessary information. (3). That Dr Penny of Aldershot, was the expert decided upon, he being a stranger, a totally independent person, and one who would, therefore, not be biased in favour of either Messrs Long and Son or myself. (4). That Dr. Penny's report, as now submitted, after several visits, personal examinations, and analysis by competent and professional experts, proves what I have all along contended viz, that a serious public nuisance exists at the Tanuery here. (5). That if Messrs. Long and Son, as they suggest, intend to furnish a report on Dr. Penny's report, and you have instructed your Medical Officer also to report on the same rep@rt, I might also claim the right to" furnish you shortly with a report from the hizhest authority," and while the people are suffering and continually run the risk of disease and death, reports on reports might make the enquiry, which has now lasted some eighteen months interminable. (6). That I consider it qui' e unnecessary for either party to report on the ex2ert's report, as you have now scientific evidence of the highest order, that a great public nuisance exists, and this is also confirmed by the sad experiences of an increasing number of victims. It is, therefore, the obvious duty of the local authority to accept their own expert's recommendation, and take prompt steps to secure the abatement of the said nuis- ance. (7) I notice that Messrs Long and Son might wish the printed reports circulated called in, and the report kept out of the newspapers. This is quite natural, but I must strongly protest against this privacy, seeing they have in their published letter to Dr Penny made a glaringly wrong statement which is grossly unjust to myself. They say in their letter to Dr Penny, " you do not, we notice, think It necessary to say what our neighbour's sewage consists of." In refutation of this grave charge I beg to draw the attention of your Council to page 10 on which is,the following clear statement The sewers and drainage system of Cuerden Hall were examined and found satisfactory." See also Dr Dunlop's analysis, report on which con- cludes as follows The above results show that this is a satisfactory, effluent, and may be safely discharged in any stream." On page 11 you will find analysis by the same eminent man of sample of effluent taken from the 12in. sewer which report states "In all respects this is a good effluent. The organic ammonia and the amount of oxygen absorbed being well below the recognized standards of purified effluents, it may be safely discharged into any stream:. I can not reconcile Messrs Long and Son's statement with these facts, and I leave your boarc- to draw their own conclusions, also upon the false charges and y circu- lated. I ask ou to read this letter at your next thB which have been so lavishly and so long en- meeting, and I reserve to myself the right to senuda copy to the Local Government Board. In conclusion I will quote the closing sentence from an able leading article in last thatSatu they.ray's 'the ruinnegotronn Rural!Rx am i ne District - Clot n will never Council quite end the Thelwall nuisance by a policy of secrecy and concealment." Yours truly It 1 NAYLOR. Thelwall View, Grappenhall, Warrington. August 9th,1901. To the Runcorn District Council, Re Thelwall Nuisance,Gentlemen,—As a ratepayer living in the immediate neigh- bourhood, I take the liberty of appealing to your Council to try and at once to put a stop to this grievous and long vexed question. That there has been a number of deaths and much sickness in the locality is beyond doubt. That a very strong and offensive smell arises from the open ditch is an indisput- able fact. And that It is now close upon two years since the matter was first brought under your notice is well-known to the Council. From the varying moods and ohameleon- like reports of your Medical Officer little success :mild be hoped for. But wh In we saw that the Council had resolved on the wise precauti m of securing outside and independent expert evidence we natur- ally thought there was a desire to redress the long-standing nuisance. Why should the Public—many of whom are more concerned than your Council— be kept in the dark as to the expert report? Why that report should be referred back to Dr Adams for ounfirmation or otherwise is certainly an enigma. The Medical Officer's reports In the past were discredited or disregarded ; yet now he is to have the final say as to the expert's report. This certainly does not seem fair to your Council, to the public or to the expert. I trust waiving every other consideration but that of the public weal your Connell will speedily see that the needful is done, and the Tbelwall nuisance has become a thing of the past. lam yours obediently, STEPHEN ti. JENKINS. Grappen hall August 10th. 1901. Geo. T Ashton Req., Clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Council, Dear Sir: In reply to yours of the 31st ult enclosing copy of Dr Penny's report and asking for any remarks thereon, we wish to say that having bsen for some time in consultation with the highest known authority in tanners' effluents, etc, Sir Henry Roscoe and his able representative Ur Frank Scudder, we thought it best to submit the report in question to them and now beg to hand you copy of his reply for,the consider- ation of your Council at their next meeting, but not for publication. Although we have, wejoelieve, adopted every known means of removing all solid matter, we are ready as ever to adopt any reasonable suggestion from such a meeting of exnerts as Mr Scudder suggests, if your hoard is still not satisfied that the true source of all the evil is from the closed up sewers and deficieut drainage on the Thelwall side of the Culvert In Waste-lane. We would ask them to explain by any other hypothesis the plain fact that no such illnesses have existed among our employ6s (as has been abundantly testified) many of them living with their families on or about the tanyard, and the remainder spending their time there every working day We cannot help feeling that the allegations brought against us are both ignorant and libellous. We are, yours faithfully, WILLIAM Loge AND SON. The Clerk then read Mr Scudder's private report, and , submitted that it was now time for the Council to Con- sider Dr Penny's rerort and Dr Adams's observations thereon.</p>
        <p>_ The report of Dr John Penny, M. 8.. D.Sc„ (Public Health) F.R.)L11,, Aldershot, showed that having received instructions from the clerk (Mr G F Ashton) to investigate the origin of an alleged nuisance arising from the Grappen- hall Tannery and the ditch or brook which divides the townships of Thelwall and Grappenhall, he visited the place on several occarions between May 10th, 1901, and June 12th. 1901. He found the alleged nuisance described, by residents and other persons living elsewhere as a •• putrid offensive:streuch like that of highly decomposed animal matter," and which appeared (from the statements of the persons affected thereby) to be injurious to health producing similar symptoms to those described ty the late Dr Ballard in his Report on Offensive Trades, published in the supplement to the reports of the Medical Officer to the Local Government Beard for 1876-77-78, which were '. Loss of appetite, nausea, sometimes actual vomiting, sometimes ' diarrhoea, headache, glddiness,falntness, and a general sense of depression, and malaise." The variety of leather manu- factured at the Tannery was chiefly " Sole Leather." Then follow some particulars regarding the process of tanning employed by Messrs Long, and a microseopie•exami- nation of the effluent taken from theces. B Sc. spool pa B in the R e ditch l reported upon by Mr F Craven Moore, Of Owens College. Manchester. and Dr W II Fothergill, m A Infirmary, Manchester, both of which indloatet a septic ferment. Continuing,Dr Penny remarks ttiat the wasted or exhaust- ed tan liquors, soak waters, Ao, were all collected in a well, from which they were primped up into several settling ta”lcs, from which the effluent ran into the brook or ditch. These settling tanks were unsuitably constructed, badly situated, and of insufficient capacity for the quantity of waste material to be treated so as to get a satisfactory effluent. Dr Thomas Dunlop, MM., D x ii., of Aldershot in his detailed report on the effluent taken by himself from the stream running from the tanyard towards the cesspool, and sealed in the presence of one of Messrs Long's workmen., states :—Both the organic ammonia and the oxygen absorbed were extremely high, the latter showing, that if an effluent was passee into any stream, it would take out of the water the dissolved oxygen and render it incapable of supporting animal (fish) life. This sample of effluent petrified on being kept and gave off a most offensive odour,and in Dr Dunlop's Opinion was not tit to be discharged into any stream Reporting on a seemed sample of sewage effluent taken and sealed in the presence of one of Mr Naylor's workmen, Dr Dunlop reports :—" A very bad effluent, which decomposed rapidly on standing and gave off a putrifying odour; quite unfit to be discharged into any stream." Dr Penny continuing his report states that as regards general cleanliness the condition of the tanyard was not so good as most of the tanneries he had inspected, and the brickwork flooring around the tanpits in some places was defective. The brook or ditch was about eighty yards in length, and received liquid material from three sources viz :—(1) The stream from Cliff-lane Tannery end. (2) A two feet sewer pipe. (3) A one foot sewer pipe at the other end. The stream passing tnrough the field, before it reached the tanyard. was stained and contained a little sediment Visible when taken up in a glass tumbler. This staining was due to the drainage of the land water through some old pits which had been filled up with ths old tan refuse. After crossing under the Knutsford-road it received a pollution from four cottages. and the tnnyard effluent which rendered it a dark brown colour and very frothy. It then flowed alongside the road (Waste-lane) inside a private fence until it reached a c.sspool about nine feet in diameter and ono-and-a-half feet deep. This cesspool, which was arched over, had its over- flow into a culvert which passed under Waste-lane and the Bridgewater Canal, and later on flowed into the River Mersey. In reporting Upon the samples of effluent taken from the cesspool, Dr Dunlop says :—" This sample scan scarcely be classed as an effluent. It is quite as foul as many samples of sewage. It decomposes rapidly on standing, giving off a putrifying odour; this sample ought not to be turned into any stream. When the above samples were collected, the brook was smelling and the cesspool had a froth covering the liquid about six inches in depth. (The two feet sewer was only running with one and a half inches wetted perimeter, Mr Naylor having diverted his sewage from entering the same. At the same time the one foot sewer from Waste-lane was absolutely dry, and appeared to have been In that condition for some days ) When collecting the sample trout the cesspool under the archway, ax offensive effluvium nuisance was distinetly perceptible, which was intensified on stirring up the contents of the cesspool. This efflavium nuisance in Dr Penny'a opinion was due to a septic ferment. The two feet sewer pipe divides Cuerden Hall grounds from Cliff lane Tanyard. On Dr Penny's early visits it re- ceived the sewage from Cuerden Hall and four cottages. Sample was taken on May 10th, 1901. The analysis of this sample Showed it to be a satisfactory leffluent and one which might safely be discharged into any stream. On his last visit there was only a flow of one-and-a- half inches wetted perimeter; Mr. Naylor having In the meantime altered the disposal of his sewage by putting the</p>
        <p>same on the land. This sewer in addition received the drainage of several fields from a ditch which was polluted. The sewers and drainage system of Cuerden Hall were ex- amined and foand satisfactory. The one foot sewer (Waste- lane) traced from the brook or ditch, bifurcated in Cuerden Hall grounds, the point of junction being ventilated by a shaft, which was attached to a tree. One branch came across the fields,conveying drainage from the same the other passed into Waste-lane and received the sewage from about tan cottages, its opening near the top of the hill, received the liquid from a pit, which, on his early visits, was praetically a "common cesspool " giviug off a disagreeable odour. On 1 his last visit this cesspool was dry. A drain baying Its exit in this pit, crossed Waste-lane, and appeared again In close proximity to a manure heap (Hewitt's Farm) and received the liquid from a ditch poltuted by the sewage from some cottages and a large dwelling hmse. Sample of eft] lent was taken from this one foot sewer. The report by Dr Dunlop on _ .. .. .</p>
        <p>this says : 'ln all ?aspect; this is is-good effluent.</p>
        <p>organic ammonia and the amount of oxygen absorbe i being well below the recognised standards of pnrified effiaents, li</p>
        <p>_ may sa'ely be discharged Into any stream." In conclusion Dr. Penny adds :—ln his opinion the brook or ditch shoald oe covered in throughout ita wbole length, . _ .</p>
        <p>thereby abolishing the cesspool,and ventilating shafts erected one at the Tannery end, and another at the end a the two- feet .sewer, which sewer should be continued beyond Cuerden</p>
        <p>Hall for another fifty Or sixty yards.</p>
        <p>The sew- age arrange-</p>
        <p>ments should be reconstructiseat the top of Waste-lane,-so that the old pit, which, as he hid previously stated, was prio- tically a cesspool, would be done away with. The eflluent from Messrs. Longs Tan yard, when of standard purity,should be conducted in special (Sanitary) pipes to the outran on the " ' .</p>
        <p>Mersey side of the Bridgewater canal ; as undoubtedly at times, an offensive effluvium nuisance injurious to health</p>
        <p>arisestherefrom.</p>
        <p>Dr Adams then read an exhaustive critisism of Dr Pennrs report. He said : .• The quotation from Dr Ballard's report on offensive trades deals with the condition of offensive</p>
        <p>-----.. tanneries of 25 or 30 years ago, and can scarcely be held to apply to modern tanneries. Moreover Dr Ballard, in this report quoted statements in another Wive that careful and repeated enquiries had failed to show that any special disease or conditions of ill-health were to be found in proximity to tanneries. This is borne out at Cliff-lan. tannery where no sickness has been known to occur among the workmen which</p>
        <p>was even suspected to have been caused by the tannery. Salted hides are largely used at this tannery. This fact affords explanation of the large pioportion of chlorine in the analyses the chlorine occurring In the effluent as chlorine of sodium or common salt which has been soaked out of the salted hides by addition of fresh water." Dr Adams proceeds to say that Dr Penny's report In regard to the processes of soaking and liming is inaccurate and mislealing ; he dissents from Dr Penny's finding of an objectionable practice in the drying of hide trimmings; and says he knows of no justification for the stat.me&amp;- that from the stage of introduction of the hides to the tan liquors up to the finish of the hides as leather for the market the process differs considerably from that of most tanneries. No evidence is adduced for the statement. But</p>
        <p>it was immaterial to the issue if the process should even differ entirely from that of all other tanneries if no nuisance was thereby created. Continuing the Medical Officer said Dr Penny's theory of an effluvium nuisance is not substantiated by any scientific observations or evidence in his report, but was merely his opinion. On the subject of the effluent resulting from the tanning process Dr Adams dissents from the expert's report thee the settling tanks at the tannery are unsuitably constructed. They have practically been con- structed in accordance with his (Dr Adams') own suggestions and in compliance with the authorities in charge of rivers pollution prevention. As to their situation it was the most suitable that the area afforded, and provided ample fall from fast to last, whilst as to their capacity he doubted if Dr Penny could furnish examples of any tanneries whose tanks excelled these in either completeness, capacity, or efficiency. Further. as I to obtaining a satisfactory effluent, he would refer the Council to the County Analyst's analyses of these effluents after the first re-construction of the tanks had been undertaken. The solid matter was now entirely removed, or was so small in quantity as to be described by Dr Peony's analyst as only .• a little brownish deposit." He (Dr Adams) anticipated that the new filter bed would remove that also, but as that deposit was " principally of bark " it was not very liable to putrefactive changes and the evolution of offensive smells. It therefore, appeared that Dr Penny condemned the tanks on the ground of inefficiency while his own analyst proved their efficiency in removing the solid materials by not considering them to be worth estimating in a °herniae! analysis. He further dissented from the interpretation placed by the analyst himself on his results The chlorine was undoubtedly high, but that was due to the chloride of sodium (common salt) which was obtained from the soaking of salted hides. The other soluble salts in solution were remarkable low, and, in fact, he doubt-d it such good analyses would be obtained from many, if from any tannery effluents. As regarted general cleanliness of the tannery be had seen no ground for complaint during the many visits he had made. and he was assured that every alley or passage was swept every night in accordance with regulations. It was true that here and there patches of defective brickwork might be found, but this was inseparable from the ordinary wear and tear of brickwork, and was remedied as occasion required. The facts revealed by the analyses of the effluents from the tannery at the cesspool, from Mr Naylor's two feet sewer and from Waste-lane one foot sewer only emphasised his ,1 (the Medical Officer's) original report on this rnattsr—that the conditions at and around the cesspool should be dealt with in accordance with sanitary requirements. Referring to Dr Ballard's report on effluvia as being a sort of disease, quoted by Dr Penny, the Medical Officer says these were the views of 30 and 40 years ago in reference to septic changes in organic material, and were entitled to the highest respsct at :that date, but in view of the now universal acceptance of the germ theory of disease he coulsinot accept Dr. Penny's effluvium theory of disease, even when the effluvium was engendered bya septic ferment. In addition to the effluvium or smell, the disease germ must be introduced to the system by some means—the typhoid bacillusVor typhoid fever,the diphtheria bacillus for diphtheria and se on. Did Dr. Penny seriously contend that such re- cognized diseases could be engendered in the human being by an effluvium alone, without the specific germ accompany- ing it ? If he did not so contend, his effluvium theory of disease fell to the ground, along with the antiquated and almost forgotten p,thology upon which it was based. If Dr Penny contended that effluvia or smells could unassisted, produce specifiedisease the doctrines of modern pathogenesis opposed him at every point. In this particular matter—the ontbreak of disease in Thelwall they were concerned to consider diphtheria only, and his (Dr. Adams) own opinion was that it was not to the effluent from the tannery that they had to turn kw an explanation of the disease which had afflicted this Par- Center township, but to a combination of insanitary conditions which were prepared and laid by other authorities than that District Council, and which conditions it became their duty now to remove, as far as possible, in view of these districts having recently been added to their sanitary charge- He regretted to have felt compelled to dissent so snorts from the tenor of Dr Penny's report, and was glad to reach a point at which their views were more in harmony, and that was in reference to the action which should be taken on this I alleged nuisance arising from the Cliffe Lane Tannery in Grappenhall. and from the ditch or brook dividing the townships of arappenhall and Theiwall. On this point he had nothing to add to his Iformer recommendations which , were practically adopted en bloc by Mr Penny in the first paragraph of his recommendation—with the exception of his first recommendation that the brook should be covered in throughout its entire length, thereby abolishing the cesspool. He would suggest (1) that large pipes should be employed Of sufficient calibre to take the tannery effluent along with the fullest access of storm water that might be anticipated in a time of continuous heavy rain. (2) The cesspool should be abolished,and the lower level that would thus be secured, of several feet shou'd be utilised to provide a snore rapid flow of the sewage and the polluted brook to its existing culvert under the Bridgewater Canal. This would provide a greatly increased rapidity of flow and would assist materially in keeping the new sewer (for such it would be) free from deposit. (3) A ventilating shaft should be pro- vided at the cesspool end. (4) A manhole should also be pro- vided at the union of the brook with the two sewers,for facili- ty ofidealing with any mishap occurring to any of the sewers. (5) A large intercepting trap should be erected at the tannery end ; and (6) a ventilating shaft should be provided at this end.(7)The two-feet sewer shoald be continued beyond its present extension for another 60 yards' and provision should be made at! its furthest extremity against the escape of sewer gas from such a lensth of sewer which would then extend to the other side of the Bridgewater Canal in an un- broken length, save for the shafts and manhole. (8) The sewerage foe Waste-lane should be carefully examined and re-constructed in accordance with modern requirements. They appeared to possess an heirloom of an insanitary past in this locality, and one whose conditions it behoved them to rectify as soon as possitle, exactly as they should deal with s similar condition of affairs had the revelation of its nature come about under ordinary circumstances. He dissented from[Dr Penny's final recommendation that the effluentfrom ,the tannery when of standard purity should beconducted in special (sanitary) pipes to the outfali on the Mersey side of the Bridgewater Canal; as. undoubtedly, an offensive effluvium nuisance Injurious to health arises therefrom. He (Dr Adams) thoueht this was in advisable, both on the ground of unnecessary expense -when an efficient outfall with right of way already existed—and on the further ground of requiring to obtain wayleaves. He did not think if all these difficulties were removed that this plan wonld present [any advantages over the course of action which he had already advocated. He did not know what Dr Penny wee referring to bya standard of purity. He had sought advice from higher quarters on this matter, but no one would lay down a standard of purity for a tannery effluent. The Council's only guide appeareo to him to be the official reports of the County analyst. He had freely sanctioned the admisssion of this effluent "to any running stream." This appeared to him to be the Council's justifiea- tion of action If any objection was to be taken to their turning the effluent into a running stream it should be taken at the instance Of those in charge of the prevention of rivers pollution. This objection was not forthcoming. Could Dr Pansy describe the sewage treatment in operation at any tannery which worked to a Standard of purity? If so what was the standard, and where was the tannery? It ap- peared necessary that these matters should be dearly defined or at least approximately defined before they could dictate to any tannery what purity standard was the least that the District Council could be satisfied with. At present their choice seemed to be limited to the adoption either of the County Analyst's description of the effluent as " fit to be turned lute any running stream,"or Dr:Dunlop's description of his three samples of the effluent as " unfit to be discharged Into any running stream." And, when the effluent was of standard purity, why should it be necessary as suggested by Dr Penny to conduct it by an expensive special method to a new outf all by a new sewer when the existing watercourse contained:their rights of way-leaves Ac.. and afforded, when altered as he (Dr Adams) had suggested, an ample scope for dealing with this effitteat. Finally, in his opinion, the entire combination of the tannery effluent, Mr Saylor'S two-feet sewer effluent, and the Waste Lane one-foot sewer effluent. when divested of the adventitious importance which had, unfortunately been imported into the case. presented a problem quite simple of solution, by the course of action he had already indicated. Mr. Wallington asked whether Dr Adams held a diploma for public health from the Local Govern- ment Board. Dr. Adams replied that he did not, and explained It was not considered necessary for anyone who had held the office for three years to go back to school to learn. He was supposed by his experience to have sufficient knowledge. (Hear, hear). Mr Woodward suggested that the Council should I follow the line suggested by Mr Scudder, and authorize Dr Adams to represent the District Coun- cil, and meet Mr Scudder or any other representa- tive on being requested to do So by Messrs Long. Mr Linaker, in fairness to Dr Penny, moved that</p>
        <p>he should be furnished with a copy of Dr. Adams' observations on his report, and also a copy of the report of Mr Scudder. Mr Wallington seconded. Mr Woodward : I think we all agree with that. This resolution was then carried. Mr Stokes understood that both Dr Penny's report and Dr Adams' remarks would go to the public. He was not present at the last meeting but understood that the report had been withheld. The Chairman: It was not withheld ; only held , over until this meeting. Mr Talbot failed to understand the action of the Vice-Chairman. He was the one, if he remembered rightly, to praise Dr. Penny and advocate the en- gaging of a specialist, no matter what the cost. Now the whole thing was to be put on one side,and they were to return to their own Medical Officer, who,before he (Mr Talbot) went away,either would not or did not satisfy them, and now he was to be sent to represent them again in the matter, It struck him as being unnecessary. They had Dr. Penny's report, and the pith of the whole •report, as he understood it, blamed the process of tanning. Why with- hold it ? If the. system was bad it was their duty to stop it. He was quite surprised to see the short report it was, and equally surprised to hear how much Dr Adams had to remark uvon it. Indeed his remarks were about 40 times as long as the re- port. He proposed that the matter should go to the public. Mr Wallington seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr Viroodrd pointed out that it woul i be un- fair for certain particulars relating to Messrs Long's process to be made public, and what were described as unfounded charges ought not to go to the public without the persons interested having an oppor- tunity of repudia.ing them. He asked for delay in the matter in order that they might have further light thrown upon the matter He gave Dr Adams a further opportunity to meet what was a direct attack upon him, and what had been brought as a direct attack upon him as Medical Officer of Health. He was for right and for the discovery of the wrong. Mr Wallington: We are all the same</p>
        <p>Mr Woodward; Question. (Laughter.) Con—l diming he argued that what they as a Council I had to deal with was" Whence does the nuiqance arise? not " How F' And when discovered, it was their dir.y as a District Council to remove it at once. He was prepared to take the steps re- quired for the abatement of the nuisance. The first thing seemed to be to work in harmony with Messrs Long for tha better arrangement of their tanyard, by the consultation of the Medical Officers named. The next step was to take up the sugges- tions of Dr Penny added to the suggestions of their own Medical Officer, and also the suggestions of Mr Scudder, and work them into a scheme that would finish the Thelwall nuisance for ever. Admiral Parry rose up not knowing where they were. (Laughter). He was quite in harmony with the suggestion that the experts should meat. At some length Admiral Parry repudiated the statement that the suggestions tor remedying the evil as claimed by Dr Aaams were Dr Adams' ideas at all. They were his own, and when he put them forward, if he remembered rightly, Mr Farrington was the only one who said that Admiral Parry's suggestions were the only way out of the difficulty The gist of the whole thing lay in the process of tanning. He was told that the process bad been changed during the last five years, and if that was so, and they could gt to know it, they would find that the history of the change was the history of the alleged nuisance.: Mr Woodward then proposed that a copy of the correspondence together with a copy of Dr Penn7's Mr Scudder's and Dr Adams' reports be forwarded to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>Mr Bradford moved also that the reports be printed, they were so lengthy the members could not be expected to grasp them all at once. _ .</p>
        <p>Dr Adams at the request of Mr Woodward then re-real that portion of his report suggesting the remedy of the alleged nuisance. Mr Linaker gathered that they were told there was no nuisance injurious to health. If that was so why should they go to the the trouble of piping ths ditch.</p>
        <p>Dr Adams said he had repeatedly stated there was a nuisance at the confluence.</p>
        <p>_ Mr Wallington: Injurious to health? Dr Adams: Presumably. Mr 'Wallington: Then why didn't you tell us before, snd save all this trouble, doctor? Dr Adams: I have stated so repeatedly both , verbally and in writing,until I was almost ashamed I of repeating it. Mr Wallington: I have asked you several times and you have refused Co certify there was a nuis- ance injurious to health. Dr Adams: I have refused to certify that the tannery nuisance is injurious to health; and refuse to recognise even now that the smell from the tannery is in any way responsible for the sickness and death in the neighbourhood. That is all I tef used.</p>
        <p>The Chairman and Clerk agreed that such was the case</p>
        <p>Ultimately it was decided that copies of the re- ports and letters should be sent to:the Local Govern- ment Board, and that Dr Penny should be supplied with the reports of Dr Adams and 31r Scudder and asked for his remarks thereon, and tbat the follow-</p>
        <p>ing committee should draw 11Pa scheme On-tl;;lines suggested by Mr Woodward with a view of effecting a remedy: Messrs Mclntyre, Pilling, the clerk pit</p>
        <p>G F Ash-ton), the Inspector, and the surveyor (ArJ Swinton). .</p>
        <p>THE EXPERT'S PEES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following :—" South Western, Hotel, Aldershot, August 10th, 1901. The Runcorn Rural DiFtrict Council. To DrJohn Penny, FR S. K.</p>
        <p>_ Drs. Report on the rihelwall and Grappen' hall Nuis- ance (including analys-s, &amp;c.,) May and June 1901. Sixty-five guineas. Mr Masker : It is a small bit of papsr but a very large bill. I think Dr Penny has seen in the news- paper the remark of the vice-chairtnan that he didn't care whether it cost £7 or .£7O. (Laughter.) Mr Wright; Is there no way of taxing it? The Clerk: Ido not see any way. (Laughter.) Mr Williams : I propose that it be paid as soon as possible and buried with the rest. (laughter. Mr Stokes ; It would bury the ditch also (Loud laughter.) Mr Wright moved that the bill be left in abey- ance for a time, and !Sir Woodwari seconding, it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCL.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr J Knowles, J.P., presided, and there was a full attendance of mem- bers and officials present. QUALITY OF FIADDSHAM WATER. Mr Moseley Williams referred to the Frodsham Lord- ship water supply, and requested that Mr Farrington (the Inspector) be allowed to read the: report of the analyst, Dr Carter Bell, on the supply. It would be more satisfactory. Mr Farrington then submitted the analyst's remarks, ' which were as follows:—" This is an exceedingly good water and fit for domestic use, but there is certainly a faint odour and also a faint fltvour rather resembling tarry rnatter. This may be caused by the water passing through new pipes which mw have been caulked with some tarry rope.--J. Carter Bell.</p>
        <p>TUE TIMERS POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>- Mr Reginald Potts. clerk to the County Council, writ mug with regard to the Rivers Pollution, on August lst 1901, intimated he had communicated the contents of the Clerk's letter of the 3rd ultimo to the Rivers Pollu tion Committee at its last meeting when he was instructed 0 intimate to the Runcorn Council that they hoped the serving of notices would be followed up by proceedings being taken against the pollutars. DOUBTFUL WATER.</p>
        <p>_ The following letter was received from Mr Reginald Potts, clerk to the County Council under date August Ist, 1901, "Runcorn Rural District Council, analysis of water. Dear Mr. I submitted the copy report trans- mitted with your letter of the 30 •ti of May last to the Public Health Committee of this Council at its last meeting when I was instructed with regard to Nos 532 and 535 to urge upon your District Council the advisability of taking all available steps for securing a proper supply of water being laid on. With reference to the sample of water submitted to the County Analyst during the quarter ending the 31st of Iltarch last, the County Medical Officer of Health suggests that your District Council should be asked to arrange for the water sampled by them as 541 being properly filtered and for the use of the water sampled by them as 52 being discontinued as a domestic supply. I shall be glad if you will bring these matters under the notice of your District Council and inform me what action if any,they take thereon."</p>
        <p>Mr J Farrington regretted he had no remark to make regarding the first two samples referred to, but with regard to the others they would be discontinued when the water was obtained from Warringt)n for The!wall Bridge,</p>
        <p>THE OLD HOSPITAL. Mr C L Evans, Norton Estate Office, Runcorn, wrote under date August 3rd asking if there was any likeli- hood of the Hospital on the Ship Canal bank being sold, and if so,what price the Council would take for It? I The Council was of opinion they could not sell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>WARRINGTON. Coming Events As will be seen from our advertising columns, Shrewsbury annual fhral Ve,e which is one of the most important horticultural Ell IWS in the country, will be held on Wednesday and Thurscay next. Warringt m people desirous of seeing this great ex- hibition will have ample facilities for doing so as special excursions will be run to Shrewsbury On the days of the fete. The flower show prom:sea to be one of the most magnibeeet ever held in England, over £l,OOO being offered in prizes. Special entertainments are announced In- cluding selections by the bands of El M Royel Hem Guards (blue), ELM. Coldstream Guards, and H.M. Royal Marines (Portsmouth). A lar.se number of accomplished gymnasts and other artistes will appear and in the evenings there win he attractive fireworks displays: ASK for CUNNINGtuuri mall-m.l3s MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS THE KING OF TONICS.-63, SAMMY-STREET, %V arrinaton.—(.lclvt.) Local Licensing Transfers. The following transfers of licence were gracted by the magistrates at the Borough Police Court on Monday :—Off-licence, Orford-lane, from John Clarke to Henry William Price; General Wolfe, Church-street, from Mrs Annie M Brundrett to William Rogers; Cooper's Arms, Bewsey-streer,from William Rogers to Mrs Maud M Lcy ; Eagle and Child, from M'S Loy to Miss Maud Elancox ; Rail- way Tavern, from William As.pinali to William Edward Longmore Temporary authority was granted to Thomas Sh w, to sell at ti,e Sc ptre Warwick:roe, in lieu of Elizabeth Bentley. LADLES BEST GLACE )S ke;) )111 UNDER TEE CLOCK, BEIDCIR-STREAT. KITCHEN RANGES. Tile Grates, Marbl. end Wood Chimney pieces. sismAND.sltrss. Bridge-at—Adv. The Harvest. The harvest in C idisnesd, Rixton an ?arting- ton is on the whole a fairly good one. Piero are certainly a few fields which have yi, lded very poor erops, but en the other hand other fislds have given an abundance. Last week, notwi-hstanding the showery weather, many fields were cut, and should the weather hold good all the corn will be safely gathered in befere September. Potatoe, are turning out fairly well, but, owing tc the dry weather, there are many small ones. Apple and pear trees are laden, and there is a good show of damsons. A Boy's Narrow Escape —Fell into the Sankey Canal. A boy named John James bong, aged 14, son of Alfred Long, who resides at 40, Psiestley-oreet,had a narrow escape from drowning on Saturday morn- ing. It appsars that about ten o'clock he and a number of playmates were playing on the bank of the Sankey and St, Helens Canal near Buttermilk Bridge, when Long slipped and fell into the water. His friends raised the alarm and a man named Alfred Smith, toolmaker, Great Sankey, and two women— Mrs Williamson and a young woman employed as a domestic servant by Mr Mather, milk-dealer, both residing in the vicinity of the canal cam upon the scene. The little fellow had gone down but Smith jumped into the water, followed by Mrs William- son, who took hold of his band, the other woman remaining on the bank and keeping hold of Mrs Williamson. Smith fortunately discovered the whereabouts of the body, and managed though with difficulty, by grasping the lad by his hair, which was very short, to bring him to tie surface. He threw the unconscious boy across his shoulder and carried him to his home. Fortunately Dr: Smith was passing at the time, and at once set to work to restore animation, and after lengthened attention the lad was declared out of danger. Distribution of Children's Prizes at Daresbury On Monday evening, the scholars attending the Board Schools at Daresbury, Moore, Hatton and Preston Brook received their annual prize pro- vided from the endowment fund belonging to the parish. The following governors were present at the proceedings which took place in the Daresbury schools : Messrs Wynne Jones,J Cooper, J Ockles- ton; C W Phillips, Peter Tickle, and the Vicar (Rev F Clifton Smith), who afterwards presented the awards to those attending the Daresbury Schools. Money prizes were given for regular attendance, good conduct, and general efficiency, as well as books and useful presents to the most deserving. The following 20 children have never once absented. themselves during the year, although many live a considerable distance from school :—Herbert Leach, Ethel Ryder, Florence Furnival, Walter Prince, Harry Williams, Harry Cartwright, Willie Jameson Emily Broome, Nellie Warburton, Gladys Taylor, Wilfred Littler, Granville Furnival, Annie Taylor, James Houghton, Alfred Warburton, Frank Light- foot, Elizabeth Littler, John Boaler, John Jameson, and Alfred Potts, After the presentation, the Vicar addressed a few words to the scholars. He stated that during the short time he had been in the parish he had noticed with pleasure the good behaviour of the scholars, Tais reflected credit upon their teachers, their schools and themselves He earnestly recommended all to receive the beet &amp;location possible, and to work hard at their studies. Mr Cooper also made suitable remarks,and after hearty cheers had been given the clAldree, were dismissed for the afternoon holiday. BEST BOOTS Pea ALL--UNDER iota OLOCE.—Aa Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, A.N. The members in connection with the Warrington Juvenile Burial Society and the Widow andl Orphans Fund of the above order assembled ia large numbers at the British Workman Public House, If orsernarket-street, on Monday, the oc- casion being the 30th annual meeting of the Juv- enile Burial Society and the 36th of the Widows, and Orphans Fund. The meeting was presided over by the president of the Societies, P P C S. Thomas Miller, who was supported by the vice-presiders:, P CS, John Haley, P P C 5, James Parr (Treas- urer), P P C S, Lawrence Weir (General Secretary), P M, William Holmes, P P C S. William Eckerslay (trustees), PP CS, Ihonias Duckworth, P PCS Frederick Massey. P P C 5, Henry Warburton, P P C 5, James Gresty, P P C 5, ;tenses Lewe, and others. The Chairman briefly al- luded to the financial work of the past year and expressed his satisfaction at their beings able to add again such a substantial gain to their funds, after paying almost .£lOO in death daima which exceeded last year's death allowance by .Ifgei 15s. He assured the members that the officers fad striven all in their power to invest their capital at the very best interest that could be obtained axxl they were now receiving interest to the amount of .£64 19s lid per year. The report of the General Secretary, PP CS Lawrence Weir, showed that the. total worth of the Juvenile Society now amounted! to £2,275 5s sd, as Compared with £2,140 2s 8d or the previous year, thus making a gain upan the- year's transaction of £135 2s 93 with a total mem- bership of 1,272. The report farther showed tat the widow and orphans ftind had a caritai £205 18s 9d as compared with £199 2s 61 in the previous year, making a gain in this fund of SIB 16s 3(1. The Secretary in conclusion stated net the average gain of the Juvenile Burial Society for the past eight years had been at the rate of .£650 per annum. The auditors P PCS Peter Edgar andrit John Jones certified that the aecounts were sror- rect. The bankbooks, co-operation bond nrortgage deeds and vouchers had been submitted for insgee- tion and were in order. In the General Secretary and treasurer they had two faithful servants, who were at all times ever watchful of she Society's hesst interests. In the election of officers, PC S Jfrohn Haley was elected President ; DC S Joseph Williams, vice-president ; PPC S Lawrence Weir, general secretary ;PPCS James Parr, treaswer ; Bros William Holmes, Peter Edgar, William &amp;kers- ley, trustees. PPCS Peter Edgar was re-eletted auditor for the ensuing two years. The following were appointed tbe Committee :—Bros Joan josses, IFrederick Massey, William Slevin, Henry Cook, Thomas Miller, Samuel Bradshaw, William Hall, Joseph Adams, David Harris, Henry Wart:mama and James Gresty. A vote of thanks was accarded the retiring president, PP CS Thomas Millar for the admirable manner in which he has conducted the business during his term of office. Bro Miller, in response, said whatever he had done in the past for the good of the Societies, the expression of feeling that had been displayed that evening had repaid him for his services.</p>
        <p>The New York correspondent of the Daily Tele- graph" writes:—Mucb significance is attached to the exposure of police collusion with vice revealed on Sat- urday by the Society for the Prevention of Crime. The Society allowed one of its agents to pretend to be bribed by gamblers, and succeeded in securing specific evi- dence that the entire Tammany police department is at the service of such criminals as pay for protection. The case appears to be so overwhelming and the commun- ity is so shaken by the revelations, that the whole Tammany structure is in a fair way to be crushed. The police are panic-stricken at the disctosures, and the Republican Governor, Mr O'Dell, declares that be wilt not hesitate to use drastic) measures in dealing with the situation.</p>
      </div>
