      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>_ SHAMEFUL CRI7ELTY TO A HORSE-DRIINEEN DRIVER</p>
        <p>FINED Thomas Arnold, whose address is 368, Wilderspool road, appeared before Mr Birchall and other magistr..tes at the Warriagton P,-dice Court on Tuesday, charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Kent. street at 1.40 the previous afternoon and with cruelly illtreating a horse at the same time—P.S. Bell stated that at the time in question be was on duty in Knuts- ford-road and saw tho prisoner driving a horse attached to a cart. He was druok and was standing up in the cart,and pulling the reins in such a manner as to repeatedly drag the bit through the horse's mouth. Tne animal appeared to be very much distressed. Witnese' called to the prisoner to stop but he did not take any notice of the request. Witness followed him along New- road, and prisoner whipped the animal frequently. He suddenly turned into Kent-street asd the horse's face came into contact with the wall. This caused blood to flow freely through iti nostrils and mouth. He took the prisoner into custody and the animal was sent to a stable. A veterinary surgeon had to be summoned to stop the bleeding. Prisoner's clothing was covered with blood from the horse. The accident was entirely due to the prisoner's condition and to want of control over the horse.—P.C. Bennett stated that the horse bled pro- fusely for two hours.—lnspector Print, R.S.P.C.A, spoke to examining the horse. Its nasal organs were injured an it had lost a grew quantity of blood, There were eight severe wounds on the animal which must have been caused by the whip. The horse must have suffered great pain, caused by brutal treatment.—Prisoner was fined 59 for being druok, and 20s aud costs f9r the cruelty (£1 10. 61 in all).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BOY3' CRUELTY TO A DONKEY.</head>
        <p>BOY3' CRUELTY TO A DONKEY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court,on Monday, before Mr Birchall (is the chair) and other magistrates, five boys named George Ball, (15), 2, Prince-strelt,Mattltiew Knowles (18), Thomas Tarburton (19), R .bert:Aoran (17), Frank Sherratt (18) all of New-road, were summoned for ill-treating a donkey on July 2nd.— Knowles did not appear.—P.C. Rylact stated that he was on duty in arptey New-road in company with P.C. Hewitt and saw the defendants in a field illtreating a donkey. Ball was on the donkey's back and was thrashing it with a thick piece of wire. Sherratt wag also beating the animal with a piece of wire, Tarburton was prodding it with a fishing rod and the other boys were throwing stones at it. There had been many com- plaints abou' 'h. illtn,itment of donkeys in that neigh- bourhood.—Al '•-•a- Shaw Green said that only on the previous day he La . b.trll some older youths illtreating the donkey. His opinion was that the owner ought not to leave the animal there.—lnspector Print said he had seen the animal since. It had four wounds, two on the back, one on the flank and one on the side. It pi esented the appearance of having been thrashed with the wire produced. The defendants were ordered to be kept in the cells for throe days, Alderman Shaw Green re- marking that the magistrates were determined to suppress such conduct.</p>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court. FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (In the chair), Aldermen Fair- dough. Sutton, anchFrancomb, Councillors W Pierpoint, Roberts, end Messrs Thornton. Hepherd and S Wright. LAUNDRY EMPLOYEE SUMMONED.—LiIy Percival, 23, Bostock-street. Stockton Heath, was summoned fr leaving her employment at the Warrington Steam Laundry without giving a week's notice. thereby putting the firm to a loss of 13s—Joseph Bernard. the manager. stated that the defendant wanted to give notice on Tuesday. but witness declined to receive it, as the custom was to give notice on Saturday. Despite this, the defendant left work on the following Satur- day—ln answer to Mr Wright witness said that there was not a notice posted up in the works with reference to giving notice.—Taklug this fact into consideration the magistrates fined defendant in the costs (40. WIFE DENERTION.—Thomas Shaw, 92, Cartwright-street, was summoned for deserting his wife, Elisabeth Shaw, 95, Longford Street, on July 19th.—Mr Browne appeared for the complainant, and stated that the parties quarrelled in March and the defendant left his wife. Ultimately be returned and they lived together until July 13th. On that day defendant did not come home with his wages until nearly six o'clock in the evening, and he was then very drunk. He gave his wife a black eye and she left him and slept at a neighbour's that night. She returned the following day and lived with him until July 19th. On that day defend- ant came home sand lornmenced to pack up. He gave his wife 10s, bade her goodbye" and left her. He had been away ever since and had sent her only 5s a week. His earnings amottrited to £3 weekly.—Defendant contradicted the statement with regard to his wages, stating that they averaged 30s. and also stated that his wits was of drunken habits and he could not live with her—The bench granted a separation order with 5s per week alimony. Mosnsg.—Before Mr Birchall (In the choir). Alderman Shaw Green and Messrs Hayward and Thornton. A VIOLENT CHARACTER.—Patrick Richard Moran, 15, Portland-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Peter-street on Saturday afternoon and also with having wilfully broken a pane of glass at 28, Winwick- street.—PX. Muskett said he was in Horsemarket-street on Saturday afternoon and was told by Mr Jthnson who kept a refreshment house in Wlnwlck-road that a man had broken one of the windows of his shop. He afterwards saw Moran in Peter-street. He was drunk and wanted to fight. Be was making use of filthy language. Prisoner was pointed out to him as being the man who had broken the window.— Evidence was also given by Mr Johnson and a boy named William Lucock, who said he saw Moran knock his band against the window and break IL—The Chief Constable said that Moran only came out of gaol on Saturday.--In default of paying a fine of 108 and costs in each case he was sent to gaol for a month. POLICEMEN VIOLENTLY AsaAULTED.—Ernest Walker, 22, Huntley-street. Sankey Bridges, was charged with having been drank and disorderly in Scotland-road at ten minutes past one that morning, and with assaulting Sergeant Bell and P.C. Reddington —Sergeant Bell said he was in Scotland Road when his attention was called to the defendant by P.C. Reddington. The man was lying across the road, He drunk. Witness was about to ask him what his name was. and where became from, when he kicked him on the legs several times, and also struck P.C. Reddington in the face. He was taken into custody and on the way to the Police Station was very violent. At the Police Station four or five policemen had to hold him down while he was searched. He refused to give any account of himself.—The Chief constable said that he had persuaded the man to speak that Morning 1 and he told him that he was a wire drawer at Sankey Wire Works and that he had had no drink on the previous even- t lug, but was subject to epileptic its. He left honie at four o'clock In the afternoon and had two fits.He had no memory of the occurrence which ended in his arrest.—James Walker said he was the uncle of the defendant, who was a very quiet and inoffensive young man. He wee subject to these fit,. Witness was told that he had had some drink on the pre- Ylous d ay.—The Chairman said these attacks on the police were getting very common and they must be put a stop to. Defendant would be fined 10s and costs or 14 da:,s' Imprison-</p>
        <p>ment. INCITING A DOG TO FIGHT —Thomas Burke. a hawker. living at 4. Knowlescyard, was summoned for illtreating a dog by causing it to fight on the sth inst,—P.C. Clough Said he was on duty in Winwick-street In plain clothes and saw • the defendant who bad a large Irish terrier. Defendant In. cited the dog to fight with a retriever. The animals fought for about five minutes in "a shameful and cruel manner." Defendant " hissed" the dog on several times fine of 10s and costs was impoied._ _ _</p>
        <p>A Savanz FIGHT.—John Wares, 2, Plllings-court, and Thomas Knockton, 26, Dial-street, were summoned for com- mitting a breach of the peace by fighting in Pilling's-court on the 2nd Inst.—P.S. Williamson stated that he was on duty at the Pollee Station when he received a complaint that the defendmts were fighting most savagely In Pllllng's-Court. A 'earful disturbance was going on in the neighbourhood and</p>
        <p>a woman told him that two men were killing each other. He found the defendants stripped to the waist. and both seemed out of breath witt fighting, Hares had received a cut over the eye and Knockton had a cut on the temple.— Defendants were bound over to keep the peace for six months. _</p>
        <p>SUNDAY GAMBIANG.—On the evidence of P C Plumb, James Thomason, 18. silver-street. and Alfred Beckett, 37, Scotland-road, were fined 10s and costs for playing at pitch and toss in a public-road, near Arpley Cricket around on Sunday the 4th inst.—The Chief Constable said that during fine week ends gambling was simply a pest and a curse in suburban districts. STONE THROMBI:I.—For throwing stones in Winwick-road on August 7th, two boys named Joseph Caddick, 15. Kerfoot- street, and James Brookes, 238, Winwick-road, were, on the nformation of P C Lewis, fined 2s 6d each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. —Before Mr_F A Frost:</p>
        <p>ALLEGED TirErr or BILLIARD BILLS IT CROFT.—John Penman, 48, John-street. Wigan, and Edward Prior, 37, Hardy Butts, Wigan both working men, were charged with stealing four billiard balls of the value of 14s, the property of James Lodge. landlord of the °moral Elliott Hotel. Croft.— Prosecutor stated that the balls produced by the police were his arid he missed them about a month ago. They were worth about 3s 6d e,ch.—The police asked that the men should be remanded to the Couaty Petty Sessions.— —Mr 11 Crompton ((essrs A Browne and Crompton), who represented the prisoners made an objection, but applied for them to be liberated on bail. This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY—Before Messrs A Thorny, H Reynolds, P I Norman and R H Posnett.</p>
        <p>" STAGE* PLAY9."—Mr Wllliam Chadwick. manager of the Theatre Royal. was granted his usual license for the perform- ance of stage plays; as was also Mr Sdgar Salkeld Lea., manager oi the Public .Hall.</p>
        <p>".&amp; MIDNIGHT FLIT."—J. Ellis, 35,Suffolk-street,was sum- moned by Mr, William Henry Warburton, agent for the executors of the late Henry White. for fraudulently removing goods from 21 Collier-street, to avoid distraint thereon for the recovery of £6 12t 6d arrears of rent. —Mr. Warburton proved effecting an order of distraint, and Mr Luke Holmes, the auctioneer, testified to the fact that the goods had gone when he went to execute the distraint. He learnt they were taken away by the defendant at midnight—Defendant was ordered to pay 101 a montls, and the Costs 14s. SLEEPING OUT.—John Burke. who has a tecord of 60 previous convictions against himself, was sent to prison for two months with hard labour for sleeping out, in Mersey- street—John Critchley. a labourer, for a similar offence was imprisoned for a month.—Superintendent Hnnion, stated numerous complaints had been .received of the damage done to property by this class of people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NEW CATHOLIC CHURCH AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CATHOLIC CHURCH AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Foundation Stone Laying.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large congregation, the founda- tion stone of the new Roman Catholic Church at Latch- ford was laid by the Lord Bishop of Shrewsbury on Sunday afternoon. The estimated cost of the new chusch is £6,000. St. Mary's, Latchfor.-1, is the second oldest Catholic mission in the borough. As briefly stated in our last issue, it was opened some 40 years ago in the old cotton mill, St. Tames'-street and served by Rev Father Alcock from Altrincham- Some 35 years ago Father Alcock bought the present site and erected the school chapel. At this time a resident priest was appointed, the Rev P Mulvaney. Two years ago the schools were pronounced by the Education Depart- ment as insufficient for the accommodation of I the children in attendance. This necessitated the present Rector, the Rev Father McGrath entering upon the undertaking of building a new church. It will be capable of accommodating 600 persons. It will be of transitional Gothic design, and built of York- shire stone, with red stone dressings. The length of the interior will be 124 feet and the width 48 feet, while the:height of the clear storey will be 45 feet. The total height, including tower and spire, will be 130 feet. The principal entrance will be from St Mary-street, and there will be one from Clifton-street. There will be a nave and two aisles, with six granite columns on CACI) side of the nave resting upon a Yorkshire stone basis, a large sanctuary, with altar, &amp;c.. two side chapels, and vestries, Scc. Entrance from the Presbytery will be obtained through the cloister leading into the vestries. There will be an organ gallery and choir accommodation over the main entrance. The architect is Mr Robert Curran, of Warrington, and the builder, Mr Peter Mc- Lachl..n of Birkenhead.</p>
        <p>Tue r,te ot blessing and laying the first stone was performed with solemnity by the Bishop, who wore above his rochetta, the amice, alb, girdle, stole, white cope, and plain mitre, and carried his pastoral staff, accompanied by the Rev Father McGrath,the Rev Canon Lyuch, Chester, and the Rev Fathers Mark and Felix, CP, Sutton, Wilson, OS B, and Gibbons, 0S B, Warrington ; Langdon, Hooton, Ryan, Warrington . Mulcaby, Chester ; Cleary, Dukinfield, Chambers, Runcorn ; and Ryan, Middlewich. On Saturday night a wooden cross had been fixed where the altar is to stand. Toe Bishop blessed salt and water and sprinkled the spot where the cross had been placed. He afterwards sprinkled the stone with holy water and taking a knife engraved the sign of the cross on each face. The stone was then laid, and the whole of the foundations sprink- led with holy water. Dering these ceremonies prayers were recited. The principal part of the musical service was arranged from the Gregorian Chants for the occasion by Mr P Bete, and together with other appropriate music, was well rendered by an augmented choir. The BIIHOP spoke from the words'" Other foundation than this no man caa lay, but that which is laid, Christ Jesus our Lord." He said they bad just completed by the blessing of God the laying of the first stone of a new church, with all the solemnity of a very old rite. The Catholic Church knew nothing new. They therefore had followed Irtter by letter the full directions of their fathers by whim the foundation stones of churches in all parts of the world and in every language had been laid from time immemorial. They had laid the stone and blessed in the name of God and of the Church the foundations of this building. There was something at- tached to the laying of the first stone of a building, no matter what that building might be, whicti seemed to be peculiar to itself. If the building were a cottage, or hall, or public building, or • palace, or a church, human nature seemed to attach, and be thought very rightly attached, to the laying of the first stone i very ' singular and primary importance. It seemed to be a matter which was considered not to concern itself only, a ceremcny of the time being,but the first stone seemed to carry with it the responsibility and all that followed lin the building which was laid upon that stone. And so there was a kind of similarity between the life of an individual and the life of a building. Both had life,and whether it was the first stone or the birthday mattered little. l'hey looked forward with sympathy to the time when the building would be finished and when the ••ild would be grown up to man's estate, and no man dared prophesy what might happen in the meantime. The laying of the foundation stone of any ordinary building and the up-bringing of any family as units of the Empire were bound up with the welfare of the comnannweal, and if that was true in that connection it WAS infinitely more true in regard to the laying of the fnuadation-etone of a new church. What was a new church ? It was something, speaking generally to fellow citizens, not only to those of his own creed, but to fellow townsmen if he might say so, which had a wonderfully wide and general and, very important value. It was called truly tbe laying of the foundation stone of the house of God. If. those words meant any- thing—if they were not mere sentiment—they meant the rearing up innate by stone among their own houses of a house to Him to whom they all paid obedience, a house in which the worship of the Most High would be . .</p>
        <p>carried on, and, what was more importan-t, in which the Most High. would live. A passing stranger who cared fn. the welfare of the town would notice in the erection of this church one more element of peace and concord, one more building in which brotherly love would be taught, and above all a building devoted to the teaching of the knowledge of God. If that were so, with regard to the stranger what must it be with the children of the Church? They locked forward to this church with no common interest. This was to them verily and indeed the houss of God. There their children and children's children would learn what the law of God was. There they would drink of the waters of life whkh ran from the Saviour's foun- tain in the sacrament of the altar there they would learn in time of trouble where to seek peace and com- fort, in time of weakness where to seek strength, in time of doubt where to seek certainty. They would gather there about the tabernacle. Their hearts would be there because their home was there,the home of their Lord God, the Lord God of Israel, Father of His people. It was this and much more which it meant to the hearts and minds of the children of the Catholic Church. It would be the common house of God's people, where the poor and the rich, the ignorant and the learned, the sick and those who were well, the saint and the sinner could—nay, must—gather together to learn their duty and do it before the Lord their God. It was therefore not a mere ceremony they had performed that day. It was a work wrought with a future of which they could not foresee the end. Indeed it would have no end until all the churches were gathered together and they met before:their Father's throne. His Lordship then thanked those who were not of that diocese for their help and presence at the ceremony and all who had in any way I extended their assistance. Much more financial help , would be required, and he hoped those who were asked to give for this house of God would do so with their whole heart,so that the giving would be its own reward. , The proceedings then terminated. The s.role com- pany then passed by the stone and laid their contribu. tions upon it.</p>
