      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>rfr E EXAMINER, FRI DA Y, AUGUST 16 1901,</head>
        <p>rfr E EXAMINER, FRI DA Y, AUGUST 16 1901,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>Interesting Proceedings at Walton</p>
        <p>Park.</p>
        <p>This year's annual show in connection with the Warrington and District Agricultural Society,which was held in Walton Perk on Thursday,last week must be numbered amongst the most successful ever or- ganized by the Society. Though there was a slight falling-off in the number of entries, the quality of the exhibits showed en al-round improvement on previous years. In this latter respect, the worthy object of the Society—to promote a greater interest in agrieulture—is being achieved, and this is a matter for 'congratulation. On l'hursday morning the meteorological conditions were not such as to inspire the promoters with t•aeguine hopes for a auccessful show in point of attendance. When the proceedings opened, dark clouds were " lowering overhead," and several heavy showers were experienced. Fortun- ately for the comfort of the visitors and all concerned matters took a brighter turn after dinner, and the remainder of the day continued beautifully fine. This had the effect of attracting a great many visitors to the grounds and the proceedings passed off without a bitch. The deep-root Jd interest which the President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, evinces in the welfare of the society was once more demonstrated. As in former years the splendidly adepted grounds at Walton were placed at the disposal of the society, and the visitors were allowed to make an inspection of Sir Gilberts demesne. The gardens are at present looking at their best, and those models of cleanli- ness, the stables and piggeries, were inspected with ' the greatest interest and evident pleasure. The ring, grand stand &amp;c., had been fitted up at the sole expense of Sir Gilbert, the work having been executed by Mr. J Muskett. A meed of praise is due to all the officials for their indefatigable labours for the success of the show.</p>
        <p>A. prize list of over £7OO was competed for, the special prizes being handsome and valuable. They included a Distric-, Champion Cup (value £10) pre- sented by Sir Gilbert Greenell for the best exhibit in the shire classes sent by a resident in the society's district. Sir Gilbert Greensll also gave two silver cups value £5 for the best shire foal in the show and the best shire exhibit being the property of one of his tenants Mr F Stableton Bretherton, the Hall, Rainhill, gave a spacial prize, value £5, for the best shire filly,and Mr S Littleton Dewhurst offered a District Chal- lenge Cup (value £l5) for the best exhibit in the Arst four classes of cattle. Messrs Day and Sons, Crewe, offered one of their universal medicine chests (value £5) for the best pair of dairy cows. Mr T Charnock, Hale, near Liverpool, offered a pre- mium of .£3 for the best foal got by the horse hired to the Warrington Shire Horse Society and Mr Whittaker Kenworthy, Kelsall, near Chester, offered a silver cup (value £5 Sc) for the best foal by any of his horses. Four medals were offered, a geld one by the London Shire Horse Society, two silver ones by the Hunters' Improvement Society, and one silver medal by the Hackney Horse Society. _</p>
        <p>The number of entries was about sixty less than last year, the decrease being mostly in produce and district classes. This is accounted for by the bad season which has been experienced. There was a noticeable improvement in the quality of the exhibits, the young stock showing signs of careful breeding. Tbe Warrington Show has the reputation of attracting splendid exhibits in the shire classes, and it was con- sidered that the shire horses and brood mares on show on Thursday, were the best seen in any local show this year. Hunters were again of superior class, and the cattle were quite up to the average. It is rather surprising, however,that the entries in the cattle classes, considering the character of the surrounding district, do not increase.</p>
        <p>Mr James Gould, Lymm, was a conspicuous winner. He secured first and second prizes in the district classes for shire brood mares, first prize in four year old shire mares, second in colt foals, second in filly foals, second in two year old fillies, first in two year old colts, and first in three year old fillies. His well known prize mare " Lymm Lass" carried off the District Champion Cup. The best animal on show in the shire classes was the bay mare Boro' Quality, owned by the Bon Victor Cavendish, M.P.. which secured the first prize. It was exhibited at Whalley 012 Monday, and carried off the first prize in a stiff competition. Her foal, a bay of 41 months, was awarded third prize in the colt foal class. In the hunter classes "Lady Belmont "owned by Mr I Farish,Northwich, was one of the premier exhibits She was shown at: Adlington the previous day, but being slightly lame, did not carry off any prize. In hackneys Mr A C Sparkes, Alcrinchana, had no difficulty in winning with Rosemary in the brood mares crass, with Oldfield Roseinsis in foals, and with Oldfield Roselind in two or three year old geldings. In the cattle classes a pair of dairy cows exhibited by [Mr Richard Clark, Warburton Park, were the finest specimens. This was the first time they had been shown this season. Last year they won three championships, ' and were successful at four consecutive shows. One of them was awarded the thirty-guinea challenge bowl at the Cheshire show in 1900. A roan dairy cow I exhibited by Mr Clarke and which carried off several prizes, last year took the first prize, in the class for tenant farmers. The second prize in this class was secured by the shorthorn" Princess Sybil" owned by Mr J R Newton, Warburton. She lathe dam of a second prize winner at Birmingham show. A wonderfully good type of shorthorn was "Sweet Melody,"exhibited by Mr j R Newton,Warburton, which carried off first prize in the class for heifers, and also the district challenge cup. She secured several prizes last year, and promises to be equally successful this season. A red heifer exhibited by Mr 8 W Goeld, of Lynn, is a splendid specimen, and promises to turn out a future prize winner. In the classes for bulls, the roan bull exhibited by Mr S ea W G appear later on at other shows and will be found very hard to beat. The second prize in this season. will Ly ni mson., was one of the best seen this Class was obtained by Mr R Clarke's fine red bull. The sire was " Moss Hawk " who we numerous prizes in 1899 and was eventually sold at Birmingham for ex- portation at a very high figure. Mr Clarke's bull won the first prize at Altrincham last year. In the classes for bulls born In 1900 Mr R L Warbur- ton's red bull carried off the first. It is a very useful bull, and is described as " growthy and sappy.. An interesting feature of the day's proceedings was the horse jumping- There were nine competitors, the animals including Daidtr Lass, a ma mare, owned and ridden by Mrs Dudley Smith, Birmingham. She however had no chance against the other competitors. Their efforts to clear the five barred gates,pool of water, and 'other obstacles, were watched with great interest, The first prize was gained by little John, owned by F Heap. Rstntmottom, and Hardcash, owned by F V Granger, Farnden, Chester, came in second, Kelveno, belonging to W Favell, Sheffield, wag awarded the third prize, and Gamecock, owned by H Reynolds, Hartford, fourth, One portion of the grounds had been allotted to the exhibits of a number of seed merchants, agricultural implement agents. kzi. Messrs Garton's Ltd.. Warrington,had a highly creditable exhibit consisting of caonedn and e sAeepelpsli,awneheisch; Starkey and Morrison, Seed attracted muck attention. Other exialhitors Included William Cartwright, Moore, Honey ; Merchants, Warrington; Dutton and Bean, Agricultural Implement Agents. Warrington G Tandy, Ackers Nurseries, Latchford and Miss Massey, Acton Grange Nurseries; The Besses o'th' Barn band was in attendance and dig coursed a selection of popular music its their accus- tomed brilliancy. Appended are the names of the officials :—President : Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart; Vice-President : Colonel H Cornwall Legh ; Committee!: MOMS Richard Ainscough, Alfred Broome, T Charnock, Richard Clarke, William Fairhurst, B Greenough, J Gould, S Gould, Edward Gibbon, James flepherd, W H Jones, J Mainearing, Henry Mawdsley, Henry Milling jun., H G Roberts, Joseph Shakesbaft, James Tinsley, jun., Frederick Tinsley, Joseph Tickle, Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., and John White ; Hon, Secretary, Mr Colin Maclver ; Hon. Treasurer, Mx Joseph Knowles ; Secretary, Mr Beres ford Melville. JIIDOES : Shires and Draught Gedings,Mr J T C Eadie, Barrow-on-Trent, Derby, and Mr F W Griffin, Borough Fen, Peterborough ; Hunters, the Right Hon. the Earl ; of Orkney, wing Lodge, Leighton Buzzard, and Mr Hubert M Wilson, The Hermitage, Holmes Chapel Hackneys and Harness, Mr Frederick Wrench, Killacoona, Blllybrack Co., Dublin, and Mr C W 'Rigmaden Park, Kirkby Lonsdale ' • Jumpers, Mr C W Rigmaden Park. Kirkby Loesdale. and Mr E W Griffith, Knipton, Grantham ; Cattle, Mr A Stratton, Wichnor, Burton-oa-Trent, and Mr George AShbUrner, Low Hal!, Kirkby-in-Furness • Butter, Cheese, Eggs and Dressed Poultry, Mr Thomas 'Welsby, Rhyl ; Potatoes, Mr Joshua Ball, Southworth Hall, near Warrington ; Consulting Veterinary Surgeons, Mr Hugh Ferguson [ Warrington, and Mr Joseph Welsby, West Derby, ILiverpool ; referee,Mr John Stuart, Rostherne. STEN-strains : Shires and Draught Geldings, Messrs Peter Keowles,Elward Gibbon, Arthur Pirnlott, Joseph Tickle and John Whitlow ; Cattle. Messrs George Whit- low, Joseph Percival, J Atkinscm,Ralph ; Hunters and Jumpers, Messrs James Tinsley Jun., H H Ferguson Henry Whitlow, Gilbert Halsall, Joseph Palmer Hackneys and Harness,Messrs B Gordon Roberts,Joseph Baxter, Henry Milling Jun., F G Fairhurst : Butter Cheese, Eggs. Dressed Poultry at.d Potatoes. Messrs J Bayley-Isaac, F H Milling, Edward Hewitt, J Shotton and J Cooper; Finance, Mr J E Seeker. The Lunchoen.</p>
        <p>_ At one o'clock luncheon was served in a large marquee Sir Gilbert Greenall presiding. At the conclusion the</p>
        <p>usual toasts were honoured.</p>
        <p>1 In proposing " Success to the Warrington and Dis- trict Agricultural Society" the President said he thought they might congratulate themselves upon having got togetherA very good show indeed. Tile Society had not been in•existence very many years and he hoped that as they got better known throughout the country they would attract More exhibitors. Their breeding classes were all local and he thought those who had seen them would agree that they were very creditable indeed for any district. The cattle classes were very 'good indeed but he was sorry they did not increase in numbers. They had very few more entries now than when they first started. He nought that was a very great pity as cattle breeding was one of the most important branches of agriculture especially in a dairy county like Cheshire. Speaking of the horses Sir Gilbert said that in the , afternoon they wonld see some of the best specimens of 1 their breed that could be found in England. Colonel CORNWALL LEGH, vice-president, responded , to the toast, and referred in eulogistic terms to the assistance rendered by Sir Gilbert Greens!l to the Society. He expressed the hope that he and his wife would live for many years to continue their hospitality. (Hear, hear.) Ald-rman MONKS proposed the health of " The Judges," and said he was sure that the exhibitors had every confidence in the gentlemen selected for the work of judging. They knew the judges would do what they thought was right andffair.He coupled with the toast the name of Mr Wilson, who had just come into the district as Master of the North Cheshire Hunt. They were ex tremely pleased to see bim present, and off 2red him a hearty welcome to the district.</p>
        <p>In responding,Mr H. M. WILSON said the classes which they:had judged that day were extraordinarily good for a local show. He proceeded to speak of the value of thc local shows. He did not wish to say anything against the Royal Show or others of that class, but in the small farming districts it seemed impossible for the ordinary farmer to exhibit with any chance at the very large shows. The local shows were an encourage- ment to the small farmers and helped to keep up a spirit of emulation and to improve the quality of the stock. The Society had succeeded in doing that in this Jistrict. Speaking of the North Cheshire Hunt he said its prosperity absolutely depended upon the good will of the farmers. They wanted the Cheshire farmers to help them in every possible way• Referring to the practice of patting up barbed wire he said that " wiring " would stop hunting not only in Cheshire but all over England and he did not believe Cheshire farmers wanted hunting stopped. The speaker alas made a brief reference to , the Cheshire Yeomanry. The Government,he said, bad issued an order that they had to increase the Cheshire Yeomanry to 400 strong. H? asked the farmers to help them in getting recruits. The MAYOR submitted the toast of " the President" and said Sir Gilbert had been a most generous god- father to the Society. He hoped the days were very far distant when he would cease to act in the capacity of President. (Applause.) SIR GILBERT GREENALL briefly responded and the proceedings terminated. Prize List. SEHRES. . . .</p>
        <p>District classes,—Brood mares 4 years old and upwards with:foal at foot: land 2,3 Gould, Model Farm, mm ;3. W Cunningham, Childwall-house Farm, Childwall. Mares 4 years old and upwards, without foals : 1, special and gold medal J Gould ;2. R Ireland Blackburne, Hale Hall, near Liverpool ; 3, B Gibbon, Yew Tree Farm. Lymm. Colt foals: 1, W Cunningham; 2, J Gould :3, T Jeffs, Hapsford Hall, Helsbv. Filly foals : 1, ,J Billington. New Farm, Hale, Cheshire; 2. J Gould; 3. Thomas Percival, Moore, near Warrington. Yearling colts or geldings : 1, J Gould, 2. B Gibbon. Yearling fillies : 1 and 3, R Ireland Blackburne, Hale Hall, near Liverpool; 2, A Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook. Two-year-old colts : 1, Gould ; 2, N Pennington, Bollington, near Altrincham. Two-year-old fillies: 1 and special, A Broome ; 2. J Gould; 3. J Whitlow, Garlaugl Hall, Northwich. Three year old fillies : 1 and special. Gould :2. 3 Knowles, Booth Bank Farm, Agden, near Warrington; 3. W Turton, Burnt Mill Farm, Hale. Geldings, foaled in 1897, 1898, 1899; 1, B Gibbon ;2, J Whitlow; 3, W Turton.</p>
        <p>Open classes—Brood mares, four years old and upwards, with foal at foot : 1. Hon Victor C W Cavendish, M.P., Holker-hall, Carke-in-Cartmel; 2,8 S Raingill, The Grange Ringway, Altrinchana, Colt foals : 1 and special, W Cunningham.; special 3 Gould. Lymm ; 2, T Charnock„ Hale: 3, Hon Victor 0 W Cavendish, M.P. Filly Foals: 1. Samuel S Raingill. The Grange, Ring Way. Altrincham . • 2, Hon VC W Cavendish, M.P 3. J Gould; special, C Baker. Geldings foaled in 1894, 1895, 1896, 1897 1, Esors of Peter Davies, Warburton; 2 and 3, T Bdwards, Bootle. Hunters (dist, lot Classes).-.-Brood mares having produced a living foal by, or stinted to, a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire in 1901: 1 and special. Mrs C Griffiths, Northwich; 2, C Schwabe. Arden near Altrincham; 3, Mrs C Griffiths, Northwich, Foals by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1. John RaVenscroft, Holford, Knutsford; 2 and 3. Mrs C Griffiths' Yearling colt, gelding or filly by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire : 1 and special, Mrs Griffiths; 2, I Farish, Northwicb; 3. J Ravenscroft. Two or three year old gelding or filly by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire.-1, special, and silver medal, I Farish ; 2, J Palmer, Lymm ; 3. I Farish. Hunter Mare or Gelding foaided in 1897, to be ridden : 1, 3 Drage. Northampton; 2 and 3. 3 H Stokes, Market flarborough. Hunter, mare, or gelding, foaled In 1899, 1895, 1896, or 1897, to be ridden, equal to carry 13st. and not more than llst 71135 1, J H Stokes ;2, Herbert B Cory. Cardiff; 3.3 I Drags. Hunter. mare, or gelding, foaided in 1894, 1895, 11898, or 1897, be be ridden, equal to carry 14st 71bs and upwards : 1 and silver medal. J H Stokes; 2,3 Drage ;3. H Stokes,</p>
