      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>INTERESTING WEDDING AT PENKETH.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING WEDDING AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>An A vent of considerable local interest took place at the We4eyan Cnapel, Penketh, on Thursday ast week, when Mr Charles H Mitchell. of Liverpool, and Miss Edith Mary Wigfleld, eldest daughter of Mr and Mrs W J Wigfteld Home Lea," Penketb, were united in marriage. Tae chapel was filled with well wishers, as Miss Wigfiell is well known and commanded the sincere reipect and admiration of the villagers. The Rev J )bn Gunnell was the officiating minister. The brde's d-ess was of ivory satin, trimmed with embroidered chiff ,n.She wore a tulle veil,with orange blossoms and carried a beautiful bouquet of white rases, lilies of the valley, and orchids. She was given away by her father. The brides- maids were her sister Miss Hilda Wigfield and Miss Hackett, cousin of the bridegroom. They were attired in dresses of white chine silk trimmed with lace, and tuscan hats, trimmed with cream roses. They carried bouquets of cream roses, and each wore a brooch of gold and pearls, the gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were undertaken by Mr W Sloane Walker, of Litherlaad. Mr Frank Hickman of Warrington, presided at the organ, and the hymns sung during the service were "The voice that breathed o'er Eden" and "God be with them." These were rendered with feeling by the choir. Mr Hickman played Wagner's "Bridal March" (Lohen- grin) and Mendelssohn's "W!dding March," as the happy pair left the chapel. The present, received by the bride •ind bridegroom were many,beautiful and costly. A rec-ption was afterwards held at " Home La," the home of the bride, when a large .number of relatives and friends of the newly married pair were present The bride's travelling dress wasEof blue Cloth trimmed with silver braid and hat to match</p>
