      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CORPORATION FINANCE. Alderman Shaw Green and the Creation of Stock.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION FINANCE. Alderman Shaw Green and the Creation of Stock.</p>
        <p>When I a-ked Busy Bee" through your columns reply to the questions I submitted I expressly stipulated that the answers should give full par- ticulars and definite data. I wished it so in order that your readers could judge of the correctness or otherwise: of his assertion, that the cost of erecting a new bridge across the Mersey could be saved by the issue of stock instead of borrowing the same money upon mortgage. Instead of this the reply is merely a lengthy historical theoretical statement of the method of issuing Stock with the names of various towns which have obtained loans by this means. " Busy Bee" says he cannot supply such details as he would desire because no Profit and Loss Account is published. How could there be a Profit and Loss Account of street improvements, sewers, removal of night-soil, cleaning streets and all similar matters ; and if there could, in what way could it possibly help him ? To those who ar • interested in financial affairs, all that your corr,,,,indent says is familiar. I wanted other information for it is no use arguing accepted matter. It is no use telling ni3 colleagues and myself that with which we are more familiar than he is. I suggested that your correspondent should take by way of illustration a loan of £lOO,OOO and give us all the costs including banker's charges incident to the loan, the cost and losses upon the investment of the sinking fund, land, indeed, all the incidental expenses connected with such an issue, and then contrast it with the cost of obtaining the like sum upon mort gage. It is simple enough and has been con- sidered by us many a time. Familiarity with the theory of a thing is helpful, and indeed most desirable, but it is the want of practical acquaintance that makes it difficult for ' Busy Bee ' to answer the question. lam not desirous of instituting comparisons but I venture to say that many of the Corporations he named would be better satisfied if they were as well off as we are. We have intimate relations with the finan- cial officers of many of those named by your cor- respondent, and we know exactly where the differ- ences lie. The al)ove remark applies to the rest of his answers. It would not be good policy neither -mould it be reasonable for the intricacies of the department to be set out in a newspaper, but I say definitely and without reservation that your correspondent is en- tirely wrong in his opinion that money cannot be borrowed upon mortgage as cheaply as by!an issue of stock.</p>
        <p>Theoretical knowledge has suggested to your correspondent that we should employ an expert. Had he only a prectical aequaintance with our affairs he would not only think but know that this would be no help. There is only one other comment I would like to make. Your correspondent suggests that the pre- sent generation is being called upon to pay more than its fair share for p-rinanent improvements. This is an evceedingly unjust and unwise state- ment and calculated to increase the difficulties of those who have the responsibility of finding the money for our. necessary works and public improve- ments. Our Corporation has ersted over fifty years and large sums have ben repaid on account of loans and thus assets have accumulated by a slow but steady process. Tilers are in addition included on the balance account large assets that have ben given or b ,queathed or obtained other- wise than saved by sinking fund and then the gas and water departments are responsible for very considerable sums. Knowing this, lam of opinion ' that than is no ground whatever for this aratement. Whilst I respect vary highly your correspondent and an always pleased to hear his views in finan- cial matters the fir-it necessity is a full ii-xiitaint- ance with the subject. Tiiis my friend can get if he will join us end help in th construction and carrying out of that poficy which upon full know- ledge and free discussion shall be found the best.</p>
        <p>J. SHAW GREEN.</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR BOYD DAWKINS AND 'WARRINGTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The following report of Professor Boyd Dawkins was read a a meetiag of the Water Comm.ttee and ordered to be entered on the minutes : Herewith I sen 1 the result of our conference on Mon- day and my ex unload= of the sites of the four shafts on Thursday last. la Of tho four shafts N 1 has been c impleted to a depth of ab int 161 feet from the surface, and yields daily a qaantity of water amountina to a minimum of 286 000 gallons, Co a maximum of 473,000 gallons, after continuous pumping from 2nd to 10:h May. The rest of the shafts now in course of being sunk, Nos 2,3, and 4 yield similar large qaantities of water, in pmportion to their dPpths. It is therefore now clearly proved that ' a very large supply of water from these works Can be added to the existing resources of Warrington. 2. When these shafts are coanected together by the proposed edit the amount obtainable from the fissures then intersected will amount to not less than one and a half million gallons per day; a yield wh ch I have frecrienly met with under similar conditions in these rocks in other places. 3. With rdgard to the depth at which the adit should be made I am of opinion that the depth selected by Mr Deas for beginning it in shalt N) 1 at 150 feet from the surface is very well chosen, because the (Pebble beds) Red Sandstocei are at this point very close grained and porous, and also becausa at that depth it will be 109'5 feet below the rest level of the water From this point it will he run to shafts 2, 3 and 4, with a rise of 3 feet between 1 and 4. 4 L Joking at the large quantity of:water in the shafts. Ido not think it necessary to sink deep er to obtain a supply which will be sufficient for Warrin gton for twenty years or more. 5. With regard t the suggeston that tie coal may' be worked underneath this area and that the water supply might be int 'Tiered with by the sinking of the land, there is, in my opinion, no ground for fear. Although the coal measures exist under the whole of the red sandstones and have been proved at Winwick, the difficulties to be overcome before they can be worked are very great. The workable seams occur at a depth of about 2000, ft. from the surface, and tle shafts neces- sary can only be sunk at very great cost because of the enormous quantity of water in the red sandstones which cover the Coal Measures. In my opinion they will not be worked in this area until all the available coal in the Lancwhire coalfields in the nottli is exhausted, a time too remote for present consideration. Even then it is an open question whether the water supply would be affected because of the impervious shales and merle, upwards of 1,000 feet in thickness, between the Coal Measures and the water-bearing sandstones. It is almost unnecessary to add that the Corporation of Warrington is fortunate in having gained so valuable an addition to its water supply. I am, my dear Sir, • Yours truly. W. BOYD DAWKINS. Jas. Dear, Assoc Inst. C.E</p>
      </div>
