      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</p>
        <p>WHAT HE WANTED.—MAHEES "No ; you've had enough cake." The Four-Year•Old ; "Oh, mamma, please can't I have too much?" THE difference between a wit and a humonrist is that a wit says things and a hurnourisb writes them.</p>
        <p>"HE says his wife hae witching ways." "I believe it. I hear she has a habib of riding over him with the broom-stick."</p>
        <p>WIG: "Jones says that he can read a woman like a book." Wag: " Yes ; but he can't shut her up like one." Morn: "I am surprised, my dear, that you Suffer a man to kiss you I" Daughter: "But, mamma, I don't call it suffering." OUTWEIGHED.—He: "Your life with me would be a path of roses." She: " Yes ; but with Jack Wheeler it would be a cycle path."</p>
        <p>EMMA : "And, Charlie, dear, would you have really shot yourself if I had refused you? " Cbarlie "Inded I would. I had already sent to four gunsmiths for price lists of revolvers." A LITERARY CONFERENCE. Sweet Sixteen: "Isn't the hero grand?" Sweet Seventeen : " Isn't he? Why, he is so noble that I was posi- tively jealous of the heroine." How HE MANAGED IT.—Willis "Has Dr. Skinner a good-paying practice ? " Wallace: "Oh, yes. His patients are never out of danger until they're paid up." GErrizro UP SOCIALLY.—" She is certainly rising in the social scale." "Is she ?" "Oh, yes, indeed I She is snubbed by a better class of people each succeeding year." AN IMPORTANF DIFFERENCE.—Sopwith : "Lom- bardo says there is no difference between genius and madness." Waggle : "Pardon me; madness gets three square meals a day." Mann (earnestly): "I want to ask you a ques- tion, George." George (also earnestly) : "What is it, dearest?" Maud (still earnestly): "If you had never met me, would you have loved me just the same?" HIS OPINION.—She "What have you to say of girl who marries a man to reform him ? " He: "I think she's spoiling a mighty good wife for some other fellow. As TO A CLIENT.—" He is the most obstinate man I ever saw," said the first lawyer. "Is he?" said his friend. "Yes. He hates to take adv:ce even when he pays for it." WORSE YET.—We should at once cease to cry out about mothers spoiling their sons, and he thankful that so few boys, comparatively, are brought up by their grandmothers. THE STATUS.—She : "Just look at Mr. and Mrs. Young! Their honeymom is evidently over." He: " Yes. I suppose matrimouy has resolved Itself into a search for a moduli vivendi." "MY cheeks are all on fire." "I thought I smelt burning paint I" ELIZABETH (visiiing town friends): "My Here's her visitin' card." Elizabeth's Husband : "What does it say?" Eizabeth : "It says she's at home Thursdays. I wonder where sue stops the rest of the time ? " NOTHING seems to arouse our scorn so much as a person who appears to be well satisfied with him- self. 111 that open door in China is not allowed to remain open, somebody's fingers may be caught in the jamb. THE VISITOR "Busy, old man?" Modern Author: " Busy ! Why, I'm writing so much that I don't have time to think." A REASONABLE QUERY.—"That fellow takes things that do not belong to him." "Ah ! Ama- teur photographer or thief?"</p>
        <p>YES, INDEED.—LittIe Elmer: "Papa, what is a genius f" Professor Broadhead: "A genius, my Eon, is a man who knows when he has said enough.</p>
        <p>AMATEUR PALMIST: "The life line indicates how long you will live." Sceptical Friend: " Yes ? Isn't it a wonder the life insurance companies pay E 9 attention to It!"</p>
        <p>OFFICIAL RECEIVES (at a meeting of creditors) : "What have you come here for I', Professional Beggar: "To put in my claim. Mr. Jones used to give me twopence every week." Promcnia.—She : "And Chinese civilisation is very old. is it not?" He: "Very. Chins was the first nation in which women wore shoes which were too small for them."</p>
        <p>ACUTE SYMPTOMS.—" Are you imre you love thab girl 1" "Well, I can't work in the morning until I gets letter from her, and after I get it I can't work."</p>
        <p>" PRISIDENT KRUGER is worth £5,000,000, and yet his wife does the cooking," said the lodger. "Great Scott 1" exclaimed the landlord. "Is there a servant, girl famine in the Transvaal, too?" HALVES AND QUARTERS. --Qaericus : "Let's See; the married men all have better halves, don't they ? " Cynicns ? "Ye.." Quericus : "Then what do the bachelors have?" Cynicus : "Better quarters 1"</p>
        <p>BESIDE THE LAKE. I thought she gave me cause to doubt. But soon I blessed my fates:</p>
        <p>Fr t.;h0n.„,04 my Ely.al ?:ook her out, 'Twas I pub on her TRIM NARROW ESCAPL—MM IrGrOiTy "01 never AR so frightened in ahl me loife I Sure the car.r.r missed me be less than six inches !" ll'Oorry "Av yez had gone a step farther the children wud ho y hod a stepmother."</p>
        <p>In 1991.—Firet Incubated Chicken: "Do you know it is said that our ancestors were originally hatched out by ordinary hens ? " Second Incu- bated Chicken: "Nonsense I I don't believe in any such &amp;beard Darwinian theory 1"</p>
        <p>No MORE DISSIMULATION —Young Bridegroom: "Darling, I think I .nould like to take your little brother with us to Niagara Falls." Bride "H kind that would be if you, Harry I" Young Bridegroom: " Yes ; I should like to push him over into the biggett of them." NOT A VIOLENT CAS/C.—Mrs. Peck: "Henry, what would you do if I were to die suddenly?" Henry: "Pray 3ou't talk of such a thing. I think It would almos:. drive me crazy." Mr. Peck : "Do you think you would marry again?" Henry: "Oh, no. I don't think I would b 3 as crazy as that." A BURGLAR who had entered A minister's blase at midnight was disturbed by t'ae awakening of the occupant of the room he wts in. Drawing his knife, he said : "If you stir, you are a dead man. I'm hunting 'Jr. money." "Lit me get up and strike a light," said the minister, "and I'll hunt with you." "Goon morning, uncle. How are yen to-day?" " Ab, nephew, I am well. How are you?" "First rate. What do you think brought me here?" "Oh, the old story, I suppose l" "I'll bat you fi”e shi.lings you can't guess?" "ru take it I Yjll came here to borrow some money." "You've lost I Pay me the five shillings! I only wanted to inquire how aunt is to-day !"</p>
        <p>RETALIATION --" Well," Bald Ddiss,Fosdick,with a satisfied air, "1 flatter myself that I have revenged myself upon that supercilious Mize Thorndyke Van Rensselaer." "How did you do it? Didn't, you Invite her to your tea ?" asked Miss Keediek. "Oh, yes, I invited her, but I spelt her name wrongly on the envelope, and also on the list of guests that I gave to ...he society papers."</p>
        <p>BREAKING IT GENTLY.--JUdgo Lynch's court having attended to the case of one Bilious Pate in the usual characteristic and conclusive manner. Alkali Ike was selected to break the news as gently as possible to the bereaved widow. "Howdy do, mom?" he saluted, when the lady had come to the door in response to his knock. "I've just dropped around to sorLer tell you that—er—er—your hus- band ain't a-goin' to live very long." "What makes you think he ain't?" returned the lady, in considerable surprise. " B'cuz he's dead now."</p>
        <p>"How in the world did you ever come to promise to be his wife if you didn't love him?" "Well, he went down on his knees and said he loved the ground I walked on." "Of course. They ail do that if they think it's necessary." "Still I said no. Then he said he would die unless I promised to be his." "I hope you didn't accept him witq the idea of saving his life." " No. I told him he was only wasting his time pleading with me, and advised him to go and try to find some other girl that he could love. Then he arose, and cried: 'Ali, you are as:beautiful as an angel, but you are heartless.'" "iWell?" "Well, after that I didn't have the heart to say no."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. A Road Sweeper Run Over. A road sweeper named Joseph Hatton, 71 years of age, residing at 51 Ellesmere-street, was follow- ing his employment in one of the main streets on Monday morning when he was knocked down and run over by a passing vehicle. He was conveyed Lo the infirmary where it was found that he was -mitering from crushed ribs, severe shock, and in- ernal injuriea.</p>
      </div>
