      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 16, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 16, 1901</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Great Company- Promoting Case. _ The Verdict.</p>
        <p>For the Plaintiff on all the Points.</p>
        <p>In the Nisi Prius Court of the Liverpool Assizes, on Thursday, before Mr Justice Bucknill, the ver- dict was given in the action Glynn V. Atherton and others. In this case the pllintiff, Mr Walter Glynn, shipowner. aid managing direct .r ot ths Leyland line, sought to recover damages ir m 3 Mr James B Atherton and others,directors of the British Electric Worts Company, and compensation for misrepre- scntation in the prospectus of the oompany.</p>
        <p>The jury returned into court after an absence of half an hour, and answered the questions formu- lated by his Lordship as follows :- I—Was the prospectus in its general tenour un- truthful ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>la—Was the prospectus false and fndulent on the part of any, and which of Etas defendants ? Yes.</p>
        <p>2—Were any of the statements in the prospntus which were complained of untrue ?—Yes. 3—H so, was the plaintiff induced to apply for shares by such statemen.s, or any of them ?—Yes. 4 —Had the defendants, or any, and which of them, reasonable grounds to believe that such statements were true ?—No.</p>
        <p>s—Did the defendants, or any, and which of them, sa believe up to the time of allotment P—No. 6—What loss or daartge di' plsintiff sustain by reason of the said untrue statements ?-2950. 7—Did the defendants,or any, and which of them make the aaid untrue statements fradulently P-- Yes ; J B Atherton, and Jacpb A•h2rton.</p>
        <p>d—Were the contracts in paragraph 6 of state- ment of claim, and any, and which of them, contracts it was material to disel-se to intending applicarits for shares ?—Yes. Mr Berridge : On that, my lord, I ask for judgment against all and each of the defendants. His Lordship : Tee ; certainly. Mr Horridge : And for a special jury ? His Lordship : Certainly. The judge exempted the jury from service for five years.</p>
        <p>Yesterday's Cricket. --- Close of Play Scores. LANCASHIRE v. SURREY .—Lancashire, 364 for 7. ESSEX v YORKSHIRE :—Essex 30 all out and 15 for 6 ; Yorkshire, 104 all out. SOMERSETSHIRE v KENT :—Kent, 435 for 4. HAMPSHIRE v. SUSSEX:—Sussez, 159; Ramp shire, 55 for 6. NorTs v. MIDDLESEX ;—Midilesex, 167 all out; Notts, 16 for 0. GLOUCESTERSHIRE v. SOUTH AFRICANS : South Africans, 234 ; GI mcester, 40 all oat, 2 for 0 wickets,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WAR</head>
        <p>THE WAR</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Big Battle in Cape Colony.</p>
        <p>Commandants Erasmus and Cachet Mortally Wounded.</p>
        <p>Commandants Kritzinger, Erasmus, Pyper, and Cachet were engaged by Colonel Clorringe on Tuesday near Steynsburg (mid Cape Colony). Erasmus and Cachet were mortally wounced. Many prisoners were taken.</p>
        <p>Other details not know n. The Rumoured Return of Lord Kitchener. War Office Denial.</p>
        <p>The War Office officially states that nothing is known of any arrangement for Lord Kitchener coin- ing home, as has been stated in the press.</p>
        <p>The Prudential Assurance Company</p>
        <p>and the War. The war is causing heavy claims to be made upon in- surance companies. The Prudential Assurance Company for instance, has been called upon to meet 3,120 claims in connection with the war in South Africa, and the amount paid to relatives up to August 7th last ttalled up to 2114,401 es ed. •</p>
        <p>A Warrington Man Captured by Vi lj eon.</p>
        <p>Mr James Whitley, of Stockton Heath, has received the following letter from his brother, Driver James Whitley, of B B porn porn section, Royal Horse Artillery, dated Middleburg, June 26th :—Just a line to let you know I have got from Wilman3rust to Middleburg all right. I suppose you heard about our disaster with our porn poms. The Boers attacked our camp at Wilmans- rush at a quarter to eight on the 12th June. when they captured our poin poms and made us all prisoners. We had 300 all told, two porn porn sections (about 24 men) and the rest was the Victorian Infantry Contingent. It was a horrible sight, it is a wonder we were not all killed. This makes the third time we have been attack- ed, Viljeon's every tinoe : but the third time be was successtul, all we drivers were behind our horses when , the firing began. One of our drivers got his ankle smashed : there were 20 killed and 41 wounded. I got up out of my blankets and walked across the lines,when Vii jean got hold of my shoulder and said "Come on, my lad, I want you, we are not going to hurt you." Su we all sat down till they got everything away, and then they marched us down to a drift about one mile and a half away. The last words of theirs were " Goodaigbt, boys, better luck next time," and then we came back to our empty camp, made a fire, and went round the camp for the wounded and the dead. A horrible sight ; never want to see such a sight again. There were 136 horses and mules killed, and 20 men killed and 41 wounded,but a lot have died since of their wounds. Major Morris was in charge of us when our column was split up. The other part of the column was miles away from us at a place called Yandykes Delft ; we were there two days, and the other part of the column was there five days. Kruger's boys are getting very vicious. Viljeon took all my curios and my winter's jacket, too. Nearly all the cqmmando could speak Eoglish. lam in fine health and ready for anything as usual, ready to come home or ready to stop out, whichever the Government likes, so they cannot vex me which way. lam keeping a diary of what I have gone through in the camps I have been in, and what Generals I have served under, and actions I have been in. I will be able to give you details when I get back. Your true brother,l7 months in South Africa. "Better luck next time." We have done good work up to this.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BURSTING OF A COPPER FURNACE AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>BURSTING OF A COPPER FURNACE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Six Men Injured</p>
        <p>A sad accident, which might have had fatal results occurred at the works of Messrs Siliechnie, copper smelters, Ditton-road, Widnes, on Wednes- day. Six men whilst following their employment feeding a furnace were severely injured by it burst- ing and blowing off the root, causing the heatml material, which was going through 'the process of heating, to fly in all directions over the men. The names of the injured men are J ; Howard, Ditton, badly injured ; T Purcell, Widnes, similar injuries; J C Singleton, Widnes, badly burnt upper part of the body; E Rylands, burns , on the body; W Travis, Ditton, slightly injured B Moran, Widnes, slightly injured. Dr Edwards was quickly on the scene after the accident, and the men were hurriedly conveyed in the ambulance and cabs to the Accident Hospital. On Thur.sday the men were progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>Theitnembers of the Council signified their approval of the resolution by standing in silence for a few minutes. ' THE SCARCITY OF WATER. TEMPORARY SUPPLY FROM THE RAILWAY COMPANY., At an extraordinary meeting of the Water Committee. the Ctiairman read a letter which he had received from Mr W Gilmour with respeet to the inadequate supply of water in the district. A letter was read from the Assistant Secretary to the Local Government Boa, d stating that the Provisional Order relating to the borrowing powers of the Couucll under the Newton Im- provement Act, 1855, for water supply purposes had been confirmed by Parliament. The Clerk read a letter which he had received from Mr T Hewitt, complaining of the supply of water in Crow-lane West. Mr 13 0 Timmins the watn• engineer, reported to the committee with respect ti the present supply of water to the in- habitants of the district, sod stated that in his opinion the mains are inadequate for the distribution of the water, and that the supply is not what it should be to keep up the demand. It was resolved that an expert be engaged to examine and report to this committee as to whether the situation of the present pumping station is the best water beariag area within the district I was resolved ' hat the Clerk be instructed to write to Mr F W Webb, Engineer to the London and North- Western Railway Company, Crewe, and inform him that thrr ugh the unfortunete death of their Eoginet r at the beginning of the year, the acheme for the supply of the district with water has not yet been completed, the con- sequence being that the present supply is inadequate, and that ttn committee hope that the Company will help the Council in this matter by again supplying suffic- ient water so that an adeqatte supply may be provided to the District on the same terms as heretofore. It was also resolved that the engineer be directed to prepare and submit to the committee a full report upon the proposed scheme for the supply of the district with water, and that the surveyor be directed to find other means for watering the streets than with water from the mains. At a further meeting cf the committee the C:erk read the correspondence with Mr F W Webb, superintendent, locomotive department, London and North-Western Rail way Company, Crowe, with respect to the supply of water by the company, in which it was stated that the company could only let tin council have a tempor- ary and limited supply of water on the isounoil agreeing to pay cne shilling per thoussnd gallons and the cost of connection, and stated that after consultation with the chairman of the committee, he bad telegraphed to Mr. Webb accepting the terms. It was resolved that the action of the Chairman of the committee and the Clerk be approved and adopted, and that the London and North-Western Railway Company's water be turned on St the Viaduct Works at the earliest opportunity. It was also resolved that the engineer be directed to obtain a Deacon's waste water meter. A letter was read from Mr J C Sanson complaining of the inadequate supply of water at Newt on Common. A third meeting of the committee was held on Mon- day afternoon when it was resolved that a special water committee consisting of Messrs Collingwood, Borron, Ormand, Smith and Thorupson be appointed to take such steps as may appear to them to be necessary for proceeding with the extension scheme and to report to the committee. Mr C B F Borron moved the confirmation of the minutes. Mr Gilmour seconded. Dr Latham stated that since they obtained an addi- tional supply from the Railway Company they had had an adequate supply in the district and he trusted that the arrangement would not be disturbed until such time as the Council were satisfied that their own supply was sufficient in quantity and quality. The Chairman said it was very gratifying that they had been able to surmount their difficulties in this way. The minuces were then passed. BUILDING PLANS DISAPPROVED. At a meeting of the Highways and Sanitary ComrniL- tee, it was resolved that the following building plans be disapproved, v:z. :—Mr H Howarth (Parklands Balmoral-road, Morecambe) ; new shop ftont and bow window, corner a Earle-street and Canal-street ; Mr G F Clarke, Cross-lane; eight dwsning-houses, corner of Market-street and Neville-street ; Mr William Stephens 24, Sankey-street ; six dwelling-houses, Neville-street. It was further aesolved that in future no building plan be approved and passed unless a permanent sewer is shewn thereon, and toe plan properly Completed. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE. The correspondence wt h Mr A C Leslie with respect to the fencing of the land known as "Thompson's Pit,' off Wargrave-road, was read and approved. It was re- solved that notice be Riven to Mr Leslie, on behalf of Lord Newton, to provide proper protection frents dancer arising through the land known as " Thompson's Pit" being unfenced by erecting a close wooden fence, and unless the notice be complied with within fourteen days the fence be provided by the Counzil, and the owner of the property charged with the cost thereof. The Clerk reported that he had written to Colonel piikingtn, If.p,, with respect to the report of the Royal Commission on sewage disposal, and that Colonel Pilkington had replied that an interim report would be issued very shortly. It was resolved thatthe Surveyor be directed to visit the Glasgow Corporation's sewage works at Bterhead, and report thereon to the committee. A letter was read from Mr A. C Leslie, stating, with respect to the insanitary condition of the house in Parkside-road, occupied by Thomas Bale, that the occupier would receive notice to quit. The Clerk reported that having plaints of the state of Millingford brook, he what sat enpds tGhoelyb ohr had received com had written to the Ashtone-ninu-Mankgerwfiehl tija rkbeann tDo ps rt re ivc et nCt tuba e ilspollution of the brook ; that the Asinon Council had, through their Clerk, stated that if the brook was polluted the pollution did not arise within their district; and that the Clerk to the Golborne Council stated that his Council had constructed nine bacterial beds and six more were being made and would be shortly finished. The Clerk also reported that he had instructed the Inspector of Nuisances to visit the stream, and submitted his report of a visit made on the 10th July together with Baronies of the sewape taken at different points in Ashton and Golborne. It was resolved that the action of the clerk be aporeved and adopted, and that the Chairman, Deputy Chairman, and Councillor A Smith be appointed a sub-committee to confer with the Golborne Cmacil, with respect to the pollution of tin brook by Ashton-in•Makerfield, with full power to act on behalf of this Council in supporting the Golborn Council in any proceedings which they may think advisable to take against the Ashton Council to prevent the pollution of the stream. The minutes were passed on the proposition of Mr Simpson.</p>
        <p>TOE TRAMWAYS SCHEME. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee a letter was read from Messrs Peace and Ellis, solicitors, enclosing cop of a letter received by them from Messrs F J Leslie and Co., the solicitors to the South Lancashire Tramwss Company, stating that they regretted that they could not advise. their ciients to pay the cost of this Council's opposition to the Tramways Bill. A letter was read from Messrs Kincaid Waller and Manville, engineers to the Sluth Lancashire Tramways Company, enclosing plans for the approval of the Council, showing the position of carter!' tra..mways in the district together with the proposed widening&amp; The plans also showed the position of the standards, both for traction and lighting. They also sent details of the standards, brackets, and section pillars,together with the specifica- tion of the works proposed to be executed. The SW. veyor having reported upon the plans it was resolved thatnd not approved on the following grounds a , be returned to the rogineer to the Train that plans be ways Co., together with the specifications, for amend- merit,is shown to indicate that the cobbles vziand—p(al Nothingaoing,now in Crow-lane. are to be taken up and reilaced with macadam. (it) No widening of the roadway is shown opposite Legh Terrace, in Crow- lane, and (*) At Sefton-street, Fairbrother's Bridge,and Church street, the plans show the footpaths to be made narrower than at present, without indicating the acquisition of land for widening the footpaths.</p>
        <p>i SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION. NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT ARE THE SCHOOLS OVERCROWDED? COUNCIL. i The minutes of the School Attendance Committee I showed the quarterly statement of attendances -Is -- 1 follows The Water Supply. , Pue'oe.r's—minifx&amp;e:te • A meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, s' Earlestown, on Monday evening. In the absence of Mr Coiling wood, the chair was occupied by Mr T Ormond, G- street—lnfante' deputy chairman. There were els) present :—Dr Wargrave—Mixed ... Patterson, Dr Latham, Messrs C B F Borron, C.C. J W Do. Infants' Emmett, JP., W Gilmour, A Smith, J Simpson 1 Thompson, J Fezgerald, E Evans, a ith C Cole w Esrlestown District—Mixed T(clerk), Do. Infants' A Bowes (surveyor), H 0 Timmios (gas and water Wesleyan—Mixed engineer) Do. Infanta' VOTE OF CONDOLENCE WITH THE KING. Roman Catholic—Mixed The Cnairman stated that before proceeding with the Do. Infants' ordinary busineia, he thought they would be lacking in Vulcan—lnfants' their day if they did not move a vote of condolence on Earlestown Manor—Mixed the death of the ,Eamoress Frederick. They all km w . Do. Infants' that during the last 13 months His Majesty had lost three who had been near and dear to him in the Persons of his mother, brother, and now his eldest sister. He submitt:d the follo .sing resolution for their acc'otsnc° : —" To the King's Most Excellent Majssty. The Urban District Council of Newton-in-Makerfleld in the County of Lancascer in Council assembled beg leave to rt,k your Majesty to graciously accept their respec.ful con- dolence and deep sympathy in your grief at the death of your be!oved sister, the Empress Frederick of Germany, and to express the nope th st the crest sympathy which has been shown by your Majesty's loyal subjects may be some consolation to you in this great sorrow."</p>
