      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WARGRAVE POST OFFICE.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr Jordan asking the Conn- ell's opinion upon the suggestion that the Wargrave sub. office should be closed on bank holidays, in order to allow the sub-Postmistress to have a holiday. After discussion it Was decided to refer the matter to the General Purposes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CONSERVATISM IN THE NEWTON</head>
        <p>CONSERVATISM IN THE NEWTON</p>
        <p>DIVISION. Lord Newton on the "Liberal Imperialists."</p>
        <p>A quarterly meeting of the Council of the Newton Division Conservative Association was held at Newton- le-Willows on Saturday. Lord Newton presided, and Colonel R Paington, M.P., the representative of the division, was present. After the transaction of formal business, Colonel PH- kington was called upon to address the meeting. Colonel Pilkington, M.P., said the inter, st of Parlia- ment and of the country was still concentrated upon Sotith Africa. It was very satisfactory to Unionist members in the HOU93 of Commcns to hear the speech of Mr Chamberlain on the previous Tue. day. Mr Chamberlain was still doing his work in the same hearty and stro,,g manner. He showed in the telegram which he sent to the Stratford-on-Avon Division that he had not altered his opinion, which had been ex- pressed during the general election, that a vote given to the Liberals was a vote gained to the Boers. Nobody exp T'ed the Colonial Secretary to send that telegram again, but he did, and after he had done it everybody said it was the right thing to do. The Liberal party, to show that it was the right thing, had since been indulg- ing in a certain number of strategic mar ceuvrea, some- times to the front and sometimes to the rear.—(Laugh- ter.) Mr Chamberlain's speech was a very interesting one, because it showed that the policy of making secure the railways and all the principal placrs in the Transvaal was being steadily and methodically carried out by Lord Kitchener. This speech, coming at the end of the sea-. 'ion, was most effective. It had been followed by the Proclamation which had !nen issued by Lord Kitchener at the suggestion of the Colonial Secretary. We now had it set forth in black and white, therefore, that the Boer leaders, who had been and still were inciting and leading these isolated attacks upon the King's soldiers would, unless 'hey surrerv'ered by a given date, never be able to enter the two colonies again.—(Hear, hear ) This notice was a most valuable thing, because if tbe=e men showed that they were thoroughly unreasonable, ad that they would not fall in with the new rule in South Africa, it was clear, from a political point of view, and for the good of these two colonies and of the Empire, that they must never again have the oppor- tunity of stirring up strife or relrllion.—(Hear, her). After some reerences o the " dissensions " in the Liberal party, Colonel Pilkington expressed regret that the Government had not been content to deal in the present session with the war and questions relating thereto, and to postpone until a future session domestic matters such as the Education Bill. But he thought that, after all, the Government had done well, and I although Unionist members might " kick up a row " and " let off steam "at Westminster, there was clearly no chance for any other Government save that of Lord Salisbury. —(Applause). Colonel Pilkington concluded by moving a vote of thanks to Lord Newton for pre- siding. Mr C B F Borron seconded the resolution, which was adopted._</p>
        <p>Lord Newton, in responding, said it could not be denied that this bad been a disappointing session, in the sense that not much had been achieved. One explana- tion that bad been given for this state ef affairs was that the Opposition was so weak. That naisht be a reason, but it seemed to him to be a singular one. A , more obvious reason was the incrlased activity of the Irish party, which could only mean increased harm to the majority of the voters in this counter. His experi- ence of the House of Commons convinced him that the country cared very little about obstruction. It was not of much use for the Government to come and complain to the electors that they could not get business done because there had been so much obstruction. The obvious answer was that if there was obstruction they ought to get rid of it. (Elear,hear.) He imagined that there would be two results from the stagnation of busi- ness which had re-tilted during the session. One of these would be that the Government would have to in- troduce new rules for regulating procedure, and the I second and far more important result which he hoped would happen was that the country would see the pro- prirty of reducing the representation of Ireland to Its proper proportion. (Applause.) With regard to the state of the Opposition and the reasons that were I advanced in explanation of its feebleness, it seemed Ito him that there was a great deal of unnecessary hntribue talked about the Liberal Imperialists' What were these so-called Liberal Imperialists ? To his mind a Liberal, whether he had an adjective before or after his name was a Liberal and a Home Ruler. Although a man might call himself an Imperialist Liberal, he remained a Home Ruler until he had die- smelted himself from Home Rule. How could a man be at once a Liberal Imperialist and a HOME Ruler ? The two things were absolutely inconsistent and contradict- ory. And then, what about the influence of the Liberal Imperialists ? What were their numbers ? He noticed that there were always the same names put farward,and they did not amount to more than three or four. If they were such fine politicians let them give some greater proof of their strength He could not understand why people should suggest that the country would be better off if the duty of government were placed in the hands of Lord Ro.ebery, Sir Edward Grey, and Sir Henry Fowler instead of in those of the present Miniatry. If on the other hand, these so-called Imperialist Liberals chose to dissociate themselves from Home Rulaen what sense were they better than the Liberal Unionists ? In his opinion there was a great deal of sham about what was called Liberal Imperialism —(Applause.) He had some slight sympathy upon this point with the extreme Radicals. If he were a Radical he would be an extreme Radical,but he would not Consent to be led by gentlemen calling themselves Imperialist Liberals or anything else whose great idea was to " run with the hare and hunt with the hounds."—(Applause.) He sm. pathised with the extreme Libtrals to this extent, that he would not submit to be dictated to by a small section of his party, and to be asked to commit himself to the leadership of Lord Rosebery or the other gentlemen whom he had mentioned, because it might fairly be argued that there was only the difference of Home Rule between them and the Liberal Unionists. (Hear, hear.) Dealing with Mr Brodrick's scheme of army reform, Lori Newton said that although the scheme had been brought forward in good faith it was not a real measure of reform at all,benause it did not In the least alter our military system. It did not create anything new, except various forces on paper, and otherwise it merely expressed various pious aspirations that things might turn out better in the future than in the past. There was no radical reform about it, and therefore it could not be looked upon as being likely to produce any real and considerable change for the better in our military system. He had no doubt in his own mind that what the country required was, in the first place, a well-paid and thoroughly efficient regular army which need not be even so large on paper as was the present army ; and behind it we required a real national fore., a real national militia, formed primarily for the purpose of home defence, but available for use in time ef emergency to supplement the regular army,— (Hear, hear.) Resubmitted that a force of thi3 kind could no longer be obtained by voluntary means. When the crisis came, our existing system was shown to be no longer equal to the strain, and if we were wise we would now set to work to repair it.— (Applause.) The only force which at present stood between the country and obligatory military service was the militia, because it was a force which was under the control of the War Office, whereas the yeomanry and the volunteers were not. We ought, therefore to educate ourselves to the view that there was nothing degrading or disgraceful in the fact that the young men of this or any other country should give a few months of their lives for the purpose of defending their own homes.</p>
        <p>I not.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of State for India has received a telegram from the Viceroy stating that the famine prospects are much Improved. The GLEN-SPEY DIsTILLERY. Situate in the finest Whisky-producing District of Scotland, is the property of W. &amp;A. Gilbev. This Whisky is made entirely from home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in hie Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold by W. &amp; A. Gilbey's agents in every town e 38. 6d pr bottle 11. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS THE KING OF TONICS.--63, SANKEY-STREET Warrington.—(AdYn• ASE for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL YALIIES.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the Board Room as the Workhouse on Wednes- day morning. Mr L J Jolley presided and there were also preaent : Mrs Clay, Dr Patterson, Rev Father Wilson Messrs A Brooks, William Houghton. G Blather. .1 Clarke, S Rooinson. R Forshaw,J Payton 8 Davies, J Billiage, A Jolley, G Pilling, T H Ashcs;ft, William Hickman,T Ritchiea W Knowles.with JC Sutton(olerk), Bieton (assistant clerk), C H Ward (master). Mr Jzsnois Fun, Locat Government Board Inspector, ale, attended the meeting.</p>
        <p>THE PUBLIC VACFINATOESHIP. SUGGESTED AIII.LOWELTION OH OFFICES. The minutes of the House Committee included insttuo- dons with respect to the ventilation of the battery room in connection witd the elentrio lighting, and after con- sideration of Mr Miller's report, and letters from Messrs Owen, an order was recommended th :t the necessary work of ventilation in battery room and engine room be proceeded with at once. A letter was received from the Local Government Board, enclosing letter received from James Keefe, an inmate of the Workhouse,—The Hoare Committee instructed the Clerk to write in reply that the complaint is as frivolous as former meg made by the same pauper. The Committee also instructed the Clerk to ask if the Local Government Board would approve of a revision of the existing arrangements whereby a resident medical officer might be appointed Po take charge of the Workhouse Infirmary and sick indoor poor, holding at the eame time the office of public vaccinator for the County Borough of Warrington, and devoting the whole of his time to the duties of these officer.</p>
        <p>Mr Baomts moved the confirmAtion of the minutes.</p>
        <p>2111" PILL/NG eeconded. __ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr BILLINGE said he Was not present at the moering of the Howe Committee at which the appointment of a public vaccinator was considered. He differed with the decision of his colleagues. The CLERK said the resolution of the committee was merely asking the Local Government Board if they would consent to such an appointment as that suggested It did not follow that the Board would carry out the scheme suggested by the committee. Ir BILLINGE : Toon it does not commit us in any way</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : It w)uh bi 9 treated as an expression of feeling. Mr BILLINGE Slid if that was the case he would move an amendment that the minutes be passed with the exception of that relating Co amalgamation of the offices of resident medical superintendent and public vaccinator for Warrington. Mr ASHCROFT seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>Tbe CHAIRMAN (Po Mr Billings) : Perhaps you will give some reason?</p>
        <p>Mr BILLINGS? said he was prepared 0 support the appointment of a resident Medical Ofß?,er at the Work- house Infirmary brut he did not avers with tho otonneal to add the duties of public vaccinator to the offi re. The dutes.of vaccinator would take the Medical Officer away from the Workhouse for the greater part of the day and the doctor might be miles a way when he was wanted in the house,</p>
        <p>The Cumnotarr, interpoqing, said that matter was car.fully gone into by the committee and it was found that the duties of public vaccinator would not require the exptmditure of more time than one hour per day all the year round.</p>
        <p>Mr BlLLusus contend,,d that if two medical men were appoi .ted as public vaccinators the Board could ask them to become honorary consulting physicians for the Workhouse Infirmary. If his memory served him right he believed the office of public vaccinator was worth practically £4OO.</p>
        <p>- • The URAIRMAN ; Last year payments amouot,d to 2320.</p>
        <p>Mr BlLLusoe (continuing) said the Warring'on die. trict was continually increasing, and be thought the course he had suzgested would be the better plan. The CHAIRMAN: Your amondment amounts to this, that a resident medical officer be a pointed for the Workhouse, and that two medical men be appointed public vaccinators, and who are also to act as honorary medical officers of the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>Mr HICK:46N supported the amendment on the ground that the meeting of the committee was summoned at a very awkward time, and when very few were able to be present. The meeting was held on the Friday before august Bank Holiday were many were absent on hail- days. He thought it would be better to wait for an intimation from the Local Government Board as to whether they would be likely to countenance the pro- posal before discussinc,othe subject. The CLERK said he took it that the Board was asking for information before dealing, with the whole question. Mr. HICHMAI•I said the Chairman had ruled that they were practically committed to it if the resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr Foesnew thought it lweuld be ;better to get an answer before gang any further. Tbe CHAIRMAN said it would seem absurd to ask the question unless the Boar d had an idea of carrying out such a scheme. Mr JENNRR FUST said it would perhaps help the Board if he stated that the Local Government Board would not be likely to eonsent to honorary visiting physicians The Board always thought that anyone who was oa duty ought to be au appointed officer, and as such, subject to rules and regulations. .)though the Board would pro- bably approve of the appointment of a resident medical officer and of a visiting physician who would have a regular appointment at a salary, he did not think they would approve of any Appointment of honorary physicians. Mr BILLINGE : That could be avoided by giving an honorarium of ten guineas a year. Mr ASHCROFT : Can Mr Jenaer Fast answer the question we are sending to the Lsoal Government Board ? _ _</p>
        <p>----- • The CattittmAN : No, No. It would be unreaeousble to ask.</p>
        <p>lir J CURICE considered that it would be difficult for one medical man to fulfil the duties of resident medical officer and vaccination officer. The Citainmes said the doting of vaccinator officer would not ihterfere with the efficiency of the work of the medical officer at the Workhouse for the time required for vaceination would not exceed an average of one hour per day. The area of the borough was within easy reach of the Workhouse. Some time ago an alternative scheme was put forward. It was that a resident medical officer should be appointed who should also • act as district medical officer but anyone who remembered the unfortunate case inquired into last year would be aware that the duties attaching to these two offices sometimes clashed and it was impossible for the officer to be in two places at once. The duties of public vaccinator would in no way clash with those of a res.dent medical officer. The deferring of the performance of vaccination for a few hours was nothing when compared with the attendance on the nick poor which was of vital importance. At the present time, the medical officer for the Workhouse SVP s' a district medical officer and also had private practice. How much more was it desirable to have • man who would give the whole of his , time to the resident sick poor with the appointment 'of vaccination officer as a secondary matter. He ven- tured to think that the Local Government Board would give their consent if the matter were put before them in that way. There was a difficulty in the way of the proposal to divide Warrington into two districts for vaccination purposes. The regulations which were still in force prohibited the division of an urban area unless the various divisions contained not less than 25,000 inhabitants, it would be difficult to divide the Warrington area into two divisions each containing the required number of inhabitants. On a vote being taken only six voted for the amend- ment which was lost.</p>
        <p>Mr PILLING then moved that no action be taken until the reply of the Local Government Board has been received.</p>
        <p>Mr J W KNOWLES seconded: The CHAIRMAN suggested that the difficulty might be overcome if the Clerk added on the mil:lnto that it was understood that the Board was not committed to the scheme.</p>
        <p>This course was agreed to. THE PURE BEER BILL.</p>
        <p>A circular letter was read from Sir Cuthbert Quiltpr, Bart, MP. asking the clerk on behalf of the committee for the promotion of pure beer to convey to the Government their appreciation of the help they had given them in regard to the Pure Beer Bill. They hoped when the time came round again that the Guardians would be good enough to accord them their powfu support. -- YACCINATION OFFICERS Alen THEIR P3IVER. _</p>
        <p>Mr G Herbert Lough, clerk to the Poplar Guardians, forwarded a copy of the following resolution passed by that Board and asking to'be informed of any action the Warrington Guardians might take thereon :—" that in the opinions of the Guardianstof this Union, it is not expedient or desirable that vaccination Officers s °pointed by Boards of Guardians should possess the power of tak- ug proceedings against any person for non-compliance with the Vaccination Laws, without the content and authority of the Board of Guardians, and that in so far as such power now rests in the hands of Vaccination Officers, it should be removed by an order of the Local Government Board, or failing that, by legislation." The Cleik said the subject had been before the Board on previous occasions and the Guardians bad always decided against taking any action in the matter. This course was again taken. The Board proceeded to consider a proposed purchase of a site for new offices, a report of which will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>Australia leads the way in the suppression of the ju- venile smoker. The Government of Victoria has decided to insert a clause in the licenses of tobacco sellers for- bidding them under a penalty of £lOO to trade with Children under the age of sixteen. Placing principle above profit the Melbourne Tobacconists' Assaciation has almost unanimously concurred in the new regula- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-08-16_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>PROPOSED NEW UNION OFFIC AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>i At the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday, a report was subraittoo by the special committee appointed to consider 3 ,5 site for proposed new Union offices at Warrington' The report was as follows: I "In reporting to the'Gnardians on their work during the past two years the Bites for Offices Corn; mites desire to refer to the great difficulties °` the task entrusted to them arising from the coo; ditions of tenure, the scarcity of suitable eites, anti; . the increasing value of land in the central parts Of . Warrington. No site on any one of the four ursin streets of the town being available except at price!, which were deemed prohibitive—over .£29 6 Pr' having been recently paid for • land so situated--; the Committee endeavoured to secure the refusal a a suitable site elsewhere. After many inquiries' in which they have been assisted by the Clerk IMCI by Mr William Owen, F.R.1.8.A., the Committee . now recommend to the Guardian, a site which iS ~ central, being equidistant from the three principal railway stations within a .stone's throw of the chief public buildings and vhich will provide space fur offices for Union, Rural District Councili and Registration and Relief work as well as fat suitable Board, Committee and Waiting Roonis' This plot has frontages to Legh-street and Bath; street, being the sites of the houses numbered d-t . and 46 Legh-street with the gardens behind there, The area, including portions of the adjoining . streets, is about 2050 square yards and forms poll of a p!o',., of land held on lease from the represents', tives of the late Lord Winmarleigh for a term 01 999 years from Novemb lr Ist, 1868. Tie houoe No 44, is at present occupied under al agreement of tenancy for 3 years, froai May Ist 1900 a'; a rent of £4O Dot annum ; the larger house No 46 is vacant, it wsB leased at £6O a year during ten years by its late tenant: it contains spacious rooms, is unusuallY well built and could easily be adapted for portions of the offices, thus reducing the outlay in the erecr tion of new buildings facingßath-st reet.The lessek are the representatives of the late Thomas .Tolle and the committee have secured from them t'. options: oae being that the Gurrdians should under lease for the residue of the term of 999 years, about half the above area having a frontage toßath-stree; at an annual ground rent of £66 13s 6d. This would necessitate the erectiod not only of new Board and Committee rooms but of entirely new offices for all departments. The alternative is that the Guardiaas should underlease, for a like term, the whole of the , described are about 2,050 square yards, with th! buildings thereon, at an annual ground rent 01- JllB. The committee are of opinion that teis is the better alternative and recommend it since it would secure a site for committee and Board rooms fo,f the use of Guardians and the Rural District Council, ' at once central, quiet and with a pleasant outlocX over the public gardens: it would give frontages to, two streets; the immediate utilisation of No. 46 ad° ' the poosible future utilisation of No. 44 for office 4 would reducesthe capital outlay and there would be land enough to provide for Union requirements in , the future. The committee are agreed that the 6ito r• is admirably adapted for the purpose and that tll 1 terms recommended for acceptance are fair ea,' reasonable. They desire to express their indebted ness to Mr William Owen for the great assistanCe wl.ich he has rendered b )th by advice and in loe' gotiations during their two years' labours.</p>
