        <p>WEDDING DISAPPOINTMENT AT WARRINGTON. An unfortunate hitch was th 3 cause of keen dis- appointment to a wedding party on Thursday last week, on which day a marriage had been fixed to take place in Wycliffe Congregational Church, Bewsey-street. At the time appoint ed,the wedding party put in an appearance and the officiating minister (the Rev John Yonge) was waiting to commence the ceremony but could not owing to the absence of the Registrar. Precious minutes anxiously slipped by and w len the bridegroom was interrogated as to hie duty of informing the Registrar, he stated that he had left that matter in the hands of his bride. She, it appears, had been to the Registrar's Offi !e a fortnight previously but had failed to call again an inform the Registrar of the day fixed for the wedding. A messenger was despatched to the Registrar's Office but on arrival it was ascertained that the much-needed official was in attendance at a weddiug at Penketh, and had the registrnr's book in his possession. It was now nearing three o'clock, and no time was to be lost if the ceremony was to be performed that afternoon, An attempt was made to find the Registrar without success, and as the fingers of the clock had got beyond the stipulated time for the ceremony to take place, it was reluctantly decided to postpone the wedding and the festivities until the following morning, It is only fair to state that the Registrar I was unaware of the wedding and had received no notice that it was to take place.</p>
