        <p>Xetters to tbe ebttor. A Few Questions for Alderman Green. Sir,— Will Aldernian Sliaw Green oblige me with the amount received by the Warrington Corporation for year ending 31st M3rch,l9ol, on account of the General District Rate, the Water Works, the Gas Works, the Longford Manure Works, and the Markets. Will he also state what the Corporation debt was on that date and the amount to be paid in interest and tne amount to be set apart as sinking fund this year on acccunt of that debt P—Yours cbediently, BUSY BEE. Warrington,</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. __— Cricket. Warrington were thoroughly ;beaten on Saturday, when for the second time in successive weeks, they encountered the Leyland eleven.Tne return engagement en the Warrington ground proved the most disastrous for the Warringtonians who suffered defeat to the ex- tent of 91 runs and three wickets. The spin of the coin was in favour of Warrington who decided to have first turn with the bat. At the outset runs came freely, and tbe batsmen were apparently bent on forcing the game as much as pos- sible. Twenty runs were recorded during the first quarter of an hour, and a run later Connor was bowled. J Briggs came next but only contributed a single when Tonge bowled through his defence, while at the same total-26—Callon was bowled with a grand bail from I'Aoson. The bowlers continued to work havoc, and B Silcock after contributing three' was sent back by Tonge, whilst Shewlis failed to trouble the scorers, half the side being out for 30. -- J W Bibby played a steady game, but runs were bard to obtain. At 34 the home skipper was tempted by Tonge, and Bibbed brought off a fine catch on the boundary. W A Silcock looked like causing trouble, and several times placed I'Anson on the leg side. -- Thompson was caught at points at 41, but a useful Stand was made when Elampscn joined Silcock, the pair adding 19 for the eighth wicket before Silcock was splendidly caught in front of the Sight board by Hib- bert. The innings cd.sed for 60, flampson carrying out his bat for eleven. -- Leyland were first represented by E Stanniug and A Tinsley, the bowlers being E A Connor and E Gibson. The first wicket fell at 32. 1 E Stanning being caught from a skier by the wicket-keeper. A prolonged stand was made by Tinsley and R Kershaw, and the Warring- ton total was passed without further loss. W M Silcock was tried. and Thewlis tuck up the attack at the town end. Tinsley and Kershaw were dismissed at 75 and 76 respectively. A fine innings by Hibbert was the; feature of the afternoon's thy, that batsman's innings realizing 52 not out. Mention should ha made of a brilliant catch by Callon, which was responsible for the ' dismissal of I 'Anson. Thompson,too, with a one-banded catch at mid on, got rid of C G Stannin,• and at the close Leyland had lost seven wickets for 151. The second eleven must be congratulated upon defeat- ing the Leyiand second contingent by a majority of 20 runs. The Leyland captain won the toss, but be decided to send Warrington in first. The innings closing for 84. Leyland were all got out for 64,Warrington thus winning by 20 runs. The Warringtoniana fielded very smartly, Rugby Football. Fletcher Russell's Club will this season figure in the Lancashire Combination (A team league). They have considerably strengthened their team and have already commenced practice. The head quarters will again be the Bowling Green Inn and the field at Lovely-lane. A limited number of season tickets will be issued at 33 6d each for which early application must be made to W Sanders, 3, Ma:tiny-street, Latchford. -- FLETCHER RUSSELL'S RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB. LEAGUE FIXTURES. September 7th Runcorn „ 14th Salford „ 21st Widnes Away „ 28th Swinton October sth Oldham 12th Broughton Rangers Away „ 19th Werneth Away „ 26th Warrington November 2nd 9th Salford „ 16th Warrington „ 23rd Rochdale Home „ 30th December 7th Broughton Rangers Home „ 14th Widnes Home „ 21st ~ 28th January let Runcorn 4th Stockport Away „ 11th „ 18th Oldham „ 25th Swinton February lit Birkenhead Away Bth Werneth Home 15th Leigh 22nd Rochdale Away ' March lst Birkenhead Home Bth Leigh IP 15th Stockport The open dates will be filled with Friendly Fixtures as rapidly as possible, Cycling. .1 A Watson, of Sankey Harriers, is fast coining to the fore in the racing world. On Saturday he figured in the winning list, at the Atherton Church House Sports, held at Atherton. With an allowance of 155 yards in the mile cycle race he succeeded in winning that event by one length, in 2min 27rece. Another local rider, A E Howard finished third in the same race. Be also won the half-mile bicycle race, finishing first by three lengths after having received a start of 75 yards. The time was lmin I.l•4ssecs. 1</p>
        <p>HARDIE, LTD. -- IMPORTANT ANNOUNCEMENT. In the course of a few weeks' time Mr R Hardie, the managing director of Hardie, Ltd, Cycle Manufacturers and Agents,of Berry-street,Liverpool ; IE2 Grange-road, Birkenhead ; and 116 Brighton-street, Seacombe, will start for his annual visit to THE CAPE, and, as has been his custom in past years, he will, prior to leaving England, dispose of,at exceedingly low rates, EIGHT HUNDRED SHOP SOILED CYCLES. This Clearance Sale will only be in operation between now and the time of Mr Hardie's departure,and persons desirous of obtaining, at unusually low rates, sound cycles, the manufacture of reputable firms, and fully guaranteed, should call at one of the depots of Hardie Limited. AT ONCE. To put off the visit for a week or two may result in the would-be purchaser being TOO LATE. Among the machines to be disposed of are : RALEIGEIS, SINGERS, OSMONDS, Hardies, and many other high-class and well known cycles, one hundred of which are offered at £5 EACH. NOTE —At the Company's shop in Berry-street Liverpool, Mr Hardie can be seen personally any time between 9 a.m. and 7 p.m. Advt.</p>
